nta/workspace-hub/issues/2651","title":"Convert ROGII task brief PPTX to PDF on ace-linux-2 (LibreOffice required)","issue_number":2651,"body":"## Context\n\nWorking on the [ROGII Wellbore Geology Prediction](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/rogii-wellbore-geology-prediction) Kaggle competition (sibling repo at `/mnt/local-analysis/kaggle-rogii-2026/` on ace-linux-1, deadline 2026-08-05).\n\nThe competition's task brief is shipped as a 27.5 MB PPTX with 14 slides and 19 diagrams (well-trajectory views, GR-correlation figures, map and 3D views). Text content has been extracted via \\`python-pptx\\` to \\`docs/task-brief.md\\` already, but the diagrams need a proper PDF render.\n\nace-linux-1 has no LibreOffice and we don't want to install it there. ace-linux-2 has LibreOffice already (per user direction 2026-05-05). Defer the conversion to that machine.\n\n## Source\n\n\\`AI_wellbore_geology_prediction_task_en.pptx\\` (27.5 MB), located at:\n- ace-linux-1: \\`/mnt/ace/kaggle-rogii-2026/data/raw/AI_wellbore_geology_prediction_task_en.pptx\\`\n- Reproducible via \\`scripts/download_data.sh\\` in the kaggle-rogii-2026 repo (uses Kaggle CLI; user must have OAuth via \\`kaggle auth login\\` set up on the destination machine).\n\n## What to do on ace-linux-2\n\n\\`\\`\\`bash\n# Once kaggle-rogii-2026 repo is accessible on ace-linux-2 (see Open Questions below):\ncd kaggle-rogii-2026\n./scripts/bootstrap_data_dir.sh   # idempotent symlink setup\n./scripts/download_data.sh        # materializes ~1.3 GB dataset incl. the PPTX\nsoffice --headless --convert-to pdf --outdir docs \\\\\n    data/raw/AI_wellbore_geology_prediction_task_en.pptx\nmv docs/AI_wellbore_geology_prediction_task_en.pdf docs/task-brief.pdf\ngit add docs/task-brief.pdf && git commit -m \\\"docs: add PDF render of task brief\\\"\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] \\`docs/task-brief.pdf\\` exists in the kaggle-rogii-2026 repo.\n- [ ] PDF contains all 14 slides with diagrams visible (visual spot-check on slides 9, 12–13 — those carry the modeling-relevant figures).\n- [ ] PDF size sanity check: ≥ 5 MB (anything smaller suggests images were dropped during conversion).\n- [ ] Commit lands and is pushed if the repo has a remote at that point.\n\n## Open questions / dependencies\n\n1. **Repo accessibility on ace-linux-2.** The kaggle-rogii-2026 sibling repo is currently local-only on ace-linux-1 (no GitHub remote). Two paths:\n   - Push kaggle-rogii-2026 to a new GitHub repo (\\`vamseeachanta/kaggle-rogii-2026\\`, public or private). Cleanest but requires user decision on visibility/license.\n   - Bundle the repo via \\`git bundle\\` and copy to ace-linux-2 manually.\n2. **Kaggle CLI auth on ace-linux-2.** Will need a separate \\`kaggle auth login\\` OAuth flow on that machine, or copy of \\`~/.kaggle/credentials.json\\` (preferred to re-auth fresh).\n\n## Why deferred (and not just sudo'd here)\n\nUser direction 2026-05-05: \\\"ace-linux-2 has libreoffice. Let us just keep this task for that machine. Add a new issue for it.\\\" Avoids ~400 MB of LibreOffice packages on ace-linux-1 where they wouldn't be reused.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2651","display_title":"Convert ROGII task brief PPTX to PDF on ace-linux-2 (LibreOffice required)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2647","title":"ANNOUNCE: llm-wiki spinout in progress (parallel-session heads-up)","issue_number":2647,"body":"## What\n\nA spinout migration is **currently executing** to move the engineering wikis (`knowledge/wikis/`) and seeds (`knowledge/seeds/`) from `vamseeachanta/workspace-hub` into a new dedicated public repo at **`vamseeachanta/llm-wiki`**.\n\nThis issue exists so any other agent or session reading the workspace-hub issue tracker is aware that:\n- `knowledge/wikis/` paths in workspace-hub will become invalid mid-flight\n- A new public repo `vamseeachanta/llm-wiki` is being created and populated\n- ~12 WRK-numbered issues are being transferred from `vamseeachanta/worldenergydata` to `vamseeachanta/llm-wiki`\n- ~6 issues are being re-routed to `vamseeachanta/digitalmodel`, 1 each to OGManufacturing and saipem\n\n## Scope split\n\n| Stays in workspace-hub | Moves to llm-wiki |\n|------------------------|-------------------|\n| `scripts/data/llm-wiki/` (corpus pipeline) | `knowledge/wikis/` (8 domain wikis) |\n| `scripts/knowledge/llm_wiki.py` | `knowledge/seeds/` |\n| `scripts/knowledge/` helpers | `knowledge/wikis/cross-links.md` |\n| `.claude/skills/research/llm-wiki/` | `tests/fixtures/llm-wiki/` (data) |\n| `.claude/skills/coordination/llm-wiki-roadmap-integration/` | |\n| `.claude/state/llm-wiki-completeness-loop/` | |\n| `data/document-index/` | |\n\n**Architectural model:** workspace-hub continues to play orchestration / tooling-hub role for sibling repos. `llm-wiki` is the *artifact / storehouse* — consumed by workspace-hub's pipeline, not run by it.\n\n## Authoritative references\n\n- Migration plan: `vamseeachanta/worldenergydata` → `docs/plans/2026-05-05-llm-wiki-spinout-migration-plan.md`\n- Override basis: this overrides the 2026-04-23 stay-embedded decision in workspace-hub#2398\n- License: MIT (code/tooling) + CC-BY-4.0 (wiki content)\n- Vendor-derivative PDFs (DNV/API standards) removed to `/mnt/ace/` (not in any git repo)\n\n## What parallel sessions should do\n\n1. **DO NOT** edit files under `knowledge/wikis/` or `knowledge/seeds/` until the cleanup PR `chore/llm-wiki-spinout-cleanup` lands — your edits will be lost when the rsync runs.\n2. **DO NOT** open new issues for wiki content — file them in the new `vamseeachanta/llm-wiki` repo once it exists, or wait and file in workspace-hub which will be migrated.\n3. **DO** continue normal work on workspace-hub orchestration, agents, skills — those paths are unaffected.\n4. **DO** treat any issue numbered #130-#142, #144-#154, #157, #172, #208 in `vamseeachanta/worldenergydata` as in-flight — their final home is changing.\n\n## Status tracking\n\nThe executor agent writes per-step status to `vamseeachanta/worldenergydata @ .planning/llm-wiki-migration-status.md`. Watch that file (or this issue's comments) for live progress.\n\nThis issue will be closed once the migration completes successfully and a final summary is posted as a comment.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2647","display_title":"ANNOUNCE: llm-wiki spinout in progress (parallel-session heads-up)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2650","title":"chore(knowledge): post-spinout cleanup for llm-wiki migration","issue_number":2650,"body":"## Summary\nTrack post-migration cleanup work after llm-wiki spinout to vamseeachanta/llm-wiki on 2026-05-05.\n\n## Tasks\n- [ ] Update workspace-hub Python files that reference removed wiki paths (~33 files identified in migration plan §3b)\n- [ ] Drop `/knowledge/wikis/*` allow-listing from .gitignore (handled in PR #2649)\n- [ ] Verify enforcement-gate.yml and skills-validation.yml workflows still pass after cleanup PR merges\n- [ ] Update `project_llm_wiki_stays_embedded.md` memory note → `project_llm_wiki_spunout.md` (handled by spinout executor)\n- [ ] Re-route per-repo issues #144-#150 from worldenergydata to digitalmodel (Step 8 of migration plan)\n- [ ] Conditional: Re-route #147 to OGManufacturing if repo exists\n- [ ] Conditional: Re-route #208 to saipem if repo exists\n- [ ] Conditional: Re-route #152 to frontierdeepwater if repo exists\n- [ ] Confirm `data/document-index/` stays in workspace-hub (intentional — derived index, not wiki content)\n- [ ] Fix tier-1 baselines (assethold ruff/mypy, worldenergydata pytest) so future PRs don't need `--no-verify`\n\n## Reference\n- Override directive: 2026-05-05 user message\n- Original decision: #2398 (closed 2026-04-23, comment added)\n- Cleanup PR: #2649\n- Migration plan: vamseeachanta/worldenergydata @ docs/plans/2026-05-05-llm-wiki-spinout-migration-plan.md\n- Heads-up issue: #2647","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2650","display_title":"chore(knowledge): post-spinout cleanup for llm-wiki migration"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2330","title":"Verify first run of daily-repo-readiness cron (trig_019GWtRosbZ9rw1HxrGpsvy9)","issue_number":2330,"body":"## Context\n\nRemote trigger `daily-repo-readiness` (id `trig_019GWtRosbZ9rw1HxrGpsvy9`) was created on 2026-04-17 and is scheduled for first run at **2026-04-18 11:00 UTC** (6am America/Chicago). It clones workspace-hub in Anthropic's cloud and runs a 6-check readiness sweep (GSD drift, repo hygiene, open PRs, issue backlog, planning health, dep drift), posting results as a comment on a rolling issue labeled `repo-readiness`.\n\n## Verification after 2026-04-18 11:00 UTC\n\n- [ ] A new issue titled *Daily repo readiness tracker* was created by the automation account (or a comment was added if it already existed)\n- [ ] The \\`repo-readiness\\` label was used correctly\n- [ ] The report markdown renders cleanly (sections, status icons, links)\n- [ ] GSD drift check reports \\`1.36.0 == 1.36.0\\` (current state) or flags if a newer release landed overnight\n- [ ] \\`gh\\` CLI auth worked in the CCR session (no auth-failure abort message)\n\n## If verification fails\n\nInspect run logs at https://claude.ai/code/scheduled/trig_019GWtRosbZ9rw1HxrGpsvy9. Common failure modes:\n- \\`gh\\` auth not configured in CCR environment → need to attach a GitHub token\n- Label creation race if first run hit \\`gh label create\\` twice → ignore non-fatal errors (label now exists ahead of first run)\n- Report >400 lines → tighten prompt\n\n## Close when\n\nEither the first run produces a clean tracking issue, OR a follow-up issue is opened with the specific remediation needed.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2330","display_title":"Verify first run of daily-repo-readiness cron (trig_019GWtRosbZ9rw1HxrGpsvy9)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2641","title":"feat(solver-queue): hands-off multi-machine inbox ingestion for OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA","issue_number":2641,"body":"## Summary\nAdd a hands-off multi-machine solver batch pipeline so a user can drop manifests/input files into a known inbox, have Linux hosts ingest/queue them, and have Windows licensed hosts run OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA/ANSYS jobs via periodic polling without manually touching the machines.\n\n## Current evidence\n- Existing solver queue exists: `queue/pending/` -> `queue/completed/` or `queue/failed/`, documented in `docs/solver/README.md` and `queue/job-schema.yaml`.\n- Existing submission path supports only `orcawave` and `orcaflex`: `scripts/solver/submit-job.sh`, `scripts/solver/submit-batch.sh`, `scripts/solver/validate_manifest.py`.\n- `ace-linux-2` is reachable and has AI/dev/open-source simulation tooling, but does **not** have OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, AQWA/ANSYS, MATLAB, or Python `OrcFxAPI`; it should coordinate/ingest/preprocess, not execute licensed solver jobs.\n- `ace-linux-2` NFS access to the ace-linux-1 knowledge/data center is via `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace`, not direct `/mnt/ace`.\n- `ace-linux-2` cron is drifted and points at stale `/mnt/workspace-hub` paths while the observed workspace is `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`.\n- `licensed-win-1` / `licensed-win-2` are registry-designated OrcaFlex/ANSYS hosts, but no SSH access is configured; Windows Task Scheduler/Git Bash is the current execution route.\n\n## Required design\n1. Define canonical inbox/drop locations and file contract:\n   - repo inbox: `queue/inbox/` for Git-backed manifests\n   - optional shared-store inbox under `/mnt/ace/...` on ace-linux-1 and `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace/...` on ace-linux-2\n   - Windows-local workspace path mapping for `D:\\workspace-hub`\n2. Add a cron-safe ingestion wrapper that scans the inbox, validates manifests, atomically stages accepted jobs into `queue/pending/`, moves invalid submissions to quarantine, logs every action, and commits/pushes only accepted queue changes.\n3. Extend schema/validation/submission for `aqwa` or `ansys-aqwa` jobs with explicit input/output patterns and batch command contract.\n4. Extend Windows queue processor dispatch to route jobs by solver and host capability:\n   - OrcaWave/OrcaFlex on licensed Orcina host\n   - AQWA/ANSYS on ANSYS host\n   - no licensed solver execution on ace-linux-2\n5. Add scheduled-task definitions in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` and Windows Task Scheduler installation notes/scripts for licensed hosts.\n6. Repair or supersede ace-linux-2 cron path drift using canonical schedule rendering.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] A manifest/input dropped into the canonical inbox is ingested without manual machine access.\n- [ ] Valid OrcaWave/OrcaFlex jobs appear in `queue/pending/` and are picked up by licensed Windows queue processing.\n- [ ] AQWA/ANSYS jobs validate through schema and route to the ANSYS-capable host instead of being rejected as unknown solver types.\n- [ ] Invalid submissions are quarantined with actionable error logs, not silently ignored.\n- [ ] `ace-linux-2` scheduled tasks use `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub` or a verified symlink/path contract, not stale `/mnt/workspace-hub` references.\n- [ ] Tests cover manifest validation, inbox ingestion, atomic moves/quarantine, and solver dispatch selection.\n- [ ] Documentation explains roles: ace-linux-1 control plane, ace-linux-2 ingestion/preprocess/overflow worker, licensed Windows hosts solver execution.\n\n## Related\n- Parent/related: #1586 solver queue hardening\n- Related: #2170 Windows licensed tool probe adapters\n- Related: #1564 ace-linux-2 crontab sync\n- Related: #1119 multi-machine work distribution\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2641","display_title":"feat(solver-queue): hands-off multi-machine inbox ingestion for OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1581","title":"Post-update display/VNC recovery on ace-linux-2","issue_number":1581,"body":"## Context\nAfter OS update on ace-linux-2, the KVM display (NVIDIA T400 + EDID drop) and VNC access likely need re-validation and re-application of the fix.\n\n## Root Cause (WRK-307)\nNVIDIA T400 loses EDID signal when KVM switches away. Wayland makes it worse. X11 + forced EDID config is the workaround. OS updates can reset:\n- `/etc/gdm3/custom.conf` (re-enable Wayland)\n- Kernel DRM card numbering (`card2-DP-3` may change)\n- NVIDIA driver DFP-N mappings\n- Stale EDID in `/etc/X11/edid.bin`\n\n## Existing Scripts\n- `scripts/operations/system/fix-kvm-display-ace-linux-2.sh`\n- `scripts/operations/connection/vnc-ace-linux-2.sh`\n\n## Steps\n1. SSH verify: `ssh vamsee@ace-linux-2 'echo ok'`\n2. Check Wayland state: `grep WaylandEnable /etc/gdm3/custom.conf`\n3. Check NVIDIA driver: `nvidia-smi`\n4. Check DRM card numbering: `ls /sys/class/drm/ | grep DP`\n5. Re-run display fix: `bash scripts/operations/system/fix-kvm-display-ace-linux-2.sh`\n6. If card numbering changed, update the fix script + re-capture EDID\n7. Verify KVM display works after switching away and back\n8. Verify VNC: `bash scripts/operations/connection/vnc-ace-linux-2.sh`\n9. Permanent fix: DP EDID emulator dongle (~$10)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] KVM display works (switch away and back without loss)\n- [ ] Wayland confirmed disabled in GDM\n- [ ] EDID captured and Xorg config applied\n- [ ] DRM card mapping verified and script updated if changed\n- [ ] VNC connection works from dev-primary\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1581","display_title":"Post-update display/VNC recovery on ace-linux-2"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2646","title":"feat(workstations): package ace-linux-2 direct-work SSH/VNC handoff runbook","issue_number":2646,"body":"## Context\nOnce `ace-linux-2` has a clean repo/mount baseline, we need a concrete operator/agent handoff package so work can be launched directly from this computer or through the existing VNC session without relying on chat memory.\n\nParent/context:\n- Baseline inventory: `docs/ops/2026-05-04-multimachine-baseline-inventory.md`\n- Parent solver/multimachine issue: #2641\n- Readiness dependency: the new `ace-linux-2` repo/mount/path readiness issue\n- Related work: #2523 reusable Hermes preflight checker, #2524 dispatch ledger/reconciler, #1583 config parity.\n\nLive check from 2026-05-05:\n- SSH to `ace-linux-2` works.\n- `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub` exists on `ace-linux-2`.\n- `x11vnc` and `vncserver` are installed on `ace-linux-2`.\n- `x11vnc` is listening on `ace-linux-2:5900` bound to localhost.\n- A local SSH tunnel already exists on this computer: `ssh -L 5900:localhost:5900 vamsee@ace-linux-2 -N`.\n- `xtigervncviewer localhost:5900` is currently running locally.\n\n## Deliverable\nCreate a durable `ace-linux-2` direct-work handoff runbook/prompt pack that lets an operator or agent launch bounded work from this computer via SSH/VNC with clean repo state, clear paths, validation, and return evidence.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope:\n- Document the exact handoff paths and roles:\n  - control machine: `ace-linux-1` / current computer\n  - worker machine: `ace-linux-2`\n  - workspace: `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`\n  - rawdata/knowledge access: `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace`\n  - scratch/cache: `/mnt/dde` or `/mnt/local-analysis`\n- Create a reusable preflight checklist for each handoff:\n  - SSH reachable\n  - VNC/tunnel reachable when GUI is needed\n  - repo clean/synced\n  - branch/worktree ownership explicit\n  - required tools present (`claude`, `codex`, `gemini`, `hermes`, `gh`, `git`, `uv`, `tmux`)\n  - no licensed-solver assumption on Linux\n- Create a self-contained prompt template for `ace-linux-2` workers with:\n  - issue number\n  - allowed paths\n  - forbidden paths\n  - tests/validation commands\n  - GitHub update expectations\n  - completion/blocker return format\n- Link or update the VNC instructions to use the actual script path `scripts/operations/connection/vnc-ace-linux-2.sh`.\n- Define the return protocol: where logs/results go and what GitHub comment format the worker should post.\n\nOut of scope:\n- Implementing a scheduler/queue dispatcher.\n- Solver execution on Windows hosts.\n- Running actual issue implementation before plan approval.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A repo-tracked handoff runbook or prompt-pack artifact exists and references exact paths, not vague machine names.\n- The artifact includes SSH-only and VNC-needed handoff modes.\n- The artifact includes a copy/paste worker prompt template for `ace-linux-2`.\n- The artifact records the VNC health check and how to reconnect using `scripts/operations/connection/vnc-ace-linux-2.sh`.\n- The artifact explicitly forbids storing secrets/tokens and forbids assuming OrcaWave/OrcaFlex/AQWA are available on `ace-linux-2`.\n\n## Planning / workflow note\nFollow the workspace issue workflow: draft plan under `docs/plans/`, adversarial review, `status:plan-review`, user approval, then implementation.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2646","display_title":"feat(workstations): package ace-linux-2 direct-work SSH/VNC handoff runbook"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2645","title":"fix(workstations): normalize ace-linux-2 repo, mount, and path readiness","issue_number":2645,"body":"## Context\nThis is the baseline issue that makes `ace-linux-2` safe to use as a direct work/delegation host from `ace-linux-1` or via VNC.\n\nParent/context:\n- Baseline inventory: `docs/ops/2026-05-04-multimachine-baseline-inventory.md`\n- Parent solver/multimachine issue: #2641\n- Related existing issues: #1582, #1583, #1564, #1565, #2520, #2523, #2524\n\nCurrent findings from 2026-05-05 checks:\n- `ace-linux-2` has `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub` present.\n- `ace-linux-2` can see `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace` and `/mnt/dde` per baseline inventory.\n- Local clones observed on `ace-linux-2`: `workspace-hub`, `cli-anything-repo`.\n- Required Tier-1 local clones for direct work are not yet normalized: `digitalmodel`, `assetutilities`, `worldenergydata`; optional by workload: `assethold`.\n- Registry/cron drift exists: registry and old cron references use stale `/mnt/workspace-hub`; observed workspace is `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`.\n- `ace-linux-2` is an AI/open-source preprocessing worker, not a licensed solver host.\n\n## Deliverable\nPrepare and document a verified `ace-linux-2` repo/mount/path baseline so work can be assigned there without guessing paths or repo availability.\n\n## Scope\nIn scope:\n- Verify or create local clones on `ace-linux-2` for required Tier-1 repos:\n  - `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`\n  - `/mnt/local-analysis/digitalmodel`\n  - `/mnt/local-analysis/assetutilities`\n  - `/mnt/local-analysis/worldenergydata`\n- Decide and implement either:\n  - update `config/workstations/registry.yaml` to `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`, or\n  - create/test a compatibility symlink `/mnt/workspace-hub -> /mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub` and document why.\n- Verify mount access from `ace-linux-2`:\n  - read-only/read-mostly: `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace`\n  - local scratch/cache: `/mnt/dde`\n  - workspace: `/mnt/local-analysis`\n- Produce a small repeatable probe/report for repo + mount + path status.\n- Update the baseline inventory doc with final verified state.\n\nOut of scope:\n- Running OrcaWave/OrcaFlex/AQWA on `ace-linux-2`.\n- Copying `/mnt/ace` or project/rawdata buckets wholesale.\n- Windows solver-host bootstrap.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A command or script can be run from `ace-linux-1` to prove the required `ace-linux-2` repo/mount/path baseline.\n- Required repos either exist locally and are syncable, or the issue documents an explicit blocker per repo.\n- `config/workstations/registry.yaml` no longer contradicts the observed `ace-linux-2` workspace path, or the compatibility symlink is created and documented.\n- Baseline inventory doc records the final paths and any remaining blockers.\n- No secrets/tokens are captured in logs, reports, issue comments, or docs.\n\n## Planning / workflow note\nFollow the workspace issue workflow: draft plan under `docs/plans/`, adversarial review, `status:plan-review`, user approval, then implementation.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2645","display_title":"fix(workstations): normalize ace-linux-2 repo, mount, and path readiness"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2643","title":"feat(llm-wiki): plan metadata-only /mnt/ace-data raw-like source coverage triage","issue_number":2643,"body":"# feat(llm-wiki): plan metadata-only /mnt/ace-data raw-like source coverage triage\n\n## Context\nA user requested extending llm-wiki completion from raw data to llm-wiki completeness for `/mnt/ace-data/raw data`.\n\nLive prerequisite checks found:\n- `/mnt/ace-data` resolves to `/mnt/ace`.\n- The exact literal path `/mnt/ace-data/raw data` does **not** exist.\n- Metadata-only discovery found 14 raw-like directories under `/mnt/ace-data` (`raw`, `raw_data`, or `Raw`), with no raw content copied into git/wiki.\n- Existing overlap must be preserved: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13 is the llm-wiki roadmap anchor, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2392 is the coverage-gap detector issue, and vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2487 already delivered the raw-data-to-readiness spine/control-plane.\n\n## Problem\nThe raw-like mounted source estate is not yet consistently classified into:\n1. llm-wiki/document-intelligence candidates,\n2. existing/in-flight coverage,\n3. code/vendor/internal exclusions,\n4. private/project-sensitive exclusions, and\n5. future source-family package candidates requiring separate approval.\n\nWithout this metadata-only triage layer, agents risk either missing high-value source families or over-promoting raw/private/project data into git/wiki.\n\n## Metadata-only inventory evidence\nRepresentative discovered raw-like directories:\n\n| Source path | Metadata-only count | Initial routing |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `/mnt/ace-data/O&G-Standards/raw` | 28,803 files / 692 dirs | standards candidate, reconcile with vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#25/#2373/#2392/#2487 and completed bounded standards promotions |\n| `/mnt/ace-data/client_projects/energy_bsee/raw_data` | 10 files | offshore/BSEE candidate; no direct duplicate issue found |\n| `/mnt/ace-data/digitalmodel/docs/aqwa/data/scripts/mooring_analysis/raw` | 28 files | AQWA candidate; map to vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2103 rather than duplicate |\n| `/mnt/ace-data/digitalmodel/docs/orcaflex/literature/examples/raw` | 38 files / 76 dirs | OrcaFlex examples candidate; map to vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2124 rather than duplicate |\n| `/mnt/ace-data/frontierdeepwater/data/raw` | 4 files / 1 dir | offshore source candidate; no direct duplicate issue found |\n| `/mnt/ace-data/worldenergydata/data/modules/hse/raw` | 97 files / 7 dirs | HSE/safety data candidate; no direct duplicate issue found |\n| `/mnt/ace-data/docs/disciplines/misc/projects/0136_das/.../Data/Raw` | 215 files / 8 dirs | indexed as `acma-projects`/project-management gap; project-sensitive until separately approved |\n\nNon-corpus/exclusion examples include empty `.gitkeep` raw dirs, Rust crate internals, Struts image assets, QGIS generated DXF output, SSI marketing images, OPM parser source internals, and similar code/vendor/generated/personal/project-sensitive surfaces.\n\n## Scope\nCreate a canonical plan for a metadata-only raw-like source triage and routing artifact. The plan should:\n- preserve the raw-data boundary: no copying/promoting raw content to git/wiki;\n- classify discovered raw-like roots by source family, sensitivity, target wiki/domain, existing issue mapping, and recommended next action;\n- update or define a durable metadata/routing artifact under the document-intelligence surface;\n- link resulting source-family work back to vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2392, and vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2487;\n- identify which families need separate follow-up issues before any summary promotion.\n\n## Out of scope\n- No raw data import into git/wiki.\n- No direct llm-wiki page generation from raw documents in this issue.\n- No standards content extraction from proprietary/copyrighted documents.\n- No project-sensitive corpus promotion (SESA/Woodfibre/ACMA/DAS/client material) without separate explicit approval.\n- No implementation before canonical plan review and user approval.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] A canonical plan is created under `docs/plans/` and indexed in `docs/plans/README.md`.\n- [ ] The plan cites the metadata-only inventory, `data/document-index/mounted-source-registry.yaml`, `data/document-index/index.jsonl`, `docs/document-intelligence/data-intelligence-map.md`, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2392, and vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2487.\n- [ ] The plan includes a raw-like source routing matrix covering all discovered raw-like roots.\n- [ ] Existing/in-flight issue mappings are explicit: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2103 for AQWA, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2124 for OrcaFlex examples, vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#25/#2373/standards wave issues for O&G standards, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2540/#2541-#2544/#2559 for Elements-derived material.\n- [ ] The final plan-review comment requests user approval and labels this issue `status:plan-review`.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2643","display_title":"feat(llm-wiki): plan metadata-only /mnt/ace-data raw-like source coverage triage"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2632","title":"META: rebind 3 llm-wiki plan-approved issues stuck on missing approval markers (#2368/#2124/#2125)","issue_number":2632,"body":"## Summary\n\nThree llm-wiki issues at `status:plan-approved` (#2368, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2124, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2125) have been stuck since 2026-04-28 due to a missing/stale approval-binding state. Codex's 10-thread parallel-implementation lanes correctly refused to execute, and the gate has held for 5+ days without remediation. This META issue captures the rot so it can be cleaned up in one pass.\n\n## Why\n\nPer the vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460 contract (memory: `project_issue_2460_approval_binding.md`), `status:plan-approved` GitHub label is necessary but not sufficient — execution requires a revision-bound approval marker at `.planning/plan-approved/<n>.md` that names the approved plan SHA + review artifact paths. Otherwise agents can't confirm WHICH version of the plan was approved.\n\nThis was specifically designed as a load-bearing user-in-loop gate (memory: `feedback_never_offer_to_self_label_plan_approved.md`) — agents must NOT self-approve by creating the marker themselves.\n\nThe gate is doing its job. But the cleanup hasn't happened, and three valuable llm-wiki improvements have been frozen.\n\n## Per-issue state (verified 2026-05-03)\n\n### vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#22 faceted portal pages for large LLM-wiki domains\n\n- GH labels: `status:plan-approved,status:working,agent:codex` ✓\n- `.planning/plan-approved/2368.md`: **MISSING**\n- Local plan `docs/plans/2026-04-23-issue-2368-faceted-portal-pages.md`: blob `b391641688` — header says `Status: draft` (REGRESSED from v2)\n- Approved revision per Codex's 2026-04-28 comment: SHA `1c01062d8` — header in that SHA says `Status: draft (v2 — addresses r1 findings)`, blob `b1df634b3`\n- Codex blocker comment: 2026-04-28T09:17:06Z (3 retry passes, all gated)\n- Likely cause of plan regression: `feedback_merge_race_silent_revert.md` pattern during auto-sync\n\n**Remediation:**\n```bash\ngit checkout 1c01062d8 -- docs/plans/2026-04-23-issue-2368-faceted-portal-pages.md\n# Then create .planning/plan-approved/2368.md with marker:\n#   plan_sha: 1c01062d8\n#   plan_blob: b1df634b3943db8f99888cb120ccf9be6ce245fe\n#   review_artifacts:\n#     - scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2368-claude.md\n#     - scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2368-codex.md\n#     - scripts/review/results/2026-04-23-plan-2368-gemini.md\n#   approved_by: vamseeachanta\n#   approved_at: <ISO timestamp of human approval>\ngit add docs/plans/2026-04-23-issue-2368-faceted-portal-pages.md .planning/plan-approved/2368.md\ngit commit -m \"markers(plan-approved): bind vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#22 to v2 SHA 1c01062d8\"\n```\n\n### vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2124 extend ingestion to Orcina resources/examples/training\n\n- GH labels: `status:plan-approved,status:working,agent:codex` ✓\n- `.planning/plan-approved/2124.md`: **MISSING**\n- Plan file: **MISSING** entirely (no `docs/plans/*2124*` exists)\n- Codex blocker comment: 2026-04-28T09:18:13Z — also flags upstream dependency on vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2471 still being open\n- Note: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2471 status as of 2026-05-03 (verify before remediating)\n\n**Remediation:** plan needs to be authored or located before binding. May be that the plan was lost to the same merge-race that ate vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#22's v2 content.\n\n### vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2125 auto-refresh ingestion on Orcina releases\n\n- GH labels: `status:plan-approved,status:working,agent:codex` ✓\n- `.planning/plan-approved/2125.md`: **MISSING**\n- Plan file: **MISSING** entirely (no `docs/plans/*2125*` exists)\n- Codex 2026-04-28T06:15:27Z comment indicates an implementation pass DID complete: branch `codex/10thread-20260428-issue-2125`, commit `e4b6193236a70ed2ec960deb92b3f65b9f876616`. But no merge to main and label still says `status:working`.\n\n**Remediation:** check if commit `e4b6193236` is salvageable; either land it or rebind plan + re-execute.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis META issue is for **diagnosis + decision-making**, not implementation:\n\n- Confirm the 3 issues' approval-binding state matches what's recorded above (re-verify before acting)\n- For each: decide rebind / re-plan / archive / continue-as-is\n- File ONE remediation issue per surviving plan\n- Update the relevant memory files if the rebinding workflow needs refinement\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Each of vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#22, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2124, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2125 reaches one of: (a) properly bound + ready for execution, (b) deferred with explicit ADR, (c) closed as superseded\n- The meta cleanup commit pattern is documented as a precedent for future \"approval-binding rot\" sweeps\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Implementation of the 3 issues' actual deliverables (separate downstream work after rebinding)\n- Codex 10-thread lane reactivation (that's a separate workflow concern)\n\n## References\n\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460 (closed) — original approval-binding contract; memory: `project_issue_2460_approval_binding.md`\n- Memory: `feedback_never_offer_to_self_label_plan_approved.md` — why agents can't self-approve\n- Memory: `feedback_merge_race_silent_revert.md` — likely cause of vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#22 plan regression\n- vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13 — llm-wiki strengthening epic (parent for vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#22/#2124/#2125)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2615 (closed 2026-05-03) — recent successful approval-binding precedent (Tier C closure)\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2632","display_title":"META: rebind 3 llm-wiki plan-approved issues stuck on missing approval markers (#2368/#2124/#2125)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117","title":"WRK-470: feat(gtm): oil-and-gas practitioner persona + 1-month GTM plan for workspace-hub ecosystem","issue_number":117,"body":"## WRK-470: feat(gtm): oil-and-gas practitioner persona + 1-month GTM plan for workspace-hub ecosystem\n\n\n\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** high | **Category:** business | **Subcategory:** gtm\n\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** medium | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [ ] Write 1-page \"value brief\" targeting the Oil Man persona (PDF via `data:md-to-pdf`)\n- [ ] Identify 3 flagship calculation demos: (1) CP cathodic protection per DNV-RP-B401,\n- [ ] Create `specs/gtm/oil-man-persona.md` with full persona card\n- [ ] Draft capability map: which repo covers which O&G discipline\n- [ ] Build shareable Jupyter notebook: CP design + DNV compliance check\n- [ ] Build shareable Jupyter notebook: production decline forecast (worldenergydata)\n- [ ] Create short README / landing copy for each repo targeting O&G audience\n- [ ] Record or document a \"5-minute demo\" flow: input → calculation → PDF report\n- [ ] Identify 10 target contacts: LinkedIn (subsea engineers, PE consultants,\n- [ ] Draft outreach message templates (email + LinkedIn DM)\n- [ ] Prepare FAQ: \"Is this open source?\", \"Does it replace OrcaFlex?\",\n- [ ] Set up simple intake: GitHub Discussions or email alias\n- [ ] Share demo notebooks on LinkedIn (personal + engineering groups)\n- [ ] Post in relevant communities: SPE LinkedIn group, r/petroleum, O&G Slack channels\n- [ ] Reach out to 5 warm contacts individually\n- [ ] Capture feedback: what resonates, what gaps remain\n- [ ] Write retrospective WRK item for Month 2 based on learnings\n- [ ] `specs/gtm/oil-man-persona.md` committed with full persona card\n- [ ] 2 shareable demo notebooks published (CP + decline curve)\n- [ ] 1-page value brief PDF generated\n- [ ] Outreach templates drafted and reviewed\n- [ ] Week 4 retrospective WRK item created\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\n- [x] **1. Capture** — done\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** — done\n- [x] **3. Triage** — done\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** — done\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** — done\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** — done\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** — done\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** — done\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** — done\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** — done\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** — done\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** — done\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** — done\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** — done\n- [x] **16. User Review** — done\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** — n\n- [ ] **18. Close** — pending\n- [ ] **19. Archive** — pending\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117","display_title":"WRK-470: feat(gtm): oil-and-gas practitioner persona + 1-month GTM plan for workspace-hub ecosystem"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2640","title":"feat(gtm): worldenergydata production decline forecast — shareable Jupyter notebook for GTM demo","issue_number":2640,"body":"## Context\n\nIssue [#117](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117) acceptance criteria includes:\n> Build shareable Jupyter notebook: production decline forecast (worldenergydata)\n\nThe `worldenergydata` library now has a production-ready `ArpsDeclineCurve` module at `worldenergydata.production.forecast.decline`. The GTM candidate review (2026-05-04) confirmed it works end-to-end:\n- Hyperbolic, exponential, harmonic curve fitting via scipy\n- EUR (Estimated Ultimate Recovery) calculation\n- 240-month forecast with confidence intervals\n- Plotly interactive visualization\n\nA 63 KB FDAS GTM HTML report was already generated (`reports/gtm/2026-05-04-fdas-field-development-economics.html`), which includes an Arps decline demo with synthetic data.\n\n## Scope\n\nCreate a standalone Jupyter notebook at `worldenergydata/notebooks/gtm_production_decline_forecast.ipynb`:\n\n1. **Section 1: Module overview** — what Arps decline is, three model types, typical GoM/North Sea use cases\n2. **Section 2: Synthetic demo** — fit a synthetic GoM well (qi=5000 bbl/month, Di=0.08/month, b=0.6), show EUR=146 Mbbls\n3. **Section 3: Multi-well portfolio** — fit 3 hypothetical wells (early, mid, late decline stages), side-by-side comparison\n4. **Section 4: Sensitivity** — how EUR changes with b (hyperbolic exponent) from 0.2 to 1.0\n5. **Section 5: Connect to FDAS economics** — feed EUR directly into `EconomicsQuery.npv()` to compute field-level NPV from decline curve\n\nAll sections must run without external data (synthetic data generated in-notebook).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Notebook executes end-to-end with `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute` in <60 seconds\n- [ ] All Plotly charts render (interactive HTML output cells)\n- [ ] A companion `reports/gtm/gtm_production_decline_forecast.html` is exported from the notebook\n- [ ] Notebook added to `worldenergydata/notebooks/README.md` index\n- [ ] No BSEE binary data required to run\n\n## References\n\n- Parent: [#117](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117)\n- Implemented ArpsDeclineCurve: `worldenergydata/src/worldenergydata/production/forecast/decline.py`\n- FDAS GTM report with Arps demo: `worldenergydata/reports/gtm/2026-05-04-fdas-field-development-economics.html`\n- Generation script reference: `worldenergydata/scripts/gtm/generate_fdas_gtm_report.py`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2640","display_title":"feat(gtm): worldenergydata production decline forecast — shareable Jupyter notebook for GTM demo"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609","title":"tracker(digitalmodel-tests): post-#543 main has 244+ broken tests, not 20 as Quality Gates reports","issue_number":2609,"body":"## Headline\n\nQuality Gates' \"20 failed, 3000 passed\" report on digitalmodel main `60d59565` is the `pytest --maxfail=20` short-circuit ceiling, **NOT the true count**. A local repro on `60d59565` (without `--maxfail`) captured **184 unique FAILED + 60 unique ERROR = 244 broken tests** before the run was killed mid-collection. True count is likely higher — pytest never emitted its `=== short test summary ===` block.\n\nThe real number is **~12x larger** than CI reported.\n\n## Evidence — bucket counts (local repro 2026-05-02 23:46Z)\n\n### FAILED breakdown (184 unique)\n\n| Bucket | Unique FAILED | Most-affected modules |\n|---|---|---|\n| `marine_ops/` | **77** | `legacy/test_mooring_catenary.py`, `test_mooring_catenary.py`, `test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py`, `legacy/test_component_database.py`, `test_unified_rao_reader.py`, `environmental_loading/test_ocimf.py`, `legacy/test_wave_spectra.py`, `test_catenary_adapter.py`, `integration/test_*` |\n| `solvers/` | **33** | `orcaflex/test_orcaflex_cli.py` (26), `orcaflex/modular_generator/`, `orcaflex/mooring-tension-iteration/`, `orcaflex/reporting/test_*_fixture_snapshot.py` |\n| `hydrodynamics/` | **25** | `diffraction/test_cli_integration.py` (12), `diffraction/test_benchmark_runner.py` (4), `diffraction/test_solver_smoke_unit.py`, `diffraction/test_unit_box_benchmark.py` |\n| `infrastructure/` | **20** | `core/test_database_manager.py` (14), `core/test_cache.py` (4), `contracts/test_api_contracts.py` |\n| `field_development/` | **16** | `test_economics.py` (TestCarbonSensitivity, TestFiscalRegimeTaxAdjustment, TestScheduleDeclineIntegration) |\n| `orcaflex/` | **4** | `test_mooring_design.py` catenary, `test_installation_analysis.py` DAF |\n| Other | **9** | naval_architecture (2), reservoir (2), orcawave (2), solver (2), contracts (2), simple_engine (1) |\n| **Total FAILED** | **184** | |\n\n### ERROR breakdown (60 unique — collection-time / fixture problems)\n\n| Bucket | Unique ERROR | Most-affected modules |\n|---|---|---|\n| `infrastructure/` | **36** | `core/test_cache.py` (28 — Redis fixture missing), `core/test_database_manager.py` (8 — SQL fixture missing) |\n| `solvers/` | **13** | `blender_automation/test_batch_processor.py` (4), `orcaflex/modular_generator/test_campaign_generator.py` (6), `orcaflex/test_orcaflex_converter_enhanced.py` (3) |\n| `orcawave/` | **5** | `test_builder_orchestration.py` (4), `test_report_builder.py` (1) |\n| `hydrodynamics/` | **3** | `passing_ship/test_passing_ship_cli.py` (2), `aqwa/test_aqwa_lis_parser_real_data.py` |\n| `data_systems/` | **3** | `test_config_loader.py` validate_schema |\n| **Total ERROR** | **60** | |\n\n## Inferred root-cause clusters (need verification before per-bucket issue creation)\n\n- **Marine ops mass-failure (77)** — concentration in `legacy/`, `catenary/`, `test_mooring_catenary.py` (12 tests), `test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py` (16 tests) suggests a SHARED dependency or fixture broke. Likely 1-3 root causes, not 77.\n- **OrcaFlex CLI (26)** — all 26 in `test_orcaflex_cli.py` likely mean the CLI command isn't registered/findable on PATH in the local env. Single fix.\n- **Infrastructure cache+db_manager (36 ERROR + 18 FAILED)** — Redis/SQL service deps absent locally. Verify against [#2585](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2585) inventory to ensure no overlap.\n- **Field-dev economics (16)** — TestCarbonSensitivity, TestFiscalRegimeTaxAdjustment, TestScheduleDeclineIntegration — these look like REAL bugs in a feature [#2076](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2076) / [#2079](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2079) / [#2081](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2081) thought was working. Cross-check against those closeouts.\n- **Hydrodynamics diffraction (25 + 3 ERROR)** — `test_cli_integration.py` 12 failures all CLI-flavored — same CLI-not-on-PATH pattern as orcaflex CLI? Or different root cause? Needs investigation.\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) — closed; covered 10 of the 244+ named tests narrowly (citations + capsys yml-utilities). 4% of the failure surface. Closing it does NOT and CANNOT green Quality Gates.\n- [#2585](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2585) — sister tracker for the **26 muted** entries (collect_ignore audit). This umbrella covers the **active 244+** failures — separate population from the muted set.\n- [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) — introduced the `pytest --maxfail=20` ceiling and `curves.py` removal that exposed the 244+ failures previously masked by collection-time poison-pills.\n\n## Plan reference\n\nFull evidence and methodology: `docs/plans/2026-05-02-issue-2580-quality-gates-followups.md` (in workspace-hub repo).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- Each bucket above gets either:\n  - A sub-issue tracking remediation, OR\n  - A documented \"won't fix\" entry with explicit reason (e.g., \"muted via collect_ignore — covered by [#2585](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2585)\").\n- Quality Gates eventually reports a passing run on digitalmodel main.\n- Per-bucket triage determines real-bug vs. flake vs. import-error vs. service-dep before sub-issues are filed (categorical determinations need investigation, not pre-classification).\n\n## Out of scope for this umbrella\n\n- Per-bucket sub-issues (file as evidence accumulates per bucket).\n- Modifications to `conftest.py` or any test source.\n- Re-triggering or patching the Quality Gates CLI (separate followup — the CLI's `output_tail` JSON-wrap truncation is what hid the 244+ count from CI logs in the first place).","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2609","display_title":"tracker(digitalmodel-tests): post-#543 main has 244+ broken tests, not 20 as Quality Gates reports"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2628","title":"epic(digitalmodel-ci): domain-divided CI architecture replacing maxfail-masking pattern","issue_number":2628,"body":"## Why now\n\nThis session surfaced that digitalmodel's QG suite has been chronically green-washed by \\`maxfail=20\\` masking 200+ pre-existing failures across 53 test files. The admin-merge-via-Quality-Gates-failure pattern (PRs [#541](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/541), [#544](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/544), [#549](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/549), [#550](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/550), [#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551), [#553](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/553), [#568](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/568), [#569](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/569)) is now the default merge path — operationally fast but strategically corrosive: every PR carries the same 'gate trips on unrelated debt' justification.\n\nRoot cause: a single repo-wide \\`pytest --maxfail=20\\` invocation can't distinguish 'this PR introduced a regression in its own scope' from 'unrelated debt elsewhere in the repo.' Per-domain CI fixes both.\n\n## Architecture\n\n**14-15 test domains** (per [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) sweep + tests/ tree):\n\n\\`asset_integrity\\`, \\`citations\\`, \\`contracts\\`, \\`field_development\\`, \\`hydrodynamics-diffraction\\`, \\`hydrodynamics-passing-ship\\`, \\`infrastructure-core\\`, \\`marine-engineering\\`, \\`naval-architecture\\`, \\`orcaflex\\`, \\`orcaflex-solver\\`, \\`solver-smoke\\`, \\`specialized\\`, \\`structural\\`, \\`workflows\\`, \\`misc\\`\n\nEach domain becomes:\n- a separate pytest job in a GH Actions matrix\n- with its own \\`--with\\` dep list (so e.g. redis/psycopg2/motor only attach to \\`infrastructure-core\\`, not the whole CI)\n- with its own conftest fixtures and skipif policies\n- with the existing \\`maxfail\\` removed (per-domain failure counts stay bounded by domain size)\n\n## Decisions locked (this session, vamseeachanta)\n\n| Decision | Choice |\n|---|---|\n| Domain owner | All domains owned by \\`vamseeachanta\\` (single owner; CODEOWNERS encodes this) |\n| Aggregator gate policy | **All domains must pass** for merge (no informational-only mode) |\n| Run cadence | **Touched-domains-only on PRs**; **full matrix nightly** (cron) |\n| Deprecation policy | No test deletion or blanket-skip without AI-attested 'source X is gone, no other consumers' commit per \\`feedback_attestation_enables_contradiction_detection\\` |\n\n## Sub-tasks\n\n- [ ] **A** — Domain inventory PR (\\`tests/DOMAINS.md\\` mapping every test directory → domain → dep set; informs all subsequent PRs). Read-only repo analysis. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **B** — \\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml\\` refactor: one gate per domain with its own command + deps. Aggregator gate depends on all. Drops repo-wide \\`maxfail\\`. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **C** — GH Actions matrix workflow: \\`.github/workflows/quality-gates-by-domain.yml\\` with \\`strategy.matrix.domain\\` for parallel runs + aggregator job. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **D** — Touched-domain detection: PR-time job that diffs changed paths against \\`tests/DOMAINS.md\\` and emits the matrix subset to run. Nightly cron uses full matrix. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **E** — Per-domain conftest extraction: move shared fixtures/skipifs from \\`tests/conftest.py\\` into each domain's local conftest. ~1 PR per domain (small each). \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **F** — CODEOWNERS file with \\`vamseeachanta\\` as default owner. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **G** — Update workspace-hub agents.md + CLAUDE.md to encode the 'always test by domain during development' rule for AI agents. \\`workspace-hub\\`.\n\n## After A-G land, the deferred cluster work re-routes by domain\n\n| Cluster | Issue | New domain home |\n|---|---|---|\n| A — sys.modules pollution | [#2623](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623) | \\`contracts\\` domain |\n| D — infrastructure dep wall | [#2624](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624) | \\`infrastructure-core\\` domain — its own \\`--with redis --with psycopg2-binary --with motor\\` |\n| E — marine-engineering regressions | [#2625](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625) | \\`marine-engineering\\` domain — its own targeted suite |\n\nThese clusters become much cheaper to fix per-domain than under repo-wide CI.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Domain inventory exists and is reviewable\n- [ ] PR for trivial change (e.g. docs typo) only runs domains that own changed paths; runs in <2 min\n- [ ] PR that touches multiple domains runs all touched domains; aggregator gates correctly\n- [ ] Nightly full matrix runs all domains; failure surfaces per-domain in GH Actions UI\n- [ ] No test files deleted as part of this work; debt that surfaces is filed per-domain for owner triage\n- [ ] Existing admin-merge cycle ends — next post-A-G PR merges through the new gate without override\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) — investigations that surfaced the masking\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) — issue-state drift / quality-gates audit umbrella\n- [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) — PR that introduced \\`maxfail=20\\`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2628","display_title":"epic(digitalmodel-ci): domain-divided CI architecture replacing maxfail-masking pattern"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2403","title":"feat(doc-intel): embeddings model-selection spike — BGE-M3 / Voyage / text-embedding-3-large","issue_number":2403,"body":"## Context\n\nSplit from #2393 (v1 closed after MAJOR×2 cross-provider review flagged scope creep: model-selection spike bundled with implementation). This issue isolates the spike.\n\n## Problem\n\nBefore building the embeddings index, we need an evidence-based model choice. Candidates differ by cost, latency, recall, and local-vs-cloud footprint.\n\n## Deliverable\n\nA single decision document at `docs/document-intelligence/embeddings-model-selection.md` comparing:\n- **BGE-M3** (local via Ollama — $0 compute, higher latency)\n- **Voyage-3** (cloud, non-OpenAI — mid cost, strong recall)\n- **text-embedding-3-large** (cloud, OpenAI — low cost, strong recall)\n\nEvaluation rubric:\n1. Recall@10 on a synthetic eval set (≥50 queries with known-good doc_key targets drawn from current L2 summaries + L3 wiki pages).\n2. $/1000 docs at current corpus scale.\n3. p95 latency per query.\n4. Secret-surface + vendor lock-in footprint.\n\nDocument concludes with a chosen model + 2-sentence rationale.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/document-intelligence/embeddings-model-selection.md` committed with rubric + decision\n- [ ] Eval set at `tests/fixtures/embeddings/eval-set.jsonl` with ≥50 queries\n- [ ] Reproducibility script at `scripts/knowledge/run_embeddings_spike.py`\n- [ ] Cost/latency/recall numbers cited with measurement commit SHA\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- Blocks: #2402 (embeddings index build+query, to be filed)\n- No hard deps on other issues.\n\n## Scope boundaries\n\n- **In scope:** comparison, measurement, decision document.\n- **Out of scope:** production index build, query CLI, cron wiring — these live in successor issue #2402.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2403","display_title":"feat(doc-intel): embeddings model-selection spike — BGE-M3 / Voyage / text-embedding-3-large"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2626","title":"fix(security): narrow #2552 external-contributor runbook tests + scenario 3 — drop privacy-leaking test, define ingestion vector, resolve interaction-limit contradiction","issue_number":2626,"body":"**Source:** #2552 plan-review queue audit 2026-05-03. Per [#2552 approval marker](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/.planning/plan-approved/2552.md), the runbook content was approved despite a 4+ day Tier-D persistent-MAJOR pattern (claude/codex/gemini all MAJOR across 4+ rounds). The architectural defects under those MAJORs are deferred here for a focused fresh plan.\n\n## Defects to address (from latest reviews)\n\n### 1. Privacy-leaking test (Gemini, repeat)\nThe proposed `test_runbook_references_triggering_issues_without_personal_name` requires hardcoding the external commenter's PII into the test file to assert its absence. **Resolution:** drop the negative-presence assertion; only positively assert presence of issue numbers (#2401, #2546, #2550).\n\n### 2. Undefined ingestion vector under `collaborators_only` lockdown (Gemini, repeat)\nPlan tells operators to reply via `CONTRIBUTOR_INTEREST_TEMPLATE` but #2546's `collaborators_only` lockdown blocks the input channel. **Resolution:** explicitly define the ingestion vector (likely email or a designated public form) since GitHub interactions are blocked.\n\n### 3. Security-posture contradiction in scenario 3 (Gemini, repeat)\nScenario 3 suggests \"temporary lift of interaction limit\" — globally undermines #2546's lockdown for one request. **Resolution:** either (a) drop scenario 3 entirely from in-scope work, or (b) define a per-user allowlist mechanism that doesn't lift the global limit.\n\n### 4. Plan-internal contradictions (Claude, repeat)\n- Untestable acceptance criteria (negative-presence checks for arbitrary unspecified strings)\n- HTML-comment metadata contradicts surrounding table (plan was patched in layers without re-reading itself)\n\n**Resolution:** restart from a clean T1 spec; do not iterate on the existing plan.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] New plan filed under `docs/plans/` addressing items 1-4 explicitly\n- [ ] Plan goes through one Claude+Gemini review cycle (Codex skipped per #2479 unless version-pin lands first)\n- [ ] No regression on the runbook content already approved via #2552\n\n## Severity\n\nMedium — the runbook content is live (approved); this issue prevents drift between the runbook and its enforcement layer.","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2626","display_title":"fix(security): narrow #2552 external-contributor runbook tests + scenario 3 — drop privacy-leaking test, define ingestion vector, resolve interaction-limit contradiction"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2510","title":"feat(cad): build Python layout/CAD automation demo for chip/package geometries","issue_number":2510,"body":"## Context\nParent: #2507\nRoadmap: `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\n\nBuild a Python-first geometry/layout automation example that bridges CAD practices into semiconductor layout and package/interposer-style structures.\n\n## Proposed scope\n- Use KLayout scripting and/or GDSFactory to generate a parameterized layout artifact.\n- Export/import GDS/OASIS and at least one human-inspectable representation such as SVG/DXF where practical.\n- Extract metadata: layers, polygons, bounding boxes, ports/nets where available, geometry invariants.\n- Include tests around geometry invariants and repeatability.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Python module/script for parameterized layout generation.\n2. Sample output artifacts or reproducible generation command.\n3. Metadata extraction report.\n4. Tests and documentation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Demo can be run locally without proprietary tools.\n- Generated artifacts are deterministic enough for tests or snapshot checks.\n- Report explains relevance to chip design, packaging, and CAD automation roles.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2510","display_title":"feat(cad): build Python layout/CAD automation demo for chip/package geometries"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2550","title":"chore(security): codify public repo interaction-limit renewal in scheduled tasks","issue_number":2550,"body":"## Summary\nMove the public-repo collaborator-only interaction-limit renewal from a Hermes-local cron reminder into workspace-hub's canonical scheduled-task system, with deterministic verification and issue-comment reporting.\n\n## Why\nIssue #2546 applied `collaborators_only` interaction limits across all public `vamseeachanta/*` repos. GitHub requires interaction limits to expire; the longest supported expiry is `six_months`. A Hermes cron job currently renews every 150 days, but this should be made repo-visible and auditable through workspace-hub scheduled-task governance.\n\n## Scope\n- Add a deterministic script that enumerates `vamseeachanta` public repos via `gh repo list`.\n- Re-apply `gh api -X PUT repos/{owner}/{repo}/interaction-limits -f limit=collaborators_only -f expiry=six_months`.\n- Verify every public repo reports `limit=collaborators_only` and capture `expires_at`.\n- Post or prepare a verification report referencing #2546.\n- Register the job in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` with a cadence safely before expiry, e.g. every 150 days / quarterly equivalent supported by the scheduler.\n- Add tests for dry-run mode and parser/report formatting.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Renewal logic is in repo-tracked script(s), not only Hermes-local cron state.\n- [ ] Script has a dry-run mode suitable for CI/offline validation.\n- [ ] Script fails non-zero when any public repo is not `collaborators_only` after renewal.\n- [ ] Schedule metadata includes command, log path, required capabilities, and operator description.\n- [ ] Documentation links back to #2546 and the current GitHub API limitation requiring expiry.\n\n## Related\n- Root hardening: #2546\n- Hermes-local renewal job created during closeout: `d9b2d1c2270d`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2550","display_title":"chore(security): codify public repo interaction-limit renewal in scheduled tasks"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625","title":"investigate(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster E — marine-engineering domain regressions (~60 failures across catenary/wave/ocimf/RAO)","issue_number":2625,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). ~60 failures concentrated in \\`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/\\` and adjacent. Without per-test detail (the maxfail=200 probe used \\`--tb=line\\` only), root causes can't be characterized inline.\n\n## Affected (by file, partial — full list at \\`/tmp/qg-failures-clean.txt\\`)\n\n| Count | File |\n|---:|---|\n| 16 | \\`test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py\\` |\n| 11 | \\`test_mooring_catenary.py\\` |\n| 11 | \\`legacy/test_mooring_catenary.py\\` (mirror of above) |\n| 5 | \\`test_unified_rao_reader.py\\` |\n| 4 | \\`test_catenary_adapter.py\\` |\n| 4 | \\`legacy/test_component_database.py\\` |\n| 3 | \\`test_marine_eng_perf.py\\` |\n| 3 | \\`integration/test_hydro_rao_integration.py\\` |\n| 3 | \\`catenary/test_simplified.py\\` |\n| ~10 | scattered across catenary/wave/ocimf integration files |\n\n## Recommended next step\n\nRun a targeted \\`--tb=short\\` re-run on \\`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/\\` to characterize root causes. Likely sub-clusters:\n\n- Domain code regressions (real defects in catenary solver, RAO reader, etc.)\n- Missing data files (test fixtures gone)\n- Knock-on effects of test pollution (could be related to Cluster A → [#2623](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623))\n\nAfter per-cluster characterization, file individual sub-trackers and link back to this umbrella.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nThis issue is the umbrella; close it once sub-trackers are filed and assigned.\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625","display_title":"investigate(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster E — marine-engineering domain regressions (~60 failures across catenary/wave/ocimf/RAO)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2552","title":"docs(security): external contributor and unsolicited paid-help response runbook","issue_number":2552,"body":"## Summary\nCreate a concise policy/runbook for handling non-collaborator comments, unsolicited paid-help offers, and external contributor requests on public repos.\n\n## Why\nIssue #2546 was triggered by an outside commenter offering paid implementation help on #2401. The technical lockdown is now in place for public repos, but operators still need a repeatable response path for existing comments, future exceptions, legitimate contributors, and paid external execution requests.\n\n## Scope\n- Document when to ignore, hide, report, or reply to external comments.\n- Define how to route legitimate contributor requests without granting broad access.\n- Define paid-pilot intake requirements: scope, NDA/confidentiality, repo access level, payment approval, and PR review gate.\n- Add a short issue-comment template for declining or redirecting unsolicited offers.\n- Link the policy from the security/governance docs or ops runbook.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Runbook covers unsolicited paid-help comments, spam, legitimate contributor requests, and collaborator onboarding.\n- [ ] Runbook preserves public-repo security posture: no collaborator access without explicit owner approval.\n- [ ] Templates avoid engaging suspicious accounts while keeping legitimate contributors routed through a safe process.\n- [ ] Documentation references #2546 and #2401 as the triggering example.\n\n## Related\n- Trigger comment context: #2401\n- Repository interaction lockdown: #2546\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2552","display_title":"docs(security): external contributor and unsolicited paid-help response runbook"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624","title":"policy(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster D — decide whether redis/psycopg2/motor belong in QG suite (51 errors)","issue_number":2624,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). 51 errors in \\`tests/infrastructure/core/\\` are environmental — pure dep-wall, not feature defects.\n\n## Affected\n\n- \\`tests/infrastructure/core/test_cache.py\\` (32 errors) — requires \\`redis\\` backend\n- \\`tests/infrastructure/core/test_database_manager.py\\` (19 errors) — logs \\`No module named 'psycopg2'\\` and references \\`motor\\` (async MongoDB)\n\nThese would also fail in the original CI run with the same env. Whether they were ever expected to run in the QG suite is a policy question.\n\n## Decision needed\n\nPick one:\n\n- **(a) Add deps to QG environment**: \\`--with redis --with psycopg2-binary --with motor\\` to the QG command. Keeps tests in the QG tier. Adds CI install time. Requires actual Redis/Postgres/Mongo containers in CI for tests to pass (not just import). Likely heaviest infrastructure lift.\n- **(b) Mark with import-probe skipif**: add \\`@pytest.mark.skipif(not _has_redis, reason=\\\"redis backend unavailable\\\")\\` per-class in those test files. Cheap fix; tests skip cleanly when the backend isn't available; tests still run on machines that have Redis/Postgres/Mongo.\n- **(c) Move to a separate \\\"integration\\\" tier outside QG**: e.g., a separate pytest mark or a dedicated tox env that QG doesn't invoke. Cleanest separation; requires a tier-routing decision.\n\nRecommendation: (b) for fastest unblock; (c) for long-term cleanliness.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] 51 errors in \\`tests/infrastructure/core/\\` either pass (with backends installed) or skip cleanly (no errors)\n- [ ] QG command produces 0 of these errors\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) — quality-gates audit umbrella","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624","display_title":"policy(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster D — decide whether redis/psycopg2/motor belong in QG suite (51 errors)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623","title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster A — sys.modules pollution leaking MagicMock into tests/contracts/ (2 failures)","issue_number":2623,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). Two tests in \\`tests/contracts/\\` fail in the full suite but pass in isolation — classic test-pollution Heisenbug, made deterministic by \\`-p no:randomly\\`.\n\n## Defect\n\n- \\`tests/contracts/test_assetutilities_common_contract.py::test_update_deep_dictionary_importable\\` → \\`AssertionError: <MagicMock> == 1\\`\n- \\`tests/contracts/test_assetutilities_data_contract.py::test_AttributeDict_importable\\` → \\`AssertionError: <MagicMock> == 'value'\\`\n\n## Root cause\n\nSome upstream test in the suite registers \\`assetutilities.common.update_deep\\` and \\`assetutilities.common.data\\` (or similar \\`assetutilities.*\\` modules) as \\`MagicMock()\\` in \\`sys.modules\\` without restoring them. With \\`-p no:randomly\\` the ordering is deterministic, so the pollution is reproducible. When the contract tests later import the real symbols they receive the mock.\n\n## Verification\n\n\\`pytest tests/contracts/\\` in isolation → **17/17 pass**. Sibling repo \\`../assetutilities/src/assetutilities/common/update_deep.py\\` carries both \\`update_deep_dictionary\\` and \\`AttributeDict\\` correctly.\n\n## Fix options\n\n- **(a) Hunt the polluter** — search candidates: tests that \\`import unittest.mock; sys.modules[...] = MagicMock()\\` or \\`@patch.dict('sys.modules', ...)\\` without proper teardown. Similar to the existing \\`tests/conftest.py:92\\` pattern for \\`digitalmodel.subsea.catenary_riser.legacy.catenary_riser_summary\\`. Then add proper teardown to that test.\n- **(b) Defensive autouse fixture** in \\`tests/contracts/conftest.py\\` that purges polluted entries before each test:\n  \\`\\`\\`python\n  for k in list(sys.modules):\n      if k.startswith('assetutilities') and isinstance(sys.modules[k], MagicMock):\n          del sys.modules[k]\n  \\`\\`\\`\n\nRecommend (a) for the right fix; (b) as a safety net if (a) requires more bisection time than is available.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Both contract tests pass in the full suite under \\`-p no:randomly\\`\n- [ ] If (a): polluter identified and fixed; \\`tests/contracts/\\` no longer needs special handling\n- [ ] If (b): conftest fixture documented with the polluter's path so it can be revisited\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster A — sys.modules pollution leaking MagicMock into tests/contracts/ (2 failures)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2523","title":"feat(workstations): add reusable Hermes preflight readiness checker","issue_number":2523,"body":"## Summary\n\nBuild a reusable workstation preflight checker for Hermes-led cross-machine dispatch. The checker should turn the manual `ace-linux-2` probe from #2519/#2520 into a repeatable command that verifies host reachability, login-shell tool visibility, provider runtime readiness, GitHub auth state, repo cleanliness, and engineering-tool smoke readiness before work is assigned.\n\n## Motivation\n\n#2519 established that `ace-linux-2` can run Hermes/Codex only through a login shell and that `gh` auth can independently be invalid. This needs to become an automated gate so future workstations are not delegated work based on stale or partial assumptions.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Add a script/CLI entrypoint for workstation preflight checks.\n- Support at least `ace-linux-1` local mode and `ace-linux-2` remote mode.\n- Use login-shell checks for remote machines where user-level tools live under `~/.local/bin` or `~/.npm-global/bin`.\n- Check and redact-sensitive-report:\n  - hostname / reachability / canonical workspace path,\n  - git repo branch/status/ahead-behind for tier-1 repos,\n  - `gh auth status` and `gh api user` success/failure without token values,\n  - Hermes provider/model config without secrets,\n  - `codex`, `claude`, `gemini` command availability/version,\n  - selected engineering tool presence/smoke checks.\n- Emit both JSON and markdown reports under `docs/reports/` or a configured artifacts directory.\n- Return non-zero only for clearly blocking failures; classify warnings vs blockers.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- A documented command can run the preflight against `ace-linux-2` via:\n  `ssh ace-linux-2 'bash -lc \"<probe>\"'` or equivalent.\n- Output never includes token/auth file contents.\n- The current `ace-linux-2` `gh` auth failure is represented as a GitHub-mutation blocker, not as a total machine failure.\n- Tests cover parsing/classification for at least reachable, auth-invalid, tool-missing, and clean-repo cases.\n- The #2519 handoff/prompts can reference this checker instead of embedding ad hoc shell snippets.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent: #2519\n- Blocker/auth repair: #2520\n- Committed baseline report: `docs/reports/2026-04-27-issue-2519-ace-linux-2-readiness-probe.md`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2523","display_title":"feat(workstations): add reusable Hermes preflight readiness checker"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2533","title":"feat(repo-portfolio): review and revise mission/objective statements across active repos","issue_number":2533,"body":"## Summary\n\nReview and revise the mission/objective statements across all active workspace repos so future GitHub issue work can quickly answer:\n\n1. what is this repo for?\n2. what work belongs here?\n3. what work should route elsewhere?\n4. is the repo active, support-only, client/archive, or deprecated/no-new-issues?\n\nThis is the portfolio-wide complement to the Tier-1 routing/indexing work already tracked under vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460-#2465.\n\n## Why\n\nThe Tier-1 contract now improves code placement and routing, but the portfolio still needs a canonical mission/objective layer across all active repos. Without that layer, agents can still mis-route issues between overlapping repos such as:\n\n- `workspace-hub` vs per-repo execution docs\n- `assetutilities` vs repo-specific utilities\n- `digitalmodel` vs client/project verticals\n- `investments` vs `assethold`\n- `client_projects` vs client-specific repos\n- active engineering repos vs archives/reference repos\n\n## Initial repo scope\n\nUse `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` and live immediate-child git repos as the starting inventory.\n\n### Tier-1\n\n- `workspace-hub`\n- `digitalmodel`\n- `assetutilities`\n- `aceengineer-website`\n\n### Tier-2\n\n- `OGManufacturing`\n- `acma-projects`\n- `frontierdeepwater`\n- `seanation`\n- `worldenergydata`\n- `investments`\n- `rock-oil-field`\n\n### Tier-3 / support / archive candidates\n\n- `aceengineer-admin`\n- `aceengineer-strategy`\n- `achantas-data`\n- `achantas-media`\n- `assethold`\n- `client_projects`\n- `doris`\n- `hobbies`\n- `pdf-large-reader` if present/active\n- `sabithaandkrishnaestates`\n- `saipem`\n- `sd-work`\n- `teamresumes`\n- `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS` if confirmed active in this workspace\n\n## Deliverables\n\n1. Canonical portfolio mission/objective artifact, preferably `docs/REPO_MISSION_PORTFOLIO.md` or an equivalent linked from `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md`.\n2. Per-repo classification:\n   - active product/library\n   - client vertical\n   - business/admin support\n   - archive/reference\n   - deprecated / no-new-issues\n3. Per-repo mission/objective statement grounded in the strongest available source:\n   - `.agent-os/product/mission.md`\n   - `README.md`\n   - `AGENTS.md`\n   - `docs/README.md`\n   - existing workspace-hub portfolio docs\n4. Per-repo routing rule:\n   - \"work belongs here when...\"\n   - \"work should route elsewhere when...\"\n5. Conflict/dedupe notes for overlapping repo missions.\n6. Update links from `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` and/or `docs/ROUTING_INDEX.md` so the mission artifact is discoverable.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Every active repo in `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` has a current mission/objective row.\n- [ ] Every immediate-child local git repo not listed in `docs/BUSINESS_BRAIN.md` is either added, explicitly excluded, or marked as inventory drift.\n- [ ] Each row has a source path proving where the mission came from.\n- [ ] Each row has an activity/status classification.\n- [ ] Each row has an issue-routing rule for work that belongs vs work that should route elsewhere.\n- [ ] Overlapping missions are explicitly called out and resolved or marked for follow-up.\n- [ ] Tier-1 mission/objective rows are consistent with vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460 and its child routing issues (#2461-#2465).\n- [ ] The artifact is linked from a canonical workspace discovery surface.\n- [ ] The plan receives adversarial review before implementation.\n\n## Suggested execution waves\n\n1. Wave 1: Tier-1 mission alignment and consistency with vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460-#2465.\n2. Wave 2: Tier-2 repo mission/objective review.\n3. Wave 3: Tier-3/support/archive classification and no-new-issues guidance.\n4. Wave 4: final portfolio table integration, dedupe/conflict notes, and discoverability links.\n\n## Related\n\n- Parent/umbrella: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1962\n- Tier-1 structure epic: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2397\n- LLM-wiki roadmap context: vamseeachanta/llm-wiki#13\n- Tier-1 routing contract: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2460\n- Tier-1 child issues: vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2461, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2462, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2463, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2464, vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2465\n\n## Planning requirements\n\nFollow the normal issue gate:\n\nIssue → resource intelligence → canonical plan in `docs/plans/` → adversarial plan review → `status:plan-review` → user approval → `status:plan-approved` → implementation.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2533","display_title":"feat(repo-portfolio): review and revise mission/objective statements across active repos"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2563","title":"Set up Telegram mobile access for Hermes AI control","issue_number":2563,"body":"## Summary\nSet up Telegram-based mobile access to Hermes/AI so Vamsee can interact with the agent, approve/adjust plans, tweak task lists, and monitor/steer work while on the move.\n\n## Current user state\n- Vamsee has logged into Telegram on the mobile device using phone/name.\n- Next user-side action is to create a Telegram bot via `@BotFather`; the bot token must be handled as a secret and must not be pasted into public issue comments or chat logs.\n\n## Recommended architecture\nUse a Telegram **bot** connected to the Hermes gateway on `ace-linux-1`, not a separate human Telegram account for the AI.\n\nRationale:\n- Telegram bots are the normal integration surface for programmatic message handling.\n- Bot token can be stored in `~/.hermes/.env` and loaded by the gateway service.\n- Access can be restricted to Vamsee's user/chat ID.\n- Hermes gateway already supports Telegram and can run as a persistent service.\n\n## Scope\n\n### Phase 1 — Private single-user Telegram bot\n- Create bot using `@BotFather`.\n- Securely store bot token in `~/.hermes/.env` or password manager.\n- Configure Hermes gateway Telegram integration.\n- Restrict access to Vamsee's Telegram user/chat ID.\n- Start gateway as persistent service on `ace-linux-1`.\n- Verify mobile command/response loop.\n\n### Phase 2 — Mobile operating guardrails\n- Keep approval mode `manual` or `smart`; do not use approval `off` for mobile operation.\n- Enable only mobile-appropriate toolsets initially.\n- Confirm secrets are never transmitted through Telegram.\n- Document restart/stop/status commands.\n- Create quick-reference command sheet for on-the-move tweaks.\n\n### Phase 3 — Optional enhancements after stable\n- Voice-message STT/TTS for hands-free operation.\n- Dedicated group/topic if DMs become cluttered.\n- Additional user allowlist if needed.\n\n## Task list\n- [ ] Create Telegram bot with `@BotFather` from Vamsee's phone.\n- [ ] Capture bot username and token securely; do not paste token into GitHub or chat.\n- [ ] Configure Hermes gateway Telegram platform with `hermes gateway setup` or config edit.\n- [ ] Add Telegram token to `~/.hermes/.env` and verify service can read it.\n- [ ] Configure allowed users/chats to Vamsee only for initial rollout.\n- [ ] Enable appropriate toolsets for mobile use: memory, session_search, cronjob, send_message, file, terminal, browser/web only as needed.\n- [ ] Install/start gateway persistently: `sudo $(which hermes) gateway install --system`, then start/status.\n- [ ] If systemd cannot see env vars, add `EnvironmentFile=/home/vamsee/.hermes/.env` to the service and restart.\n- [ ] Test `/help`, `/status`, a simple task tweak, and a safe approval-gated action from mobile.\n- [ ] Write a concise mobile command sheet for status checks, plan approvals, task-list updates, pause/kill commands, and GitHub-link requests.\n- [ ] Optional: configure/test STT/TTS with one short voice note.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Telegram bot responds to Vamsee from mobile through Hermes gateway.\n- Unauthorized users/chats are blocked or ignored.\n- Gateway survives shell logout/reboot via systemd or documented equivalent.\n- Bot token is stored only in secret/config surfaces, not committed or posted.\n- Mobile workflow supports: ask status, tweak a task list, request a GitHub link, and approve/revise/hold a plan.\n- Quick-reference command sheet exists in a durable repo/docs location.\n\n## Security / operations notes\n- Do not paste the bot token into this issue.\n- Prefer single-user allowlist for the first rollout.\n- Keep mobile destructive actions approval-gated.\n- Avoid approval mode `off` unless explicitly approved for a bounded test.\n- Check gateway logs at `~/.hermes/logs/gateway.log` for troubleshooting.\n\n## Suggested labels\n`enhancement`, `cat:ai-orchestration`, `cat:operations`, `domain:integrations`, `domain:notification`, `priority:high`, `status:pending`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2563","display_title":"Set up Telegram mobile access for Hermes AI control"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2517","title":"Compliance alert: W18 — 42% (high)","issue_number":2517,"body":"## Compliance Alert — 2026-04-27\n\n**Compliance rate:** 42% | **Severity:** high\n**Total commits:** 56 | **Reviewed:** 11 | **Unreviewed:** 15\n**Dashboard verdict:** FAIL\npass\n\n### Unreviewed Commits\n```\n  - 8daf482b planning: prepare cross-section reporting demo plan\n  - 90bfa08d docs: draft semiconductor layout CAD plan for issue 2510\n  - b09e5a7e feat(automation): add tier-1 indexing freshness audit (#2465)\n  - 7379ad38 feat: add semiconductor package FEM benchmark for issue 2511\n  - 827907f3 docs: sync issue 2514 plan approval state\n  - 310904bc docs: plan semiconductor package FEM benchmark for issue 2511\n  - 942efe2e docs: implement semiconductor CAD FEM knowledge base for issue 2508\n  - fb2ca7f0 docs: approve issue 2508 plan\n  - dc79c6ab docs: plan semiconductor CAD FEM knowledge base for issue 2508\n  - 6c98a65a Implement continuous planning pipeline report\n  - 268dbf8a docs: add chip design CAD FEM roadmap\n  - 3b924b32 docs: sync issue 2488 plan approval state\n  - 6cdae8a1 Approve plan for issue 2489\n  - 1496e02d docs: add issue 2489 exit handoff\n  - 187ead29 docs: revise continuous planning pipeline plan\n\n```\n\n### Thresholds\n| Range | Action |\n|-------|--------|\n| >= 80% | Pass — no action |\n| 50-79% | Medium priority issue |\n| 30-49% | High priority issue |\n| < 30% | Critical — immediate action |\n\n### Recommended Actions\n- [ ] Run cross-review on unreviewed commits\n- [ ] Investigate bypass patterns\n- [ ] Address root cause if systemic\n\n---\n*Auto-generated by `scripts/enforcement/compliance-cron.sh` (#2031)*\n*Dedup key: compliance-alert-2026-W18*","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2517","display_title":"Compliance alert: W18 — 42% (high)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2585","title":"tracker(digitalmodel-tests): 26 collect_ignore entries from 2026-03-24 slim-down need real fixes","issue_number":2585,"body":"## Context\n\nCommit [`2c185d2d`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/commit/2c185d2d) (2026-03-24, \"fresh repo after slimming\") added 26 entries to `digitalmodel/tests/conftest.py:collect_ignore` without filing follow-up issues. A subsequent commit `4515cd01` (2026-05-01, via [#2574](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2574)) added 3 more entries — those 3 ARE tracked in [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580). The other 26 are documented-but-unowned: comments say *why* they're skipped, but no owner, no exit criteria, no link to a fix plan.\n\nAudit reference: [`docs/sessions/2026-05-02-collect-ignore-cumulative-audit.md`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/docs/sessions/2026-05-02-collect-ignore-cumulative-audit.md)\n\nThis umbrella issue exists so that closing [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) does not silently leave 90% of the `collect_ignore` list untracked.\n\n## Inventory (26 untracked entries)\n\nAll paths relative to `digitalmodel/tests/`. Line numbers refer to `digitalmodel/tests/conftest.py` at HEAD as of 2026-05-02.\n\n| # | Path | Bucket | Inline comment | Line |\n|---|------|--------|----------------|------|\n| 1 | `marine_ops/artificial_lift/dynacard/test_vision_benchmark.py` | A. Missing local modules | \"standalone scripts, not real tests\" | 11 |\n| 2 | `solvers/orcaflex/examples_integration/test_converter.py` | A. Missing local modules | same | 12 |\n| 3 | `solvers/orcaflex/examples_integration/test_single_download.py` | A. Missing local modules | same | 13 |\n| 4 | `structural/fatigue_analysis/test_reference_seastate_scaling.py` | A. Missing local modules | same | 14 |\n| 5 | `visualization/design_tools/pilot_program/test_case_1_separator.py` | A. Missing local modules | same | 15 |\n| 6 | `solvers/orcaflex/test_orcaflex_unit.py` | B. Missing src modules | \"not yet implemented\" | 17 |\n| 7 | `structural/fatigue_apps/test_load_scaling.py` | B. Missing src modules | same | 18 |\n| 8 | `subsea/pipeline/test_on_bottom_stability.py` | B. Missing src modules | same | 19 |\n| 9 | `test_plate_capacity.py` | B. Missing src modules | same | 20 |\n| 10 | `visualization/test_anomaly_detection.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | \"Deleted orcaflex-dashboard service files\" | 22 |\n| 11 | `visualization/test_comparative_analysis.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 23 |\n| 12 | `visualization/test_component_classifier.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 24 |\n| 13 | `visualization/test_csv_parser.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 25 |\n| 14 | `visualization/test_data_validator.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 26 |\n| 15 | `visualization/test_loading_decoder.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 27 |\n| 16 | `visualization/test_sensitivity_analysis.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 28 |\n| 17 | `visualization/test_statistical_analysis.py` | C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | same | 29 |\n| 18 | `workflows/orcawave/test_com_connection.py` | D. Platform-specific / optional deps | \"Platform-specific or missing optional deps\" | 31 |\n| 19 | `workflows/orcawave/test_end_to_end.py` | D. Platform-specific / optional deps | same | 32 |\n| 20 | `workflows/orcawave/test_integration.py` | D. Platform-specific / optional deps | same | 33 |\n| 21 | `workflows/standalone/markitdown/test_converter.py` | D. Platform-specific / optional deps | same | 34 |\n| 22 | `test_workflow_checkpoints.py` | E. pytest-asyncio / hypothesis conflict | \"Requires pytest-asyncio plugin (disabled due to hypothesis conflict)\" | 36 |\n| 23 | `marine_ops/marine_engineering/test_component_database.py` | F. Data file deps not in git | \"hull profiles and chain database\" | 38 |\n| 24 | `hydrodynamics/hull_library/test_hull_library_expansion.py` | F. Data file deps not in git | same | 39 |\n| 25 | `specialized/cathodic_protection/test_abs_ship_variants_wrk271.py` | G. CP random-ordering shared state | \"fail intermittently with random ordering (shared state issue)\" | 41 |\n| 26 | `specialized/cathodic_protection/test_cathodic_protection_b401.py` | G. CP random-ordering shared state | same | 42 |\n\n## Per-bucket triage suggestion\n\n| Bucket | Entries | Suggested disposition |\n|--------|---------|------------------------|\n| A. Missing local modules | 1–5 | **Decide: convert to real tests OR delete.** Comment says \"standalone scripts, not real tests\" — if true, delete the files; if shippable as tests, port the missing local-module imports. Either way the `collect_ignore` line goes away. |\n| B. Missing src modules | 6–9 | **Fix or delete.** \"Not yet implemented\" — pair each test with its missing src module, and either implement the module or delete the test. No permanent skips. |\n| C. Deleted orcaflex-dashboard | 10–17 | **Delete the test files.** The service was deleted; the tests should follow. `collect_ignore` is the wrong durable home for \"tests of deleted code\". |\n| D. Platform-specific / optional deps | 18–21 | **Convert to `pytest.importorskip` / `pytest.mark.skipif`.** Skip-at-collect via `collect_ignore` masks the *reason* on Linux CI; in-test markers express the dep gate cleanly and let Windows/with-deps runs exercise the code. |\n| E. pytest-asyncio / hypothesis conflict | 22 | **Resolve the plugin conflict.** Either pin compatible versions or split the test from the hypothesis-using suite. Permanent skip is not acceptable for an async-checkpoint test. |\n| F. Data file deps not in git | 23–24 | **Add fixtures or `pytest.skip` with `xfail`-style data-missing diagnostic.** Hull profiles and chain database belong in `tests/fixtures/` (small samples) or downloaded by a fixture builder; either way the test should run when data is present and skip with a clear pointer when not. |\n| G. CP random-ordering shared state | 25–26 | **Fix the shared state.** This is a flake, not a skip-worthy condition — the tests should be deterministic regardless of ordering. Diagnose via `pytest -p no:randomly` to confirm, then isolate the shared state. |\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nThis umbrella is satisfied when **every one of the 26 entries** has reached one of:\n\n1. A dedicated sub-issue is filed and linked here, with a plan, and the entry is annotated in `conftest.py` with the issue number; OR\n2. A real fix has landed that allows removal of the `collect_ignore` line (verified by removing the line and the test passing in CI); OR\n3. A documented \"permanent skip\" justification exists at a stable doc path (e.g. `docs/decisions/`) AND the `conftest.py` comment cites that doc by path. The justification must explain why the test cannot be made to run on supported CI.\n\nThe umbrella issue itself does not deliver fixes — it tracks the disposition of every line. A close-out comment must enumerate the 26 entries and link each to its disposition (sub-issue, fix PR, or decision doc).\n\n## Relationship to other issues\n\n- **Sister to [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580)** — that issue covers exactly 3 of the 29 `collect_ignore` entries (`citations/test_registry.py`, `citations/test_schema.py`, `asset_integrity/test_yml_utilities_additional.py`, lines 45/46/50 in `conftest.py`). Closing [#2580](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2580) alone leaves the 26 entries above untracked — this umbrella prevents that.\n- **Cross-reference [#2414](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2414)** — title \"test(ecosystem-sync): autobuild or skip missing local git fixtures\" superficially looks similar to bucket F (data file deps), but the body confirms it scopes to `tests/ecosystem-sync/fixtures/repos/` in the **workspace-hub root**, not `digitalmodel/tests/`. **[#2414](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2414) does NOT cover any of the 26 entries above.** It remains its own concern.\n\n## Labels — fallbacks used\n\nThe requested labels `tracker`, `area:testing`, and `repo:digitalmodel` do not exist on this repo (verified via `gh label list` 2026-05-02). Applied fallback:\n- `domain:testing` (closest existing equivalent of `area:testing`)\n- No equivalent of `tracker` or `repo:digitalmodel` exists; the title prefix `tracker(digitalmodel-tests):` carries that semantics in lieu of a label.\n- No `status:needs-plan` label exists; left status unset (per project policy this issue lands at `status:plan-review` only after its own plan is drafted).\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2585","display_title":"tracker(digitalmodel-tests): 26 collect_ignore entries from 2026-03-24 slim-down need real fixes"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1583","title":"Hermes config parity via repo ecosystem templates","issue_number":1583,"body":"## Context\nClaude, Codex, and Gemini agent configs are managed via repo templates in `config/agents/{claude,codex,gemini}/` and synced to home directories by `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`. Hermes is not yet covered.\n\nThe `harness-update.sh` cron script already has `update_hermes()` but only runs on ace-linux-1. ace-linux-2 needs inclusion.\n\n## What Needs to Happen\n\n### A) Add Hermes templates to repo\n- `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template` — model defaults, toolsets, terminal (no secrets)\n- `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md` — persona template\n\n### B) Extend sync-agent-configs.sh\nAdd Hermes to `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh`:\n- Template: `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template`\n- Target: `~/.hermes/config.yaml`\n- Merge strategy: template provides defaults, machine-local overrides preserved\n- Skip auth.json and .env (secrets, never in repo)\n\n### C) Custom skills versioning\n- Bundled skills managed by `hermes update` (no action needed)\n- Custom workflow skills → `config/agents/hermes/custom-skills/` in repo\n- Sync script copies/symlinks to `~/.hermes/skills/`\n\n### D) Add ace-linux-2 to harness-update cron\nIn `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml`:\n```yaml\n# Change from:\nmachines: [dev-primary, ace-linux-1]\n# To:\nmachines: [dev-primary, ace-linux-1, dev-secondary, ace-linux-2]\n```\nThen run `setup-cron.sh` on ace-linux-2.\n\n### E) Memories policy\n- `MEMORY.md` and `USER.md` are personal/accumulated — do NOT sync via repo\n- They build naturally per machine as Hermes is used\n- Existing `claude-memory-backup` cron already backs up to ace-linux-2\n\n## Files to Create/Modify\n- [ ] `config/agents/hermes/config.yaml.template` (new)\n- [ ] `config/agents/hermes/SOUL.md` (new)\n- [ ] `scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh` (extend)\n- [ ] `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` (add ace-linux-2 to harness-update)\n- [ ] `docs/ops/scheduled-tasks.md` (update documentation)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Hermes config template exists in repo\n- [ ] sync-agent-configs.sh handles Hermes merge\n- [ ] harness-update cron runs on ace-linux-2\n- [ ] `bash scripts/_core/sync-agent-configs.sh --dry-run` shows Hermes sync\n- [ ] Running sync on a new machine bootstraps Hermes config correctly\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1583","display_title":"Hermes config parity via repo ecosystem templates"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112","title":"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking","issue_number":2112,"body":"## Summary\nBackfill the minimum SubseaIQ equipment-count fields required to unblock #2055 subsea cost benchmarking.\n\n## Context\nAssessment of #2055 found two active blockers:\n1. `status:needs-data`\n2. the required equipment-count fields are absent from every current GoM record in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n\nCurrent state from assessment:\n- required threshold: >=10 GoM fields with equipment counts\n- current state: 0/10 records have `num_trees`, `num_manifolds`, or `tieback_distance_km`\n\nWithout this backfill, the cost benchmarking implementation issue cannot proceed honestly.\n\n## Scope\n- Backfill or derive, for at least 10 GoM fields, the following fields in the SubseaIQ-derived dataset:\n  - `num_trees`\n  - `num_manifolds`\n  - `tieback_distance_km`\n- Document provenance / confidence for each backfilled field\n- Update the dataset and any ingestion/normalization helpers needed to preserve the fields\n- Add a minimal validation check proving the threshold is satisfied\n\n## Target data\n- `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n- any normalization or ingestion surfaces that write/read these fields\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- At least 10 GoM records contain non-null equipment counts and tieback distance\n- Provenance/confidence is documented\n- A validation command or test confirms the threshold is met\n- #2055 can remove `status:needs-data` after verification\n\n## Relationship\n- Direct prerequisite for #2055\n- Should be completed before any cost-per-tree / cost-per-manifold implementation work\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112","display_title":"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2016","title":"feat(gtm): client conversion pipeline -- turn repo capability into paying clients","issue_number":2016,"body":"     1|## Mission: Convert engineering capability to paying clients\n     2|\n     3|> \"All of this is paper work until I get some clients.\"\n     4|\n     5|ACE Engineer consulting firm with 23yr P.E. experience, $120K/yr retainer target. Everything in this repo (skills, workflows, knowledge bases, agent automation) is infrastructure toward that single goal.\n     6|\n     7|### Current GTM Position\n     8|\n     9|**What ACE does**: Core engineering -- OrcaFlex/mooring/riser/FEA/cathodic/API 579/Python\n    10|**Target**: Offshore 10-50 engineer firms\n    11|**GTM activity**: 1281 ace contacts, 20+ prospects in pipeline, job market scanner cron running\n    12|**Expert networks**: GLG, AlphaSights, Guidepoint (#1994)\n    13|\n    14|### What Exists vs What Needs to Exist\n    15|\n    16|**Has**:\n    17|- Engineering skills covering marine/offshore, drilling, FEA, CFD, standards\n    18|- 691 skills across external repos\n    19|- Knowledge bases and wiki infrastructure\n    20|- Automated email triage (3 Gmail accounts)\n    21|- Cron-based job market scanning\n    22|- Multi-agent workflow (Claude, Codex, Gemini)\n    23|- Repo as living demo of capability\n    24|\n    25|**Needs**:\n    26|1. Parametric demo reports (HTML/PDF) -- show, don't tell\n    27|2. Engineering chatbot demo -- see #engineering-chatbot skill\n    28|3. Expert network responses -- turn insights into revenue\n    29|4. Follow-up engine from 1281 contacts\n    30|5. Case studies from digitalmodel/ work\n    31|\n    32|### The Ask\n    33|\n    34|This issue tracks the conversion funnel from capability to revenue. Sub-issues for each GTM lever below.\n    35|\n    36|### Acceptance Criteria\n    37|- [ ] At least one paying client engaged via any channel\n    38|- [ ] Demo materials (parametric reports + chatbot) ready for outreach\n    39|- [ ] 100 expert network queries submitted with responses\n    40|- [ ] 50 personalized follow-ups sent from contact database\n    41|- [ ] 3+ public-facing engineering analyses published (aceengineer.com)\n    42|- [ ] Monthly revenue > $10K from any source\n    43|\n    44|### Related\n    45|- #1994 (expert network registration)\n    46|- #1993 (Rigzone job pipeline)\n    47|- GTM: aceengineer-strategy/ notes\n    48|\n    49|\n    50|## GTM EXECUTION PLAN — Ordered by Priority\n    51|Last updated: 2026-04-07\n    52|\n    53|### TIER 0 — IMMEDIATE REVENUE (this week)\n    54|Pure hustle, zero engineering work, highest ROI.\n    55|\n    56|| # | Sub-issues | Status | Effort |\n    57||---|-----------|--------|--------|\n    58|| 1994 | Register expert networks (GLG, AlphaSights, Guidepoint) | Open | 2 hours |\n    59|| 1993 | Rigzone job apps — check live postings, tailor resume, apply | Open | 4 hours |\n    60|\n    61|### TIER 1 — DEMO MATERIALS (show don't tell)\n    62|Demo 2 (wall thickness) is DONE. Build the rest in dependency order.\n    63|\n    64|| Step | Sub-issues | Status | Blocked By |\n    65||------|-----------|--------|-----------|\n    66|| Inventory models | 1904, 1932 | Open | -- |\n    67|| Vessel data | 1798 (HLV specs), 1799 (pipelay specs) | Open | -- |\n    68|| Demo 2 polish | 1831, 1833 (done), 1832 (--from-cache) | Open | -- |\n    69|| Demo 3 | 1931/1872 (mudmat, 180 cases) | OPEN | vessel data, cache flag |\n    70|| Demo 5 | 1874, 1905 (jumper model) | Open | Demo 3 code |\n    71|| Demo 1 | 1930/1871 (freespan, ~680 cases) | Open | cache flag |\n    72|| Notebook | 1792 (VIV demo notebook) | Open | Demo 1 |\n    73|| Demo 4 | 1873 (shallow water pipelay) | Open | pipelay specs, cache flag |\n    74|| Screencasts | 1809 (5x .gif recordings) | Open | all 5 demos |\n    75|\n    76|### TIER 2 — GTM INFRASTRUCTURE\n    77|Runs parallel with demo building.\n    78|\n    79|| Sub-issues | What | Status |\n    80||-----------|------|--------|\n    81|| 1707, 1708, 1709 | Scanner fixes (rate limiting, dedup, retention) | Open |\n    82|| 1971 | Email triage workflow (aceengineer.com) | Open |\n    83|\n    84|### TIER 3 — OUTREACH\n    85|Requires demos + infrastructure ready first.\n    86|\n    87|| Sub-issues | What | Blocked By |\n    88||-----------|------|-----------|\n    89|| 191 | Chatbot fundamentals — persona, tone, pitch template | None |\n    90|| 117 | Oil-and-gas persona + 1-month GTM plan | #191, 2+ demos |\n    91|| 1669 | Vessel contractor email outreach (20+ targets) | demos, #191, #117 |\n    92|| 197 | Narada/WhatsApp AI agent replicate guide | None (best after outreach starts) |\n    93|\n    94|### TIER 4 — PIPELINE MODULES (expand portfolio)\n    95|\n    96|| Sub-issues | What | Effort |\n    97||-----------|------|--------|\n    98|| 1834 | Field Development Pipeline Engineering skill | 16 hrs |\n    99|| 1835 | On-bottom stability (DNV-RP-F109) | 12 hrs |\n   100|| 1836 | Shore approach (HDD, trenching, pull-in) | 10 hrs |\n   101|| 1837 | Pipeline CAPEX cost model | 8 hrs |\n   102|\n   103|### CRITICAL PATH TO FIRST CLIENT\n   104|\n   105|```\n   106|#1994 (register expert networks) -- 2hrs --> $300-600/hr consultations\n   107|     |\n   108|     v\n   109|#1904 (inventory) --> #1798/#1799 (vessel data) --> #1931 (Demo 3) --> #1874 (Demo 5)\n   110|     |                                                      |\n   111|#1831/#1832 (cache infra) --> #1930 (Demo 1) --> #1873 (Demo 4) --> #1809 (GIFs)\n   112|     |\n   113|     v\n   114|#1669 (email outreach with demos) --> paying clients\n   115|```\n   116|\n   117|### RELATED ISSUES\n   118|- #108 — WRK-148: ACE-GTM parent strategy stream (Stage 17)\n   119|- #1670 — Energy company job market scan (CLOSED)\n   120|- #1671 — US-wide job market scan (CLOSED)\n   121|- #898 — Repo portfolio steering skill (CLOSED)\n   122|","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2016","display_title":"feat(gtm): client conversion pipeline -- turn repo capability into paying clients"}]}},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
