{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"codex_apps","tool":"github_search_issues","arguments":{"repo":"vamseeachanta/workspace-hub","query":"is:issue is:open label:cat:engineering","sort":"updated","order":"desc","topn":30},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"issues\": [{\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269\", \"title\": \"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation\", \"issue_number\": 2269, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nStandardize the canonical OpenFOAM baseline for portable engineering work, using repo-tracked docs, scripts, example cases, and validation.\\n\\nPart of #1782.\\nPrimary reference: #25.\\nSupports downstream work such as #1268.\\n\\n## Motivation\\nOpenFOAM is already CLI-native, but the baseline workflow is not yet fully encoded as a portable artifact bundle. We need a declared version target, canonical entrypoints, and a smoke validation path.\\n\\n## Scope\\nCreate the baseline OpenFOAM portability package in workspace-hub.\\n\\nExpected deliverables:\\n- baseline docs\\n- run/validation entrypoints\\n- minimal smoke example\\n- troubleshooting guidance\\n- shared skill update if reusable guidance is improved\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] baseline doc declares the target fork/version explicitly (default: ESI/OpenFOAM.com v2312 unless changed by decision)\\n- [ ] canonical runner command(s) are documented\\n- [ ] minimal smoke case exists under a reproducible repo-tracked path\\n- [ ] validator produces pass/fail output with explicit checks\\n- [ ] common failure modes and version/fork mismatches are documented\\n- [ ] workflow can be executed on the canonical engineering host with documented prerequisites\\n\\n## Notes\\nImplementation should prefer:\\n- explicit version detection and fail-fast behavior\\n- repo-tracked wrappers over tribal command memory\\n- logs/results/validation outputs in predictable locations\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269\", \"display_title\": \"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2003\", \"title\": \"Fix 5 failing tests in worldenergydata \\u2014 test suite cleanup (#1962 Phase 3)\", \"issue_number\": 2003, \"body\": \"## Context\\\\n\\\\nAs part of the Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring (#1962), the audit found worldenergydata has 5 failing tests that must be resolved before any refactoring can proceed safely.\\\\n\\\\n## Current State\\\\n\\\\n- Total tests: 431 (5 errors detected during collection)\\\\n- Command: `cd assetutilities && uv run python -m pytest ../worldenergydata/tests/ --noconftest` (or run from worldenergydata root)\\\\n\\\\n## Failing Tests\\\\n\\\\nRun `cd /mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub/worldenergydata && uv run python -m pytest tests/ -v 2>&1 | grep FAILED` to identify exact failures.\\\\n\\\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\\\n- [ ] All 431 tests pass with 0 errors\\\\n- [ ] No test regressions\\\\n- [ ] Commits follow TDD convention\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2003\", \"display_title\": \"Fix 5 failing tests in worldenergydata \\u2014 test suite cleanup (#1962 Phase 3)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2006\", \"title\": \"audit(worldenergydata): 14000+ orphan modules \\u2014 triage for archival vs active use (#1962 Phase 3)\", \"issue_number\": 2006, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nTier-1 Repo Audit (#1962) identified **10+ orphan modules** in worldenergydata (files with 0 imports).\\n\\n## Orphan Detection Method\\n\\nThe audit used grep-based import counting \\u2014 a weak heuristic that misses dynamic imports, __import__(), and importlib usage. So the true orphan count may be lower.\\n\\n## Work\\n\\n1. Run AST-based orphan detection (proper import analysis)\\n2. For each confirmed orphan: check if it's a CLI entry point (not imported but executed)\\n3. Archive truly unused modules or mark with `# CLI entry point` header\\n4. Update documentation accordingly\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] AST-based orphan scan complete\\n- [ ] All orphans categorized (archive / CLI / dynamic import / keep)\\n- [ ] Unused code archived with clear git history\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2006\", \"display_title\": \"audit(worldenergydata): 14000+ orphan modules \\u2014 triage for archival vs active use (#1962 Phase 3)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2005\", \"title\": \"audit(digitalmodel): worldenergydata test collection timeout + 14K bytecode files \\u2014 investigate and resolve\", \"issue_number\": 2005, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nTier-1 Repo Audit (#1962) found critical infrastructure issues in worldenergydata:\\n\\n### Test Collection Timeout\\n- The test suite times out during collection (>30s)\\n- Cannot even enumerate tests, let alone run them\\n- Likely caused by heavy imports or expensive fixture setup\\n\\n### 14,145 Bytecode Files\\n- Extremely deep dependency tree\\n- 11.71s compile time \\u2014 major code smell\\n- May indicate excessive dependencies in pyproject.toml\\n- Consider lazy imports or splitting dev dependencies\\n\\n## Investigation Steps\\n\\n1. `uv run python -c \\\"import time; start=time.time(); import worldenergydata; print(f'import took {time.time()-start:.2f}s')\\\"`\\n2. Audit pyproject.toml \\u2014 check for unnecessary dev dependencies being installed\\n3. Profile imports: `python -X importtime -m worldenergydata 2>&1 | tail -50`\\n4. Identify which 14K .pyc files belong to which packages\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] Test collection completes within 30s\\n- [ ] All 431 tests pass with 0 errors\\n- [ ] Bytecode file count explained or reduced\\n- [ ] pyproject.toml cleaned if needed\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2005\", \"display_title\": \"audit(digitalmodel): worldenergydata test collection timeout + 14K bytecode files \\u2014 investigate and resolve\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962\", \"title\": \"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring \\u2014 audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode\", \"issue_number\": 1962, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nSystematic refactoring of the workspace-hub repo ecosystem, starting with tier-1 repositories. The goal is to reduce architectural debt, consolidate duplicate patterns, enforce consistent structure, and improve agent navigability across all tier-1 repos.\\n\\n## Tier-1 Repos (Priority Order)\\n\\n| # | Repo | Purpose | Maturity | Depends On |\\n|---|------|---------|----------|------------|\\n| 1 | `digitalmodel/` | Engineering digital twin (asset integrity, CP, GIS, hydro) | beta | assetutilities |\\n| 2 | `assetutilities/` | Shared engineering utilities (config, units, calculations) | stable | \\u2014 |\\n| 3 | `assethold/` | Asset portfolio financial analysis | beta | assetutilities |\\n| 4 | `worldenergydata/` | Global energy market data (BSEE, EIA, drilling) | beta | assetutilities |\\n| 5 | `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS/` | CAD solvers, models, database, utilities | beta | \\u2014 |\\n| 6 | `aceengineer-website/` | Consulting website | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| 7 | `aceengineer-strategy/` | GTM, business strategy | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n| 8 | `sabithaandkrishnaestates/` | SKEstates Inc. business ops | \\u2014 | \\u2014 |\\n\\n## Refactoring Scope\\n\\n### Phase 1: Audit (analysis-only, no code changes)\\n- [ ] Run `repo-architecture-analysis` on each tier-1 repo\\n- [ ] Identify cross-repo duplicate code (especially assetutilities consumers)\\n- [ ] Map dependency graph (imports, shared configs, data flows)\\n- [ ] Catalog test coverage per repo (current state)\\n- [ ] Identify orphaned/dead code, unused modules, oversized files (>400 lines)\\n- [ ] Score each repo against the Control-Plane Contract (AGENTS.md, provider adapters)\\n- [ ] Identify skill duplicates across repos (134 known from vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 audit)\\n\\n### Phase 2: Plan (per-repo refactor plans)\\n- [ ] Generate refactor plan per tier-1 repo with estimated effort\\n- [ ] Prioritize by: business value \\u00d7 technical debt severity \\u00d7 dependency impact\\n- [ ] Identify breaking changes and migration paths\\n- [ ] Define test coverage targets (tier-1 = 85% per pytest.tier1.ini)\\n\\n### Phase 3: Execute (TDD, incremental, per-repo)\\n- [ ] Execute refactors repo-by-repo following dependency order (assetutilities first)\\n- [ ] TDD mandatory \\u2014 tests before implementation\\n- [ ] Each repo refactor as a separate sub-issue for tracking\\n\\n## Execution Strategy: Claude Code Plan Mode (Cloud)\\n\\n### Claude Code `--permission-mode plan` (\\\"Ultraplan\\\")\\n\\nClaude Code v2.1.92 supports `--permission-mode plan` which restricts the agent to **read-only analysis and planning** \\u2014 no file writes, no shell mutations. This is ideal for Phase 1 (Audit) and Phase 2 (Plan).\\n\\n**Proposed execution:**\\n\\n```bash\\n# Phase 1: Audit each tier-1 repo in plan mode (safe, read-only)\\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\\\\n  --print \\\\\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\\\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\\\\n  -p \\\"Audit the repository architecture of digitalmodel/. Analyze: module structure, dependency graph, test coverage, dead code, file sizes >400 lines, AGENTS.md compliance. Output a structured JSON report.\\\"\\n\\n# Phase 2: Generate refactor plan\\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\\\\n  --print \\\\\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\\\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\\\\n  -p \\\"Given the audit report, generate a prioritized refactor plan for digitalmodel/. Include: breaking changes, migration steps, test targets, estimated effort per module.\\\"\\n```\\n\\n### Cloud Execution Considerations\\n\\n| Aspect | Status | Notes |\\n|--------|--------|-------|\\n| **Claude Code CLI** | Installed (v2.1.92) | Available on ace-linux-1 |\\n| **Plan mode** | Supported | `--permission-mode plan` \\u2014 read-only, safe for audit |\\n| **API-key mode** | Supported | `--bare` + `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for headless/cloud runs |\\n| **Print mode** | Supported | `-p` / `--print` for non-interactive batch execution |\\n| **Budget cap** | Supported | `--max-budget-usd` to control spend per run |\\n| **Cloud sandbox** | Possible | Can run via Hermes `delegate_task` with `acp_command='claude'` |\\n| **Max subscription** | Claude Max $200/mo | Rate-limited but sufficient for plan-mode audit |\\n\\n### Recommended Approach\\n1. **Phase 1 + 2 (Audit + Plan)**: Run Claude Code in plan mode via `delegate_task(acp_command='claude')` \\u2014 subagents get isolated context, read-only, budget-capped\\n2. **Phase 3 (Execute)**: Requires interactive Claude Code sessions with full permissions \\u2014 NOT suited for autonomous cloud execution without user oversight\\n\\n## Adversarial Review Requirements\\n\\nPer [AI Review Routing Policy](docs/standards/AI_REVIEW_ROUTING_POLICY.md):\\n\\n- **3-agent adversarial review at BOTH stages** (plan + code/artifact)\\n- Claude: orchestrator + reviewer\\n- Codex: implementation worker + adversarial reviewer\\n- Gemini: architecture reviewer + adversarial reviewer\\n- Max 3 review iterations per deliverable\\n- All MAJOR findings must be resolved before completion\\n\\n### Review Checkpoints\\n1. **After Phase 1 (Audit)**: All 3 agents review the audit findings\\n2. **After Phase 2 (Plan)**: All 3 agents review each repo's refactor plan\\n3. **After Phase 3 (Execute)**: All 3 agents review code changes per repo\\n\\n## Related Issues\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1567 \\u2014 Feature: Continuous Repo Architecture Intelligence\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1545 \\u2014 Agentic feature progression for repo ecosystem productivity under GSD\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1603 \\u2014 Multi-repo architecture scan \\u2014 expand scanner to all tier-1 repos\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 \\u2014 Cross-agent session corpus audit (134 duplicate skills identified)\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1734 \\u2014 chore: add domain skills to assethold (tier-1 with 0 skills)\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1661 \\u2014 Architecture scanner: dependency cycle detection and layering violations\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#113 \\u2014 ROADMAP: Repo ecosystem 3-6 month horizon\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] Audit report generated for each tier-1 repo\\n- [ ] Refactor plan per repo reviewed and approved by all 3 agents\\n- [ ] Phase 3 sub-issues created with clear scope per repo\\n- [ ] Test coverage meets tier-1 target (85%) after refactoring\\n- [ ] Zero cross-repo duplicate skills remaining\\n- [ ] All repos score 100% on Control-Plane Contract compliance\\n- [ ] Dependency graph documented and validated\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962\", \"display_title\": \"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring \\u2014 audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2656\", \"title\": \"chore(repo-structure): normalize workspace-hub folder/file structure\", \"issue_number\": 2656, \"body\": \"## Objective\\n\\nPlan a conservative, repo-by-repo folder/file structure normalization for `workspace-hub` under the workspace-hub tier-1 ecosystem workflow.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nPlanning only until explicit user approval. The plan must inventory current structure, identify root clutter/generated-output risk, propose a bounded Phase 1 refactor contract, define TDD/verification gates, and preserve evidence needed for rollback.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n\\n- Canonical plan artifact under `docs/plans/`.\\n- Review artifact under `scripts/review/results/`.\\n- Final issue comment linking the plan and review artifact.\\n\\n## Approval Gate\\n\\nNo file moves, package reorganization, deletion, or cleanup execution is authorized until this issue is explicitly approved after plan review.\\n\\nParent ecosystem anchors: `workspace-hub#1962`, `workspace-hub#2397`.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2656\", \"display_title\": \"chore(repo-structure): normalize workspace-hub folder/file structure\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2653\", \"title\": \"WRK-694: Per-session log files in session-logger.sh\", \"issue_number\": 2653, \"body\": \"## WRK-694: Per-session log files in session-logger.sh\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** Stage 18: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** pipeline\\n\\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** simple | **Machine:** dev-primary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### What\\n\\nModify `.claude/hooks/session-logger.sh` so each Claude session gets its own\\nlog file instead of merging all activity for the day into one file.\\n\\n### Why\\n\\nWhen multiple sessions run on the same day (or across a long session), the\\ncurrent `session_YYYYMMDD.jsonl` file merges all tool calls into one file.\\nPer-session files make it easier to correlate log entries with a specific\\nconversation, debug session issues, and feed individual sessions into the RAG\\npipeline cleanly.\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\n- [ ] New session \\u2192 new log file with `session_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS` name\\n- [ ] Resumed session (within 2h) \\u2192 same log file continues\\n- [ ] Session idle >2h \\u2192 next tool call starts a new log file\\n- [ ] Both log paths (state/sessions/ and logs/orchestrator/claude/) use the session ID\\n- [ ] State file is gitignored (already under `.claude/state/` which is gitignored)\\n- [ ] Hook remains <1s execution time (raw-write only principle)\\n- [ ] Existing date-based fans in analyze-sessions.sh or similar still work\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\n- [x] **1. Capture** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **3. Triage** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **13. Agent Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **14. Verify Gate Evidence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **15. Future Work Synthesis** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **16. Resource Intelligence Update** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **17. User Review - Implementation** \\u2014 done\\n- [ ] **18. Reclaim** \\u2014 n\\n- [ ] **19. Close** \\u2014 pending\\n- [ ] **20. Archive** \\u2014 pending\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2653\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-694: Per-session log files in session-logger.sh\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2641\", \"title\": \"feat(solver-queue): hands-off multi-machine inbox ingestion for OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA\", \"issue_number\": 2641, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd a hands-off multi-machine solver batch pipeline so a user can drop manifests/input files into a known inbox, have Linux hosts ingest/queue them, and have Windows licensed hosts run OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA/ANSYS jobs via periodic polling without manually touching the machines.\\n\\n## Current evidence\\n- Existing solver queue exists: `queue/pending/` -> `queue/completed/` or `queue/failed/`, documented in `docs/solver/README.md` and `queue/job-schema.yaml`.\\n- Existing submission path supports only `orcawave` and `orcaflex`: `scripts/solver/submit-job.sh`, `scripts/solver/submit-batch.sh`, `scripts/solver/validate_manifest.py`.\\n- `ace-linux-2` is reachable and has AI/dev/open-source simulation tooling, but does **not** have OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, AQWA/ANSYS, MATLAB, or Python `OrcFxAPI`; it should coordinate/ingest/preprocess, not execute licensed solver jobs.\\n- `ace-linux-2` NFS access to the ace-linux-1 knowledge/data center is via `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace`, not direct `/mnt/ace`.\\n- `ace-linux-2` cron is drifted and points at stale `/mnt/workspace-hub` paths while the observed workspace is `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`.\\n- `licensed-win-1` / `licensed-win-2` are registry-designated OrcaFlex/ANSYS hosts, but no SSH access is configured; Windows Task Scheduler/Git Bash is the current execution route.\\n\\n## Required design\\n1. Define canonical inbox/drop locations and file contract:\\n   - repo inbox: `queue/inbox/` for Git-backed manifests\\n   - optional shared-store inbox under `/mnt/ace/...` on ace-linux-1 and `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace/...` on ace-linux-2\\n   - Windows-local workspace path mapping for `D:\\\\workspace-hub`\\n2. Add a cron-safe ingestion wrapper that scans the inbox, validates manifests, atomically stages accepted jobs into `queue/pending/`, moves invalid submissions to quarantine, logs every action, and commits/pushes only accepted queue changes.\\n3. Extend schema/validation/submission for `aqwa` or `ansys-aqwa` jobs with explicit input/output patterns and batch command contract.\\n4. Extend Windows queue processor dispatch to route jobs by solver and host capability:\\n   - OrcaWave/OrcaFlex on licensed Orcina host\\n   - AQWA/ANSYS on ANSYS host\\n   - no licensed solver execution on ace-linux-2\\n5. Add scheduled-task definitions in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` and Windows Task Scheduler installation notes/scripts for licensed hosts.\\n6. Repair or supersede ace-linux-2 cron path drift using canonical schedule rendering.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- [ ] A manifest/input dropped into the canonical inbox is ingested without manual machine access.\\n- [ ] Valid OrcaWave/OrcaFlex jobs appear in `queue/pending/` and are picked up by licensed Windows queue processing.\\n- [ ] AQWA/ANSYS jobs validate through schema and route to the ANSYS-capable host instead of being rejected as unknown solver types.\\n- [ ] Invalid submissions are quarantined with actionable error logs, not silently ignored.\\n- [ ] `ace-linux-2` scheduled tasks use `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub` or a verified symlink/path contract, not stale `/mnt/workspace-hub` references.\\n- [ ] Tests cover manifest validation, inbox ingestion, atomic moves/quarantine, and solver dispatch selection.\\n- [ ] Documentation explains roles: ace-linux-1 control plane, ace-linux-2 ingestion/preprocess/overflow worker, licensed Windows hosts solver execution.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Parent/related: #1586 solver queue hardening\\n- Related: #2170 Windows licensed tool probe adapters\\n- Related: #1564 ace-linux-2 crontab sync\\n- Related: #1119 multi-machine work distribution\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2641\", \"display_title\": \"feat(solver-queue): hands-off multi-machine inbox ingestion for OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2640\", \"title\": \"feat(gtm): worldenergydata production decline forecast \\u2014 shareable Jupyter notebook for GTM demo\", \"issue_number\": 2640, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nIssue [#117](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117) acceptance criteria includes:\\n> Build shareable Jupyter notebook: production decline forecast (worldenergydata)\\n\\nThe `worldenergydata` library now has a production-ready `ArpsDeclineCurve` module at `worldenergydata.production.forecast.decline`. The GTM candidate review (2026-05-04) confirmed it works end-to-end:\\n- Hyperbolic, exponential, harmonic curve fitting via scipy\\n- EUR (Estimated Ultimate Recovery) calculation\\n- 240-month forecast with confidence intervals\\n- Plotly interactive visualization\\n\\nA 63 KB FDAS GTM HTML report was already generated (`reports/gtm/2026-05-04-fdas-field-development-economics.html`), which includes an Arps decline demo with synthetic data.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nCreate a standalone Jupyter notebook at `worldenergydata/notebooks/gtm_production_decline_forecast.ipynb`:\\n\\n1. **Section 1: Module overview** \\u2014 what Arps decline is, three model types, typical GoM/North Sea use cases\\n2. **Section 2: Synthetic demo** \\u2014 fit a synthetic GoM well (qi=5000 bbl/month, Di=0.08/month, b=0.6), show EUR=146 Mbbls\\n3. **Section 3: Multi-well portfolio** \\u2014 fit 3 hypothetical wells (early, mid, late decline stages), side-by-side comparison\\n4. **Section 4: Sensitivity** \\u2014 how EUR changes with b (hyperbolic exponent) from 0.2 to 1.0\\n5. **Section 5: Connect to FDAS economics** \\u2014 feed EUR directly into `EconomicsQuery.npv()` to compute field-level NPV from decline curve\\n\\nAll sections must run without external data (synthetic data generated in-notebook).\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Notebook executes end-to-end with `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute` in <60 seconds\\n- [ ] All Plotly charts render (interactive HTML output cells)\\n- [ ] A companion `reports/gtm/gtm_production_decline_forecast.html` is exported from the notebook\\n- [ ] Notebook added to `worldenergydata/notebooks/README.md` index\\n- [ ] No BSEE binary data required to run\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Parent: [#117](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117)\\n- Implemented ArpsDeclineCurve: `worldenergydata/src/worldenergydata/production/forecast/decline.py`\\n- FDAS GTM report with Arps demo: `worldenergydata/reports/gtm/2026-05-04-fdas-field-development-economics.html`\\n- Generation script reference: `worldenergydata/scripts/gtm/generate_fdas_gtm_report.py`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2640\", \"display_title\": \"feat(gtm): worldenergydata production decline forecast \\u2014 shareable Jupyter notebook for GTM demo\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2628\", \"title\": \"epic(digitalmodel-ci): domain-divided CI architecture replacing maxfail-masking pattern\", \"issue_number\": 2628, \"body\": \"## Why now\\n\\nThis session surfaced that digitalmodel's QG suite has been chronically green-washed by \\\\`maxfail=20\\\\` masking 200+ pre-existing failures across 53 test files. The admin-merge-via-Quality-Gates-failure pattern (PRs [#541](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/541), [#544](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/544), [#549](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/549), [#550](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/550), [#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551), [#553](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/553), [#568](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/568), [#569](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/569)) is now the default merge path \\u2014 operationally fast but strategically corrosive: every PR carries the same 'gate trips on unrelated debt' justification.\\n\\nRoot cause: a single repo-wide \\\\`pytest --maxfail=20\\\\` invocation can't distinguish 'this PR introduced a regression in its own scope' from 'unrelated debt elsewhere in the repo.' Per-domain CI fixes both.\\n\\n## Architecture\\n\\n**14-15 test domains** (per [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) sweep + tests/ tree):\\n\\n\\\\`asset_integrity\\\\`, \\\\`citations\\\\`, \\\\`contracts\\\\`, \\\\`field_development\\\\`, \\\\`hydrodynamics-diffraction\\\\`, \\\\`hydrodynamics-passing-ship\\\\`, \\\\`infrastructure-core\\\\`, \\\\`marine-engineering\\\\`, \\\\`naval-architecture\\\\`, \\\\`orcaflex\\\\`, \\\\`orcaflex-solver\\\\`, \\\\`solver-smoke\\\\`, \\\\`specialized\\\\`, \\\\`structural\\\\`, \\\\`workflows\\\\`, \\\\`misc\\\\`\\n\\nEach domain becomes:\\n- a separate pytest job in a GH Actions matrix\\n- with its own \\\\`--with\\\\` dep list (so e.g. redis/psycopg2/motor only attach to \\\\`infrastructure-core\\\\`, not the whole CI)\\n- with its own conftest fixtures and skipif policies\\n- with the existing \\\\`maxfail\\\\` removed (per-domain failure counts stay bounded by domain size)\\n\\n## Decisions locked (this session, vamseeachanta)\\n\\n| Decision | Choice |\\n|---|---|\\n| Domain owner | All domains owned by \\\\`vamseeachanta\\\\` (single owner; CODEOWNERS encodes this) |\\n| Aggregator gate policy | **All domains must pass** for merge (no informational-only mode) |\\n| Run cadence | **Touched-domains-only on PRs**; **full matrix nightly** (cron) |\\n| Deprecation policy | No test deletion or blanket-skip without AI-attested 'source X is gone, no other consumers' commit per \\\\`feedback_attestation_enables_contradiction_detection\\\\` |\\n\\n## Sub-tasks\\n\\n- [ ] **A** \\u2014 Domain inventory PR (\\\\`tests/DOMAINS.md\\\\` mapping every test directory \\u2192 domain \\u2192 dep set; informs all subsequent PRs). Read-only repo analysis. \\\\`digitalmodel\\\\`.\\n- [ ] **B** \\u2014 \\\\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml\\\\` refactor: one gate per domain with its own command + deps. Aggregator gate depends on all. Drops repo-wide \\\\`maxfail\\\\`. \\\\`digitalmodel\\\\`.\\n- [ ] **C** \\u2014 GH Actions matrix workflow: \\\\`.github/workflows/quality-gates-by-domain.yml\\\\` with \\\\`strategy.matrix.domain\\\\` for parallel runs + aggregator job. \\\\`digitalmodel\\\\`.\\n- [ ] **D** \\u2014 Touched-domain detection: PR-time job that diffs changed paths against \\\\`tests/DOMAINS.md\\\\` and emits the matrix subset to run. Nightly cron uses full matrix. \\\\`digitalmodel\\\\`.\\n- [ ] **E** \\u2014 Per-domain conftest extraction: move shared fixtures/skipifs from \\\\`tests/conftest.py\\\\` into each domain's local conftest. ~1 PR per domain (small each). \\\\`digitalmodel\\\\`.\\n- [ ] **F** \\u2014 CODEOWNERS file with \\\\`vamseeachanta\\\\` as default owner. \\\\`digitalmodel\\\\`.\\n- [ ] **G** \\u2014 Update workspace-hub agents.md + CLAUDE.md to encode the 'always test by domain during development' rule for AI agents. \\\\`workspace-hub\\\\`.\\n\\n## After A-G land, the deferred cluster work re-routes by domain\\n\\n| Cluster | Issue | New domain home |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| A \\u2014 sys.modules pollution | [#2623](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623) | \\\\`contracts\\\\` domain |\\n| D \\u2014 infrastructure dep wall | [#2624](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624) | \\\\`infrastructure-core\\\\` domain \\u2014 its own \\\\`--with redis --with psycopg2-binary --with motor\\\\` |\\n| E \\u2014 marine-engineering regressions | [#2625](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625) | \\\\`marine-engineering\\\\` domain \\u2014 its own targeted suite |\\n\\nThese clusters become much cheaper to fix per-domain than under repo-wide CI.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Domain inventory exists and is reviewable\\n- [ ] PR for trivial change (e.g. docs typo) only runs domains that own changed paths; runs in <2 min\\n- [ ] PR that touches multiple domains runs all touched domains; aggregator gates correctly\\n- [ ] Nightly full matrix runs all domains; failure surfaces per-domain in GH Actions UI\\n- [ ] No test files deleted as part of this work; debt that surfaces is filed per-domain for owner triage\\n- [ ] Existing admin-merge cycle ends \\u2014 next post-A-G PR merges through the new gate without override\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) \\u2014 investigations that surfaced the masking\\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) \\u2014 issue-state drift / quality-gates audit umbrella\\n- [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) \\u2014 PR that introduced \\\\`maxfail=20\\\\`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2628\", \"display_title\": \"epic(digitalmodel-ci): domain-divided CI architecture replacing maxfail-masking pattern\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2510\", \"title\": \"feat(cad): build Python layout/CAD automation demo for chip/package geometries\", \"issue_number\": 2510, \"body\": \"## Context\\nParent: #2507\\nRoadmap: `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\\n\\nBuild a Python-first geometry/layout automation example that bridges CAD practices into semiconductor layout and package/interposer-style structures.\\n\\n## Proposed scope\\n- Use KLayout scripting and/or GDSFactory to generate a parameterized layout artifact.\\n- Export/import GDS/OASIS and at least one human-inspectable representation such as SVG/DXF where practical.\\n- Extract metadata: layers, polygons, bounding boxes, ports/nets where available, geometry invariants.\\n- Include tests around geometry invariants and repeatability.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n1. Python module/script for parameterized layout generation.\\n2. Sample output artifacts or reproducible generation command.\\n3. Metadata extraction report.\\n4. Tests and documentation.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- Demo can be run locally without proprietary tools.\\n- Generated artifacts are deterministic enough for tests or snapshot checks.\\n- Report explains relevance to chip design, packaging, and CAD automation roles.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2510\", \"display_title\": \"feat(cad): build Python layout/CAD automation demo for chip/package geometries\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625\", \"title\": \"investigate(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster E \\u2014 marine-engineering domain regressions (~60 failures across catenary/wave/ocimf/RAO)\", \"issue_number\": 2625, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). ~60 failures concentrated in \\\\`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/\\\\` and adjacent. Without per-test detail (the maxfail=200 probe used \\\\`--tb=line\\\\` only), root causes can't be characterized inline.\\n\\n## Affected (by file, partial \\u2014 full list at \\\\`/tmp/qg-failures-clean.txt\\\\`)\\n\\n| Count | File |\\n|---:|---|\\n| 16 | \\\\`test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py\\\\` |\\n| 11 | \\\\`test_mooring_catenary.py\\\\` |\\n| 11 | \\\\`legacy/test_mooring_catenary.py\\\\` (mirror of above) |\\n| 5 | \\\\`test_unified_rao_reader.py\\\\` |\\n| 4 | \\\\`test_catenary_adapter.py\\\\` |\\n| 4 | \\\\`legacy/test_component_database.py\\\\` |\\n| 3 | \\\\`test_marine_eng_perf.py\\\\` |\\n| 3 | \\\\`integration/test_hydro_rao_integration.py\\\\` |\\n| 3 | \\\\`catenary/test_simplified.py\\\\` |\\n| ~10 | scattered across catenary/wave/ocimf integration files |\\n\\n## Recommended next step\\n\\nRun a targeted \\\\`--tb=short\\\\` re-run on \\\\`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/\\\\` to characterize root causes. Likely sub-clusters:\\n\\n- Domain code regressions (real defects in catenary solver, RAO reader, etc.)\\n- Missing data files (test fixtures gone)\\n- Knock-on effects of test pollution (could be related to Cluster A \\u2192 [#2623](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623))\\n\\nAfter per-cluster characterization, file individual sub-trackers and link back to this umbrella.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\nThis issue is the umbrella; close it once sub-trackers are filed and assigned.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625\", \"display_title\": \"investigate(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster E \\u2014 marine-engineering domain regressions (~60 failures across catenary/wave/ocimf/RAO)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624\", \"title\": \"policy(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster D \\u2014 decide whether redis/psycopg2/motor belong in QG suite (51 errors)\", \"issue_number\": 2624, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). 51 errors in \\\\`tests/infrastructure/core/\\\\` are environmental \\u2014 pure dep-wall, not feature defects.\\n\\n## Affected\\n\\n- \\\\`tests/infrastructure/core/test_cache.py\\\\` (32 errors) \\u2014 requires \\\\`redis\\\\` backend\\n- \\\\`tests/infrastructure/core/test_database_manager.py\\\\` (19 errors) \\u2014 logs \\\\`No module named 'psycopg2'\\\\` and references \\\\`motor\\\\` (async MongoDB)\\n\\nThese would also fail in the original CI run with the same env. Whether they were ever expected to run in the QG suite is a policy question.\\n\\n## Decision needed\\n\\nPick one:\\n\\n- **(a) Add deps to QG environment**: \\\\`--with redis --with psycopg2-binary --with motor\\\\` to the QG command. Keeps tests in the QG tier. Adds CI install time. Requires actual Redis/Postgres/Mongo containers in CI for tests to pass (not just import). Likely heaviest infrastructure lift.\\n- **(b) Mark with import-probe skipif**: add \\\\`@pytest.mark.skipif(not _has_redis, reason=\\\\\\\"redis backend unavailable\\\\\\\")\\\\` per-class in those test files. Cheap fix; tests skip cleanly when the backend isn't available; tests still run on machines that have Redis/Postgres/Mongo.\\n- **(c) Move to a separate \\\\\\\"integration\\\\\\\" tier outside QG**: e.g., a separate pytest mark or a dedicated tox env that QG doesn't invoke. Cleanest separation; requires a tier-routing decision.\\n\\nRecommendation: (b) for fastest unblock; (c) for long-term cleanliness.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] 51 errors in \\\\`tests/infrastructure/core/\\\\` either pass (with backends installed) or skip cleanly (no errors)\\n- [ ] QG command produces 0 of these errors\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)\\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) \\u2014 quality-gates audit umbrella\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624\", \"display_title\": \"policy(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster D \\u2014 decide whether redis/psycopg2/motor belong in QG suite (51 errors)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623\", \"title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster A \\u2014 sys.modules pollution leaking MagicMock into tests/contracts/ (2 failures)\", \"issue_number\": 2623, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). Two tests in \\\\`tests/contracts/\\\\` fail in the full suite but pass in isolation \\u2014 classic test-pollution Heisenbug, made deterministic by \\\\`-p no:randomly\\\\`.\\n\\n## Defect\\n\\n- \\\\`tests/contracts/test_assetutilities_common_contract.py::test_update_deep_dictionary_importable\\\\` \\u2192 \\\\`AssertionError: <MagicMock> == 1\\\\`\\n- \\\\`tests/contracts/test_assetutilities_data_contract.py::test_AttributeDict_importable\\\\` \\u2192 \\\\`AssertionError: <MagicMock> == 'value'\\\\`\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\nSome upstream test in the suite registers \\\\`assetutilities.common.update_deep\\\\` and \\\\`assetutilities.common.data\\\\` (or similar \\\\`assetutilities.*\\\\` modules) as \\\\`MagicMock()\\\\` in \\\\`sys.modules\\\\` without restoring them. With \\\\`-p no:randomly\\\\` the ordering is deterministic, so the pollution is reproducible. When the contract tests later import the real symbols they receive the mock.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n\\\\`pytest tests/contracts/\\\\` in isolation \\u2192 **17/17 pass**. Sibling repo \\\\`../assetutilities/src/assetutilities/common/update_deep.py\\\\` carries both \\\\`update_deep_dictionary\\\\` and \\\\`AttributeDict\\\\` correctly.\\n\\n## Fix options\\n\\n- **(a) Hunt the polluter** \\u2014 search candidates: tests that \\\\`import unittest.mock; sys.modules[...] = MagicMock()\\\\` or \\\\`@patch.dict('sys.modules', ...)\\\\` without proper teardown. Similar to the existing \\\\`tests/conftest.py:92\\\\` pattern for \\\\`digitalmodel.subsea.catenary_riser.legacy.catenary_riser_summary\\\\`. Then add proper teardown to that test.\\n- **(b) Defensive autouse fixture** in \\\\`tests/contracts/conftest.py\\\\` that purges polluted entries before each test:\\n  \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`python\\n  for k in list(sys.modules):\\n      if k.startswith('assetutilities') and isinstance(sys.modules[k], MagicMock):\\n          del sys.modules[k]\\n  \\\\`\\\\`\\\\`\\n\\nRecommend (a) for the right fix; (b) as a safety net if (a) requires more bisection time than is available.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Both contract tests pass in the full suite under \\\\`-p no:randomly\\\\`\\n- [ ] If (a): polluter identified and fixed; \\\\`tests/contracts/\\\\` no longer needs special handling\\n- [ ] If (b): conftest fixture documented with the polluter's path so it can be revisited\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623\", \"display_title\": \"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster A \\u2014 sys.modules pollution leaking MagicMock into tests/contracts/ (2 failures)\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112\", \"title\": \"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking\", \"issue_number\": 2112, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nBackfill the minimum SubseaIQ equipment-count fields required to unblock #2055 subsea cost benchmarking.\\n\\n## Context\\nAssessment of #2055 found two active blockers:\\n1. `status:needs-data`\\n2. the required equipment-count fields are absent from every current GoM record in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\\n\\nCurrent state from assessment:\\n- required threshold: >=10 GoM fields with equipment counts\\n- current state: 0/10 records have `num_trees`, `num_manifolds`, or `tieback_distance_km`\\n\\nWithout this backfill, the cost benchmarking implementation issue cannot proceed honestly.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Backfill or derive, for at least 10 GoM fields, the following fields in the SubseaIQ-derived dataset:\\n  - `num_trees`\\n  - `num_manifolds`\\n  - `tieback_distance_km`\\n- Document provenance / confidence for each backfilled field\\n- Update the dataset and any ingestion/normalization helpers needed to preserve the fields\\n- Add a minimal validation check proving the threshold is satisfied\\n\\n## Target data\\n- `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\\n- any normalization or ingestion surfaces that write/read these fields\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- At least 10 GoM records contain non-null equipment counts and tieback distance\\n- Provenance/confidence is documented\\n- A validation command or test confirms the threshold is met\\n- #2055 can remove `status:needs-data` after verification\\n\\n## Relationship\\n- Direct prerequisite for #2055\\n- Should be completed before any cost-per-tree / cost-per-manifold implementation work\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112\", \"display_title\": \"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2558\", \"title\": \"data(field-dev): primary-source GoM equipment/cost source pack for #2112/#2055\", \"issue_number\": 2558, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nCreate the next #2112 source-quality pass: a primary/public-source evidence pack for #2055's GoM field-development cost benchmarking gate.\\n\\nThis is a follow-up to the provisional artifact committed in `624c87f12`, which intentionally kept #2055 blocked because the candidate dataset failed source/semantic review.\\n\\n## Parent / related\\n- Parent blocker: #2112\\n- Downstream benchmark feature: #2055\\n- Related field-development roadmap: #1842, #1861\\n- Current provisional report: `docs/reports/field-development-unblock-2112.md`\\n- Current provisional dataset: `data/field-development/gom-field-development-unblock-2112.json`\\n\\n## Problem\\nThe provisional dataset is useful as a source-lead list but is not correlation-ready. The adversarial review found that:\\n\\n- `num_manifolds` was sometimes proxy-coded from drill centers/risers/field centers.\\n- `tieback_distance_km` mixed incompatible definitions.\\n- `cost_usd_bn` mixed public project costs with estimates/seed values.\\n- Some facts came from snippets or weak secondary references.\\n\\n## Scope\\nBuild a citable source pack and normalize definitions before any correlation/model implementation.\\n\\nRequired source-backed fields per accepted record:\\n\\n| Field | Required interpretation |\\n|---|---|\\n| `num_trees` | actual subsea/wet tree count, or clearly sourced well/tree count |\\n| `num_manifolds` | actual subsea manifold/PLEM/PLET count only; no drill-center proxy |\\n| `tieback_distance_km` | one explicitly named definition, preferably subsea field/well/cluster to host/receiving facility |\\n| `cost_usd_bn` | project/phase cost with year/currency/scope; no generic cost-class seed |\\n| `source_notes` | durable public URL(s), source type, quote/paraphrase, confidence |\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- A source evidence table/report under `docs/reports/` or `.planning/intel/`.\\n- Updated dataset or companion JSON that distinguishes accepted records from rejected/needs-source records.\\n- A validator that fails if accepted records lack source/definition evidence.\\n- Clear recommendation on whether #2112 can close and whether #2055 can drop `status:needs-data`.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- At least 10 GoM records pass the public-source/no-invented-values semantic gate, **or** the report explicitly says fewer than 10 pass and keeps #2055 blocked.\\n- Every accepted numeric field has a durable public citation and consistent definition.\\n- No snippet-only, proxy-coded, or seeded numeric values are treated as accepted facts.\\n- #2055 remains `status:needs-data` unless the validator confirms the threshold.\\n\\n## Execution constraints\\n- Plan first per repo policy; do not execute until approved.\\n- Use TDD/validator-first for the accepted-record gate.\\n- Prefer null + `needs_source` over estimates.\\n- Do not implement cost correlations until the source pack passes.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2558\", \"display_title\": \"data(field-dev): primary-source GoM equipment/cost source pack for #2112/#2055\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055\", \"title\": \"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts\", \"issue_number\": 2055, \"body\": \"## Summary\\n\\nBuild cost-per-equipment-unit benchmark curves (cost/tree, cost/km-flowline, cost/manifold) by water-depth band, correlating SubseaIQ equipment counts against sanctioned project costs.\\n\\n## Problem\\n\\nThe scaffold (#1861) provides `SubseaProject` with equipment-count fields and `load_projects()`, but zero cost functions exist. No module currently correlates equipment counts with project costs.\\n\\n## Data Prerequisites (must be met before implementation)\\n\\n- [ ] Equipment counts (num_trees, num_manifolds, tieback_distance_km) backfilled for >= 10 GoM fields in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\\n- [ ] Confirmed name-match mapping between SubseaIQ project names and CostDataPoint project names for overlapping records\\n\\n## v1 Scope (band-level aggregation)\\n\\nUse depth-band-level aggregation to compute cost-per-equipment ratios. This avoids per-project name-match fragility and works with sparse data.\\n\\n### New functions in `digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/field_development/benchmarks.py`:\\n- `cost_per_tree(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\\n- `cost_per_km_flowline(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\\n- `cost_per_manifold(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\\n- `unit_cost_curves(projects) -> dict` combining all three metrics\\n- `cost_benchmark_bands(projects) -> dict` with low/base/high by depth band and concept type\\n\\n### New helper in `worldenergydata/subseaiq/analytics/cost_correlation.py`:\\n- `correlate_equipment_costs(subseaiq_records, cost_records)` \\u2014 depth-band-level join\\n- `aggregate_costs_by_depth_band(merged)` \\u2014 band-level cost aggregation\\n\\n## Deferred to v2\\n\\n- Per-project cost-per-tree correlation (requires CostDataPoint schema extension with equipment fields)\\n- Cross-validation of equipment-derived costs against individual sanctioned project costs\\n- Full 71-record equipment-count backfill\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- [ ] Equipment counts populated for >= 10 GoM fields\\n- [ ] `cost_per_tree` returns values in $10M-$200M range per depth band\\n- [ ] `cost_per_km_flowline` returns values in $5M-$50M range per depth band\\n- [ ] `cost_per_manifold` returns physically reasonable values per depth band\\n- [ ] `cost_benchmark_bands` returns nested dict with low/base/high structure\\n- [ ] All 27 existing `test_benchmarks.py` tests pass unchanged\\n- [ ] New cost-related tests added and passing\\n- [ ] `benchmarks` exported from `digitalmodel.field_development.__init__`\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n- #1861 (scaffold) \\u2014 DONE\\n- Equipment-count data backfill \\u2014 REQUIRED before implementation\\n\\n## Labels\\n\\n`enhancement`, `priority:high`, `cat:engineering`, `dark-intelligence`, `agent:claude`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055\", \"display_title\": \"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/18\", \"title\": \"WRK-023: Property GIS development timeline with future projection and Google Earth animation\", \"issue_number\": 18, \"body\": \"## WRK-023: Property GIS development timeline with future projection and Google Earth animation\\n\\n\\n\\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** low | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** pipeline\\n\\n**Repo:** ['assethold'] | **Complexity:** complex | **Machine:** dev-primary\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n### What\\n\\nFor a given property, build a GIS-based timeline showing how the surrounding area has developed over time and project how future development may evolve. Leverage historical satellite imagery (Google Earth historical imagery, Landsat, Sentinel), permit/zoning data, and development patterns to create:\\n1. **Historical timeline**: visual progression of development around the property over years/decades\\n2. **Future projection**: anticipated development based on zoning plans, permit filings, growth patterns\\n3. **Animation**: Google Earth-style animated flythrough or timelapse showing development progression\\n\\n### Why\\n\\nUnderstanding how an area has developed \\u2014 and where it's heading \\u2014 is critical for property investment decisions. A visual GIS timeline makes development patterns immediately apparent and supports investment thesis with spatial evidence rather than just market comparables.\\n\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n\\n\\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\\n\\n- [ ] Historical satellite/aerial imagery retrieval for a property location across multiple time periods\\n- [x] Development change detection: identify new construction, road expansion, land use changes\\n- [x] Permit and zoning data integration where available (local government open data)\\n- [x] Timeline visualization: side-by-side or slider comparison of historical imagery\\n- [x] Future development projection based on:\\n- [x] Google Earth animation / KML timelapse export\\n- [ ] Blender 3D visualization option for development progression\\n- [x] Report output with timeline narrative and key development milestones\\n- [x] Document data sources, projection methodology, and confidence levels\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\\n\\nNot yet available\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\\n\\n- [x] **1. Capture** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **3. Triage** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** \\u2014 done\\n- [x] **16. User Review** \\u2014 done\\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** \\u2014 n\\n- [ ] **18. Close** \\u2014 pending\\n- [ ] **19. Archive** \\u2014 pending\\n\\n</details>\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/18\", \"display_title\": \"WRK-023: Property GIS development timeline with future projection and Google Earth animation\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327\", \"title\": \"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation\", \"issue_number\": 2327, \"body\": \"## Context\\n\\nCadQuery is a Python library for parametric 3D CAD on the OpenCASCADE B-rep kernel. STEP export is high-fidelity, so downstream meshers (gmsh, Salome) can control density without being trapped by authoring-time facets \\u2014 a good shape for offshore component geometry that feeds AQWA.\\n\\nDiscovered via HN discussion 2026-04-17 (180 points, 47 comments): https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\\n\\n> **Revised 2026-04-17 per [adversarial review](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327#issuecomment-4269065194)**: time-boxed; scope narrowed to one family and one pipeline; go/no-go criteria quantified; LLM-codegen question dropped (covered by #2328); current-state baseline required in plan.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n**Time-box:** 4 hours of hands-on work. Stop and report at the cap regardless of completeness.\\n\\n**Target pipeline (committed):** STEP (CadQuery) \\u2192 gmsh \\u2192 AQWA `QPPL DIFF` panel diffraction. Chosen because panel diffraction has the tightest mesh-quality requirements (hardest pipeline test); digitalmodel already consumes AQWA downstream; OrcaFlex line-element path is a trivial follow-up if this works.\\n\\n**Target family (committed):** H-link mooring shackle, 4 variants (MBL 45 t, 65 t, 85 t, 125 t). Chosen because mooring hardware is a recurring workspace-hub domain (#2070 cost-tracking, mooring-failures knowledge seed) and 4 variants exercises the \\\"generate N from parameters\\\" claim without inflating scope.\\n\\n## Current State (document in the plan's Resource Intel section)\\n\\n- Current `digitalmodel/` geometry pipeline \\u2014 tools, inputs, outputs\\n- Where STEP files come from today (if at all)\\n- Which solver consumers exist and their geometry-input formats\\n\\n## Go / No-Go Acceptance Criteria\\n\\nAll three must hold for **go**:\\n\\n1. **Time-to-first-STEP < 30 min** for a Python-comfortable engineer working from CadQuery docs alone (no pre-existing exemplars)\\n2. **Regenerating all 4 H-link variants < 60 s** of compute total\\n3. **STEP \\u2192 gmsh \\u2192 AQWA `QPPL DIFF`** runs with no fatal mesh errors on first attempt for at least **2 of 4** variants\\n\\n## Deliverable\\n\\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/h-link-family/` \\u2014 1 parametric script, 4 STEP outputs, 4 meshes, 1 successful AQWA run log\\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/README.md` \\u2014 timings, mesh quality notes, explicit verdict against the 3 criteria above\\n\\n## Planning Workflow (per CLAUDE.md)\\n\\nPlan file: `docs/plans/2026-MM-DD-issue-2327-cadquery-digitalmodel-spike.md` using `docs/plans/_template-issue-plan.md`. Resource-intel section must include the current-state documentation above. No implementation until `status:plan-approved`.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n- Wiki entity: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\\n- Source summary: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/2026-04-17-hn-cadquery.md`\\n- Marine cross-page: `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\\n- Sibling issues: #2328 (tool comparison \\u2014 covers the LLM-codegen question), #2329 (methodology decision \\u2014 blocked by this)\\n- HN thread: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\\n- CadQuery: https://cadquery.github.io/\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327\", \"display_title\": \"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2028\", \"title\": \"feat: review gate strict mode + GitHub Actions CI enforcement workflow\", \"issue_number\": 2028, \"body\": \"## Enable review gate strict mode and add GitHub Actions CI enforcement\\n\\n### Current State\\n- require-review-on-push.sh exists but REVIEW_GATE_STRICT defaults to 0 (advisory)\\n- Pre-push hook calls review gate but only warns\\n- No GitHub Actions workflow for PR-level enforcement\\n- Compliance at 20% (improving from 4%)\\n\\n### What Needs to Happen\\n\\n**Phase 1: Enable Local Review Gate Strict Mode**\\nAfter plan gate is working (#2025), escalate:\\n```bash\\n# In .git/hooks/enforcement-env:\\nexport REVIEW_GATE_STRICT=1\\n```\\nThis will block pushes without cross-review evidence.\\n\\n**Phase 2: GitHub Actions CI Workflow**\\nCreate `.github/workflows/enforcement-gate.yml`:\\n```yaml\\nname: Enforcement Gate\\non: [pull_request]\\njobs:\\n  check-plan-approval:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\\n      - name: Check Plan Approval\\n        run: bash scripts/enforcement/require-plan-approval.sh --strict\\n      - name: Check Review Evidence\\n        run: bash scripts/enforcement/require-review-on-push.sh HEAD $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)\\n  compliance-dashboard:\\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\\n    steps:\\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\\n      - name: Run Compliance Check\\n        run: bash scripts/enforcement/compliance-dashboard.sh\\n```\\n\\n**Phase 3: Progressive Rollout**\\n| Week | What | Risk |\\n|------|------|------|\\n| 1 | Plan gate strict (local only) | Low -- blocks obvious violations |\\n| 2 | Review gate strict (local only) | Medium -- may block legitimate work |\\n| 3 | CI workflow (PR level) | Medium -- external visibility |\\n| 4 | Full enforcement (all gates) | Low -- everything tested |\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] REVIEW_GATE_STRICT=1 in enforcement-env\\n- [ ] Push without review evidence is blocked\\n- [ ] GitHub Actions workflow created and passing\\n- [ ] PRs without plan approval are blocked by CI\\n- [ ] Compliance dashboard runs as CI step\\n- [ ] Bypass logging captures all override attempts\\n\\n### Related\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2025 -- Plan gate strict mode and hook wiring\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1876 -- Enforcement infrastructure (parent)\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2017 -- Agent bypass resistance (metrics)\\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2012 -- Review backlog audit\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2028\", \"display_title\": \"feat: review gate strict mode + GitHub Actions CI enforcement workflow\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2474\", \"title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): add OrcaFlex native reverse-parser equivalence proof\", \"issue_number\": 2474, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd stronger OrcaFlex native YAML -> canonical semantic extraction/equivalence proof so OrcaFlex reaches parity with the OrcaWave roundtrip proof added in PR #528.\\n\\n## Why now\\nPR #528 established OrcaWave reverse parser roundtrip coverage for L03 and deterministic forward OrcaFlex YAML assertions for jumpers/risers. The handoff identifies bidirectional OrcaFlex reverse parsing as the next major semantic-proof gap.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Inventory existing OrcaFlex native YAML readers/parsers, if any.\\n- Define the minimum semantic extraction contract for generated native OrcaFlex YAML back to canonical intent.\\n- Add tests for at least the currently covered PLET-to-PLEM, lazy-wave, and steep-wave fixtures if feasible.\\n- Document intentionally ignored native defaults/formatting differences.\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n- Licensed `.sim`/`.dat` binary parsing without OrcFxAPI.\\n- Full solver execution proof; track separately under licensed-machine proof.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Closed first wave: #2455, #2456, #2457\\n- Existing real `.sim` fixture issue: #1652\\n- Handoff: `docs/handoffs/2026-04-23-orcawave-orcaflex-semantic-proof-exit-handoff.md`\\n- digitalmodel PR: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/528\\n\\n## Acceptance target\\nA plan should define parser/extractor ownership, TDD tests, equivalence criteria, and explicit binary-file/licensed-machine boundaries.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2474\", \"display_title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): add OrcaFlex native reverse-parser equivalence proof\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2516\", \"title\": \"feat(digitalmodel): flexible pipe and dynamic riser cross-section mechanics follow-up\", \"issue_number\": 2516, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nPlan a deeper flexible pipe and dynamic riser cross-section mechanics module after the basic cable/umbilical/pipeline schema exists.\\n\\n## Why\\nFlexible pipes/risers have layer interactions, carcass/liner behavior, pressure armour, tensile armour, outer sheath, collapse, bend radius, torsion, fatigue, and annulus monitoring concerns. They should not be squeezed into the first generic cross-section schema without dedicated mechanics and validation.\\n\\n## Proposed scope\\n- Research public standards/source map for API 17J/API 17B, vendor primers, and open literature on unbonded flexible pipe layers.\\n- Define what can be represented as metadata vs what requires mechanics/calculation modules.\\n- Identify OrcaFlex property export needs for flexible risers and dynamic jumpers.\\n- Produce a bounded implementation plan with fixtures and tests.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- Clear boundary between first-pass cross-section schema and flexible-pipe mechanics.\\n- Source-backed taxonomy of layers: carcass/liner, pressure sheath, pressure armour, tensile armour, outer sheath, insulation or anti-wear layers where applicable.\\n- Follow-up implementation plan includes TDD fixtures and validation paths for bend radius, collapse/pressure metadata, and section-property export.\\n- No implementation starts without plan review and user approval.\\n\\n## Related knowledge\\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/subsea-cable-umbilical-cross-sections.md`\\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/comparisons/offshore-wind-oil-gas-cross-section-assessment.md`\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2516\", \"display_title\": \"feat(digitalmodel): flexible pipe and dynamic riser cross-section mechanics follow-up\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2513\", \"title\": \"feat(knowledge): catalogue offshore wind and oil gas subsea cross-section sources\", \"issue_number\": 2513, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nBuild a source-backed catalogue for offshore wind and oil and gas subsea cross-section families: inter-array/export cables, electro-hydraulic umbilicals, power/optical umbilicals, rigid pipelines/flowlines, and flexible pipe/riser follow-ups.\\n\\n## Why\\nThe new marine-engineering wiki synthesis shows that cross-section data is scattered across offshore wind guides, vendor PDFs, umbilical training material, and pipeline/coating standards. We need a durable catalogue before implementing reusable digitalmodel geometry/section-property tools.\\n\\n## Seed knowledge already created\\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/subsea-cable-umbilical-cross-sections.md`\\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/sources/offshore-cable-umbilical-cross-section-recon-2026-04-26.md`\\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/comparisons/offshore-wind-oil-gas-cross-section-assessment.md`\\n\\n## Initial source anchors\\n- Guide to a Floating Offshore Wind Farm: array/export cable core and outer pages\\n- Prysmian 66 kV submarine cable systems PDF\\n- SUT Subsea Umbilical Systems PDF\\n- Prysmian Power / Optical Umbilicals PDF\\n- DNV-ST-F101 submarine pipeline systems page\\n- Vallourec line pipe coating solutions page\\n- Octal concrete weight coating page\\n\\n## Proposed deliverables\\n- YAML/Markdown catalogue of source exemplars with provenance, asset family, typical layers/components, and known numeric ranges.\\n- Standards map for API 17E/17J/17B, DNV-ST-F101, DNV-RP-F106/F102, IEC/CIGRE submarine cable references, and offshore wind cable guidance.\\n- Gap list separating open/public sources from paywalled/manual standards.\\n- Update wiki pages with any higher-quality exemplar data found.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- At least 12 source/exemplar entries across offshore wind cables, oil and gas umbilicals, rigid pipelines, and flexible pipes/risers.\\n- Each entry records URL/local path, source type, asset family, key layers/components, usable numeric fields, and licensing/access status.\\n- Duplicate/overlapping source entries are deduplicated and linked to the wiki pages above.\\n- No proprietary or paywalled standard text is copied into repo artifacts.\\n\\n## Planning note\\nThis is an issue-intake artifact only. Per workspace policy, implementation requires a canonical plan, adversarial review, and explicit approval before code/data changes beyond planning.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2513\", \"display_title\": \"feat(knowledge): catalogue offshore wind and oil gas subsea cross-section sources\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2507\", \"title\": \"Feature: semiconductor chip-design CAD/FEM career lane\", \"issue_number\": 2507, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nCreate a focused semiconductor / chip-design CAD + FEM career lane in workspace-hub, using ACE Engineer strengths in CAD automation, FEM, document intelligence, and job-market scanning.\\n\\n## Roadmap\\n- `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\\n\\n## Why now\\nLocal job-market scans already show semiconductor-adjacent roles in ASIC program management, advanced packaging, IC packaging simulation, and semiconductor mechanical engineering. This lane converts existing offshore/CAD/FEM strengths into a credible semiconductor portfolio.\\n\\n## Scope\\n1. Build semiconductor CAD/FEM research and job taxonomy.\\n2. Create an open-source EDA RTL-to-GDS demonstration using OpenLane/OpenROAD and open PDKs.\\n3. Create a Python CAD/layout automation demonstration using KLayout and/or GDSFactory.\\n4. Create a semiconductor packaging thermal/thermo-mechanical FEM benchmark.\\n5. Build a job-application packet mapping artifacts to target roles.\\n\\n## Policy\\nFollow repository gates: issue -> plan -> adversarial review -> user approval -> TDD implementation. This umbrella tracks the lane; child issues should carry implementation details.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2507\", \"display_title\": \"Feature: semiconductor chip-design CAD/FEM career lane\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2509\", \"title\": \"feat(eda): create reproducible OpenLane/OpenROAD RTL-to-GDS demo report\", \"issue_number\": 2509, \"body\": \"## Context\\nParent: #2507\\nRoadmap: `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\\n\\nCreate a small open-source EDA demonstration that proves RTL-to-GDS flow literacy and produces a hiring/client-facing report.\\n\\n## Proposed scope\\n- Select a tiny reference RTL design: counter, FIFO, simple ALU, or equivalent.\\n- Run or document a reproducible OpenLane/OpenROAD flow against an open PDK such as Sky130 or GF180MCU.\\n- Capture and explain timing, area, power, DRC/LVS, DEF/GDS, and log artifacts.\\n- Generate an HTML or markdown report that explains what the artifacts mean.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n1. Container/environment instructions.\\n2. Source design and flow config.\\n3. Generated report artifact and artifact inventory.\\n4. Tests/smoke checks where practical, especially around scripts and report generation.\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n- A fresh checkout can reproduce the demo or clearly replay committed sample artifacts.\\n- The report maps EDA artifacts to semiconductor job keywords.\\n- No proprietary PDK or licensed-tool dependency is required.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2509\", \"display_title\": \"feat(eda): create reproducible OpenLane/OpenROAD RTL-to-GDS demo report\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1572\", \"title\": \"Domain-specific capability roadmaps \\u2014 OrcaWave/OrcaFlex, structural, hydrodynamics, pipeline\", \"issue_number\": 1572, \"body\": \"Child of #1567. Create 4 domain roadmap docs mapping: implemented modules \\u2192 test status \\u2192 open GH issues \\u2192 gaps. (1) docs/roadmaps/orcawave-orcaflex-capability-roadmap.md, (2) structural-capability-roadmap.md, (3) hydrodynamics-capability-roadmap.md, (4) pipeline-capability-roadmap.md. Cross-ref with GH issues. Related: docs/plans/2026-04-01-orcawave-orcaflex-intensive-plan.md\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1572\", \"display_title\": \"Domain-specific capability roadmaps \\u2014 OrcaWave/OrcaFlex, structural, hydrodynamics, pipeline\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2473\", \"title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): prove OrcaWave-to-OrcaFlex hydrodynamic handoff semantics\", \"issue_number\": 2473, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd an end-to-end semantic proof for OrcaWave hydrodynamic outputs flowing into OrcaFlex vessel/hydrodynamic native inputs without losing units, headings, frequencies, phase/sign, body axes, damping, or added-mass provenance.\\n\\n## Why now\\nThe first wave proved selected canonical specs become semantically correct native YAML. The handoff gap is now the critical workflow proof: `OrcaWave output -> RAO/added-mass/damping data -> RAODatabase/bridge -> OrcaFlex vessel input`.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Use existing RAO extractor / database / `rao_data_to_diffraction_results()` / OrcaFlex export surfaces where possible.\\n- Define fixture(s) and provenance fields to assert.\\n- Include checks for frequency/heading preservation, rotational-unit convention, phase/sign convention, damping/added-mass matrix provenance, body-frame metadata, and source-file traceability.\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n- New licensed solver execution unless separately approved under the licensed proof protocol.\\n- Rebuilding the already closed #1592/#1605 pipeline unless resource intelligence shows a true gap.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Closed first wave: #2455, #2456, #2457\\n- Prior closed handoff work: #1592, #1597, #1605, #1765, #1766, #1768\\n- Handoff: `docs/handoffs/2026-04-23-orcawave-orcaflex-semantic-proof-exit-handoff.md`\\n- digitalmodel PR: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/528\\n\\n## Acceptance target\\nA plan should identify exact existing code/test surfaces, avoid duplicating closed work, and specify deterministic TDD tests proving hydrodynamic semantic preservation.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2473\", \"display_title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): prove OrcaWave-to-OrcaFlex hydrodynamic handoff semantics\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2472\", \"title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): validate CALM/SPM buoy OrcaFlex semantic proof\", \"issue_number\": 2472, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nAdd a canonical `spec.yml -> ProjectInputSpec -> ModularModelGenerator -> native OrcaFlex YAML` semantic proof for CALM/SPM buoy systems.\\n\\n## Why now\\nPR #528 closed the first semantic-proof wave for PLET-to-PLEM jumpers, lazy/steep-wave risers, and the L03 OrcaWave ship benchmark. The exit handoff identified CALM/SPM buoy systems as the next high-value offshore structure family not yet proof-covered.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Identify or add a minimal CALM/SPM buoy canonical fixture.\\n- Prove object identity and generated native OrcaFlex structure for buoy, mooring/anchor/fairlead intent, line types, environmental/load-case intent, and vessel/tanker interaction boundary where applicable.\\n- Keep this deterministic and license-free unless a separate licensed-machine issue is approved.\\n\\n## Out of scope\\n- Full licensed OrcaFlex execution/load-run proof.\\n- AQWA vs OrcaFlex benchmark comparison from legacy #21 unless explicitly pulled into this issue by plan approval.\\n\\n## Related\\n- Closed first wave: #2455, #2456, #2457\\n- Existing related SPM benchmark issue: #21\\n- Handoff: `docs/handoffs/2026-04-23-orcawave-orcaflex-semantic-proof-exit-handoff.md`\\n- digitalmodel PR: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/528\\n\\n## Acceptance target\\nA plan should define the fixture path, generator assertions, TDD commands, semantic-equivalence criteria, and follow-up licensed-proof boundary before implementation.\\n\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2472\", \"display_title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): validate CALM/SPM buoy OrcaFlex semantic proof\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2454\", \"title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): validate flagship generic-track OrcaFlex mooring case via turret-moored FPSO semantic proof\", \"issue_number\": 2454, \"body\": \"## Summary\\nValidate a flagship generic-track OrcaFlex mooring case \\u2014 preferably turret-moored FPSO \\u2014 for semantic equivalence from canonical `spec.yml` to generated native OrcaFlex inputs.\\n\\n## Why\\nCurrent review shows the largest remaining OrcaFlex readiness gap is not generation capability but family-level proof for generic-track structures. A turret-moored FPSO is the highest-leverage case because it exercises vessel, mooring, environment, references, and generic pass-through behavior in one structure family.\\n\\n## Scope\\n- Select a representative generic-track mooring model, with preference for `c03_turret_moored_fpso`\\n- Establish canonical fixture path from `spec.yml` to generated native OrcaFlex YAML\\n- Run semantic comparison using the OrcaFlex semantic taxonomy\\n- Identify allowed vs blocking differences explicitly\\n- Document claim boundary for this family\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n- Fixture-backed validation for one flagship FPSO/mooring case\\n- Semantic diff artifact mapped to `digitalmodel/docs/domains/orcaflex/SEMANTIC_DIFF_TAXONOMY.md`\\n- Regression test or validation harness committed in digitalmodel\\n- Short readiness note added to the roadmap/operator docs if the family is promoted\\n\\n## Success criteria\\n- Generated native inputs preserve analysis-significant fields for the selected FPSO case\\n- Remaining differences are classified and justified, not hand-waved\\n- The family can be upgraded from partial to ready in the readiness matrix\\n\\n## Parent roadmap\\n- Reopened roadmap anchor: #1572\\n\\n## Related active issues\\n- Proof/hardening: #1652, #1788, #1586\\n- Structure readiness source: `docs/roadmaps/orcawave-orcaflex-canonical-spec-contract-roadmap.md`\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2454\", \"display_title\": \"feat(canonical-spec): validate flagship generic-track OrcaFlex mooring case via turret-moored FPSO semantic proof\"}, {\"url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1788\", \"title\": \"OrcaFlex .sim snapshot testing: HTML report from minimal_test.sim fixture\", \"issue_number\": 1788, \"body\": \"## Context\\n#1652 requested integration tests with real .sim fixtures and HTML snapshot testing. The minimal_test.sim fixture is now committed. The next step is building the snapshot test.\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n1. Test that loads minimal_test.sim metadata (without OrcFxAPI \\u2014 use the .dat text companion)\\n2. Generate an HTML report from the model metadata\\n3. Snapshot test: compare generated HTML against committed baseline\\n4. Extend to mooring_with_raos.sim if feasible without OrcFxAPI\\n\\n## Fixtures available\\n- digitalmodel/tests/fixtures/minimal_test.sim (318 KB)\\n- digitalmodel/tests/fixtures/minimal_test.dat (140 KB, text-readable)\\n- digitalmodel/tests/fixtures/solver/mooring_with_raos.sim (13.8 MB)\\n\\n## Related\\n- #1652 (parent)\", \"state\": null, \"user\": null, \"assignees\": null, \"labels\": null, \"milestone\": null, \"state_reason\": null, \"comments\": null, \"created_at\": null, \"updated_at\": null, \"closed_at\": null, \"display_url\": \"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1788\", \"display_title\": \"OrcaFlex .sim snapshot testing: HTML report from minimal_test.sim fixture\"}]}"}],"structured_content":{"issues":[{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269","title":"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation","issue_number":2269,"body":"## Summary\nStandardize the canonical OpenFOAM baseline for portable engineering work, using repo-tracked docs, scripts, example cases, and validation.\n\nPart of #1782.\nPrimary reference: #25.\nSupports downstream work such as #1268.\n\n## Motivation\nOpenFOAM is already CLI-native, but the baseline workflow is not yet fully encoded as a portable artifact bundle. We need a declared version target, canonical entrypoints, and a smoke validation path.\n\n## Scope\nCreate the baseline OpenFOAM portability package in workspace-hub.\n\nExpected deliverables:\n- baseline docs\n- run/validation entrypoints\n- minimal smoke example\n- troubleshooting guidance\n- shared skill update if reusable guidance is improved\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] baseline doc declares the target fork/version explicitly (default: ESI/OpenFOAM.com v2312 unless changed by decision)\n- [ ] canonical runner command(s) are documented\n- [ ] minimal smoke case exists under a reproducible repo-tracked path\n- [ ] validator produces pass/fail output with explicit checks\n- [ ] common failure modes and version/fork mismatches are documented\n- [ ] workflow can be executed on the canonical engineering host with documented prerequisites\n\n## Notes\nImplementation should prefer:\n- explicit version detection and fail-fast behavior\n- repo-tracked wrappers over tribal command memory\n- logs/results/validation outputs in predictable locations\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2269","display_title":"feat(openfoam): standardize ESI v2312 baseline workflow and validation"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2003","title":"Fix 5 failing tests in worldenergydata — test suite cleanup (#1962 Phase 3)","issue_number":2003,"body":"## Context\\n\\nAs part of the Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring (#1962), the audit found worldenergydata has 5 failing tests that must be resolved before any refactoring can proceed safely.\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n- Total tests: 431 (5 errors detected during collection)\\n- Command: `cd assetutilities && uv run python -m pytest ../worldenergydata/tests/ --noconftest` (or run from worldenergydata root)\\n\\n## Failing Tests\\n\\nRun `cd /mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub/worldenergydata && uv run python -m pytest tests/ -v 2>&1 | grep FAILED` to identify exact failures.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n- [ ] All 431 tests pass with 0 errors\\n- [ ] No test regressions\\n- [ ] Commits follow TDD convention","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2003","display_title":"Fix 5 failing tests in worldenergydata — test suite cleanup (#1962 Phase 3)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2006","title":"audit(worldenergydata): 14000+ orphan modules — triage for archival vs active use (#1962 Phase 3)","issue_number":2006,"body":"## Context\n\nTier-1 Repo Audit (#1962) identified **10+ orphan modules** in worldenergydata (files with 0 imports).\n\n## Orphan Detection Method\n\nThe audit used grep-based import counting — a weak heuristic that misses dynamic imports, __import__(), and importlib usage. So the true orphan count may be lower.\n\n## Work\n\n1. Run AST-based orphan detection (proper import analysis)\n2. For each confirmed orphan: check if it's a CLI entry point (not imported but executed)\n3. Archive truly unused modules or mark with `# CLI entry point` header\n4. Update documentation accordingly\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AST-based orphan scan complete\n- [ ] All orphans categorized (archive / CLI / dynamic import / keep)\n- [ ] Unused code archived with clear git history\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2006","display_title":"audit(worldenergydata): 14000+ orphan modules — triage for archival vs active use (#1962 Phase 3)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2005","title":"audit(digitalmodel): worldenergydata test collection timeout + 14K bytecode files — investigate and resolve","issue_number":2005,"body":"## Context\n\nTier-1 Repo Audit (#1962) found critical infrastructure issues in worldenergydata:\n\n### Test Collection Timeout\n- The test suite times out during collection (>30s)\n- Cannot even enumerate tests, let alone run them\n- Likely caused by heavy imports or expensive fixture setup\n\n### 14,145 Bytecode Files\n- Extremely deep dependency tree\n- 11.71s compile time — major code smell\n- May indicate excessive dependencies in pyproject.toml\n- Consider lazy imports or splitting dev dependencies\n\n## Investigation Steps\n\n1. `uv run python -c \"import time; start=time.time(); import worldenergydata; print(f'import took {time.time()-start:.2f}s')\"`\n2. Audit pyproject.toml — check for unnecessary dev dependencies being installed\n3. Profile imports: `python -X importtime -m worldenergydata 2>&1 | tail -50`\n4. Identify which 14K .pyc files belong to which packages\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Test collection completes within 30s\n- [ ] All 431 tests pass with 0 errors\n- [ ] Bytecode file count explained or reduced\n- [ ] pyproject.toml cleaned if needed\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2005","display_title":"audit(digitalmodel): worldenergydata test collection timeout + 14K bytecode files — investigate and resolve"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962","title":"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring — audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode","issue_number":1962,"body":"## Summary\n\nSystematic refactoring of the workspace-hub repo ecosystem, starting with tier-1 repositories. The goal is to reduce architectural debt, consolidate duplicate patterns, enforce consistent structure, and improve agent navigability across all tier-1 repos.\n\n## Tier-1 Repos (Priority Order)\n\n| # | Repo | Purpose | Maturity | Depends On |\n|---|------|---------|----------|------------|\n| 1 | `digitalmodel/` | Engineering digital twin (asset integrity, CP, GIS, hydro) | beta | assetutilities |\n| 2 | `assetutilities/` | Shared engineering utilities (config, units, calculations) | stable | — |\n| 3 | `assethold/` | Asset portfolio financial analysis | beta | assetutilities |\n| 4 | `worldenergydata/` | Global energy market data (BSEE, EIA, drilling) | beta | assetutilities |\n| 5 | `CAD-DEVELOPMENTS/` | CAD solvers, models, database, utilities | beta | — |\n| 6 | `aceengineer-website/` | Consulting website | — | — |\n| 7 | `aceengineer-strategy/` | GTM, business strategy | — | — |\n| 8 | `sabithaandkrishnaestates/` | SKEstates Inc. business ops | — | — |\n\n## Refactoring Scope\n\n### Phase 1: Audit (analysis-only, no code changes)\n- [ ] Run `repo-architecture-analysis` on each tier-1 repo\n- [ ] Identify cross-repo duplicate code (especially assetutilities consumers)\n- [ ] Map dependency graph (imports, shared configs, data flows)\n- [ ] Catalog test coverage per repo (current state)\n- [ ] Identify orphaned/dead code, unused modules, oversized files (>400 lines)\n- [ ] Score each repo against the Control-Plane Contract (AGENTS.md, provider adapters)\n- [ ] Identify skill duplicates across repos (134 known from vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 audit)\n\n### Phase 2: Plan (per-repo refactor plans)\n- [ ] Generate refactor plan per tier-1 repo with estimated effort\n- [ ] Prioritize by: business value × technical debt severity × dependency impact\n- [ ] Identify breaking changes and migration paths\n- [ ] Define test coverage targets (tier-1 = 85% per pytest.tier1.ini)\n\n### Phase 3: Execute (TDD, incremental, per-repo)\n- [ ] Execute refactors repo-by-repo following dependency order (assetutilities first)\n- [ ] TDD mandatory — tests before implementation\n- [ ] Each repo refactor as a separate sub-issue for tracking\n\n## Execution Strategy: Claude Code Plan Mode (Cloud)\n\n### Claude Code `--permission-mode plan` (\"Ultraplan\")\n\nClaude Code v2.1.92 supports `--permission-mode plan` which restricts the agent to **read-only analysis and planning** — no file writes, no shell mutations. This is ideal for Phase 1 (Audit) and Phase 2 (Plan).\n\n**Proposed execution:**\n\n```bash\n# Phase 1: Audit each tier-1 repo in plan mode (safe, read-only)\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\n  --print \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\n  -p \"Audit the repository architecture of digitalmodel/. Analyze: module structure, dependency graph, test coverage, dead code, file sizes >400 lines, AGENTS.md compliance. Output a structured JSON report.\"\n\n# Phase 2: Generate refactor plan\nclaude --permission-mode plan \\\n  --print \\\n  --model claude-opus-4-6 \\\n  --max-budget-usd 5 \\\n  -p \"Given the audit report, generate a prioritized refactor plan for digitalmodel/. Include: breaking changes, migration steps, test targets, estimated effort per module.\"\n```\n\n### Cloud Execution Considerations\n\n| Aspect | Status | Notes |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| **Claude Code CLI** | Installed (v2.1.92) | Available on ace-linux-1 |\n| **Plan mode** | Supported | `--permission-mode plan` — read-only, safe for audit |\n| **API-key mode** | Supported | `--bare` + `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` for headless/cloud runs |\n| **Print mode** | Supported | `-p` / `--print` for non-interactive batch execution |\n| **Budget cap** | Supported | `--max-budget-usd` to control spend per run |\n| **Cloud sandbox** | Possible | Can run via Hermes `delegate_task` with `acp_command='claude'` |\n| **Max subscription** | Claude Max $200/mo | Rate-limited but sufficient for plan-mode audit |\n\n### Recommended Approach\n1. **Phase 1 + 2 (Audit + Plan)**: Run Claude Code in plan mode via `delegate_task(acp_command='claude')` — subagents get isolated context, read-only, budget-capped\n2. **Phase 3 (Execute)**: Requires interactive Claude Code sessions with full permissions — NOT suited for autonomous cloud execution without user oversight\n\n## Adversarial Review Requirements\n\nPer [AI Review Routing Policy](docs/standards/AI_REVIEW_ROUTING_POLICY.md):\n\n- **3-agent adversarial review at BOTH stages** (plan + code/artifact)\n- Claude: orchestrator + reviewer\n- Codex: implementation worker + adversarial reviewer\n- Gemini: architecture reviewer + adversarial reviewer\n- Max 3 review iterations per deliverable\n- All MAJOR findings must be resolved before completion\n\n### Review Checkpoints\n1. **After Phase 1 (Audit)**: All 3 agents review the audit findings\n2. **After Phase 2 (Plan)**: All 3 agents review each repo's refactor plan\n3. **After Phase 3 (Execute)**: All 3 agents review code changes per repo\n\n## Related Issues\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1567 — Feature: Continuous Repo Architecture Intelligence\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1545 — Agentic feature progression for repo ecosystem productivity under GSD\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1603 — Multi-repo architecture scan — expand scanner to all tier-1 repos\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1720 — Cross-agent session corpus audit (134 duplicate skills identified)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1734 — chore: add domain skills to assethold (tier-1 with 0 skills)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1661 — Architecture scanner: dependency cycle detection and layering violations\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#113 — ROADMAP: Repo ecosystem 3-6 month horizon\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Audit report generated for each tier-1 repo\n- [ ] Refactor plan per repo reviewed and approved by all 3 agents\n- [ ] Phase 3 sub-issues created with clear scope per repo\n- [ ] Test coverage meets tier-1 target (85%) after refactoring\n- [ ] Zero cross-repo duplicate skills remaining\n- [ ] All repos score 100% on Control-Plane Contract compliance\n- [ ] Dependency graph documented and validated\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1962","display_title":"FEATURE: Tier-1 Repo Ecosystem Refactoring — audit, plan, execute with Claude Code plan mode"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2656","title":"chore(repo-structure): normalize workspace-hub folder/file structure","issue_number":2656,"body":"## Objective\n\nPlan a conservative, repo-by-repo folder/file structure normalization for `workspace-hub` under the workspace-hub tier-1 ecosystem workflow.\n\n## Scope\n\nPlanning only until explicit user approval. The plan must inventory current structure, identify root clutter/generated-output risk, propose a bounded Phase 1 refactor contract, define TDD/verification gates, and preserve evidence needed for rollback.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- Canonical plan artifact under `docs/plans/`.\n- Review artifact under `scripts/review/results/`.\n- Final issue comment linking the plan and review artifact.\n\n## Approval Gate\n\nNo file moves, package reorganization, deletion, or cleanup execution is authorized until this issue is explicitly approved after plan review.\n\nParent ecosystem anchors: `workspace-hub#1962`, `workspace-hub#2397`.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2656","display_title":"chore(repo-structure): normalize workspace-hub folder/file structure"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2653","title":"WRK-694: Per-session log files in session-logger.sh","issue_number":2653,"body":"## WRK-694: Per-session log files in session-logger.sh\n\n\n\n**Status:** Stage 18: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** medium | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** pipeline\n\n**Repo:** ['workspace-hub'] | **Complexity:** simple | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### What\n\nModify `.claude/hooks/session-logger.sh` so each Claude session gets its own\nlog file instead of merging all activity for the day into one file.\n\n### Why\n\nWhen multiple sessions run on the same day (or across a long session), the\ncurrent `session_YYYYMMDD.jsonl` file merges all tool calls into one file.\nPer-session files make it easier to correlate log entries with a specific\nconversation, debug session issues, and feed individual sessions into the RAG\npipeline cleanly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [ ] New session → new log file with `session_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS` name\n- [ ] Resumed session (within 2h) → same log file continues\n- [ ] Session idle >2h → next tool call starts a new log file\n- [ ] Both log paths (state/sessions/ and logs/orchestrator/claude/) use the session ID\n- [ ] State file is gitignored (already under `.claude/state/` which is gitignored)\n- [ ] Hook remains <1s execution time (raw-write only principle)\n- [ ] Existing date-based fans in analyze-sessions.sh or similar still work\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\n- [x] **1. Capture** — done\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** — done\n- [x] **3. Triage** — done\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** — done\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** — done\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** — done\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** — done\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** — done\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** — done\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** — done\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** — done\n- [x] **13. Agent Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **14. Verify Gate Evidence** — done\n- [x] **15. Future Work Synthesis** — done\n- [x] **16. Resource Intelligence Update** — done\n- [x] **17. User Review - Implementation** — done\n- [ ] **18. Reclaim** — n\n- [ ] **19. Close** — pending\n- [ ] **20. Archive** — pending\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2653","display_title":"WRK-694: Per-session log files in session-logger.sh"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2641","title":"feat(solver-queue): hands-off multi-machine inbox ingestion for OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA","issue_number":2641,"body":"## Summary\nAdd a hands-off multi-machine solver batch pipeline so a user can drop manifests/input files into a known inbox, have Linux hosts ingest/queue them, and have Windows licensed hosts run OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA/ANSYS jobs via periodic polling without manually touching the machines.\n\n## Current evidence\n- Existing solver queue exists: `queue/pending/` -> `queue/completed/` or `queue/failed/`, documented in `docs/solver/README.md` and `queue/job-schema.yaml`.\n- Existing submission path supports only `orcawave` and `orcaflex`: `scripts/solver/submit-job.sh`, `scripts/solver/submit-batch.sh`, `scripts/solver/validate_manifest.py`.\n- `ace-linux-2` is reachable and has AI/dev/open-source simulation tooling, but does **not** have OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, AQWA/ANSYS, MATLAB, or Python `OrcFxAPI`; it should coordinate/ingest/preprocess, not execute licensed solver jobs.\n- `ace-linux-2` NFS access to the ace-linux-1 knowledge/data center is via `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace`, not direct `/mnt/ace`.\n- `ace-linux-2` cron is drifted and points at stale `/mnt/workspace-hub` paths while the observed workspace is `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub`.\n- `licensed-win-1` / `licensed-win-2` are registry-designated OrcaFlex/ANSYS hosts, but no SSH access is configured; Windows Task Scheduler/Git Bash is the current execution route.\n\n## Required design\n1. Define canonical inbox/drop locations and file contract:\n   - repo inbox: `queue/inbox/` for Git-backed manifests\n   - optional shared-store inbox under `/mnt/ace/...` on ace-linux-1 and `/mnt/remote/ace-linux-1/ace/...` on ace-linux-2\n   - Windows-local workspace path mapping for `D:\\workspace-hub`\n2. Add a cron-safe ingestion wrapper that scans the inbox, validates manifests, atomically stages accepted jobs into `queue/pending/`, moves invalid submissions to quarantine, logs every action, and commits/pushes only accepted queue changes.\n3. Extend schema/validation/submission for `aqwa` or `ansys-aqwa` jobs with explicit input/output patterns and batch command contract.\n4. Extend Windows queue processor dispatch to route jobs by solver and host capability:\n   - OrcaWave/OrcaFlex on licensed Orcina host\n   - AQWA/ANSYS on ANSYS host\n   - no licensed solver execution on ace-linux-2\n5. Add scheduled-task definitions in `config/scheduled-tasks/schedule-tasks.yaml` and Windows Task Scheduler installation notes/scripts for licensed hosts.\n6. Repair or supersede ace-linux-2 cron path drift using canonical schedule rendering.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] A manifest/input dropped into the canonical inbox is ingested without manual machine access.\n- [ ] Valid OrcaWave/OrcaFlex jobs appear in `queue/pending/` and are picked up by licensed Windows queue processing.\n- [ ] AQWA/ANSYS jobs validate through schema and route to the ANSYS-capable host instead of being rejected as unknown solver types.\n- [ ] Invalid submissions are quarantined with actionable error logs, not silently ignored.\n- [ ] `ace-linux-2` scheduled tasks use `/mnt/local-analysis/workspace-hub` or a verified symlink/path contract, not stale `/mnt/workspace-hub` references.\n- [ ] Tests cover manifest validation, inbox ingestion, atomic moves/quarantine, and solver dispatch selection.\n- [ ] Documentation explains roles: ace-linux-1 control plane, ace-linux-2 ingestion/preprocess/overflow worker, licensed Windows hosts solver execution.\n\n## Related\n- Parent/related: #1586 solver queue hardening\n- Related: #2170 Windows licensed tool probe adapters\n- Related: #1564 ace-linux-2 crontab sync\n- Related: #1119 multi-machine work distribution\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2641","display_title":"feat(solver-queue): hands-off multi-machine inbox ingestion for OrcaWave, OrcaFlex, and AQWA"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2640","title":"feat(gtm): worldenergydata production decline forecast — shareable Jupyter notebook for GTM demo","issue_number":2640,"body":"## Context\n\nIssue [#117](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117) acceptance criteria includes:\n> Build shareable Jupyter notebook: production decline forecast (worldenergydata)\n\nThe `worldenergydata` library now has a production-ready `ArpsDeclineCurve` module at `worldenergydata.production.forecast.decline`. The GTM candidate review (2026-05-04) confirmed it works end-to-end:\n- Hyperbolic, exponential, harmonic curve fitting via scipy\n- EUR (Estimated Ultimate Recovery) calculation\n- 240-month forecast with confidence intervals\n- Plotly interactive visualization\n\nA 63 KB FDAS GTM HTML report was already generated (`reports/gtm/2026-05-04-fdas-field-development-economics.html`), which includes an Arps decline demo with synthetic data.\n\n## Scope\n\nCreate a standalone Jupyter notebook at `worldenergydata/notebooks/gtm_production_decline_forecast.ipynb`:\n\n1. **Section 1: Module overview** — what Arps decline is, three model types, typical GoM/North Sea use cases\n2. **Section 2: Synthetic demo** — fit a synthetic GoM well (qi=5000 bbl/month, Di=0.08/month, b=0.6), show EUR=146 Mbbls\n3. **Section 3: Multi-well portfolio** — fit 3 hypothetical wells (early, mid, late decline stages), side-by-side comparison\n4. **Section 4: Sensitivity** — how EUR changes with b (hyperbolic exponent) from 0.2 to 1.0\n5. **Section 5: Connect to FDAS economics** — feed EUR directly into `EconomicsQuery.npv()` to compute field-level NPV from decline curve\n\nAll sections must run without external data (synthetic data generated in-notebook).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Notebook executes end-to-end with `jupyter nbconvert --to notebook --execute` in <60 seconds\n- [ ] All Plotly charts render (interactive HTML output cells)\n- [ ] A companion `reports/gtm/gtm_production_decline_forecast.html` is exported from the notebook\n- [ ] Notebook added to `worldenergydata/notebooks/README.md` index\n- [ ] No BSEE binary data required to run\n\n## References\n\n- Parent: [#117](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/117)\n- Implemented ArpsDeclineCurve: `worldenergydata/src/worldenergydata/production/forecast/decline.py`\n- FDAS GTM report with Arps demo: `worldenergydata/reports/gtm/2026-05-04-fdas-field-development-economics.html`\n- Generation script reference: `worldenergydata/scripts/gtm/generate_fdas_gtm_report.py`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2640","display_title":"feat(gtm): worldenergydata production decline forecast — shareable Jupyter notebook for GTM demo"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2628","title":"epic(digitalmodel-ci): domain-divided CI architecture replacing maxfail-masking pattern","issue_number":2628,"body":"## Why now\n\nThis session surfaced that digitalmodel's QG suite has been chronically green-washed by \\`maxfail=20\\` masking 200+ pre-existing failures across 53 test files. The admin-merge-via-Quality-Gates-failure pattern (PRs [#541](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/541), [#544](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/544), [#549](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/549), [#550](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/550), [#551](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/551), [#553](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/553), [#568](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/568), [#569](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/569)) is now the default merge path — operationally fast but strategically corrosive: every PR carries the same 'gate trips on unrelated debt' justification.\n\nRoot cause: a single repo-wide \\`pytest --maxfail=20\\` invocation can't distinguish 'this PR introduced a regression in its own scope' from 'unrelated debt elsewhere in the repo.' Per-domain CI fixes both.\n\n## Architecture\n\n**14-15 test domains** (per [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) sweep + tests/ tree):\n\n\\`asset_integrity\\`, \\`citations\\`, \\`contracts\\`, \\`field_development\\`, \\`hydrodynamics-diffraction\\`, \\`hydrodynamics-passing-ship\\`, \\`infrastructure-core\\`, \\`marine-engineering\\`, \\`naval-architecture\\`, \\`orcaflex\\`, \\`orcaflex-solver\\`, \\`solver-smoke\\`, \\`specialized\\`, \\`structural\\`, \\`workflows\\`, \\`misc\\`\n\nEach domain becomes:\n- a separate pytest job in a GH Actions matrix\n- with its own \\`--with\\` dep list (so e.g. redis/psycopg2/motor only attach to \\`infrastructure-core\\`, not the whole CI)\n- with its own conftest fixtures and skipif policies\n- with the existing \\`maxfail\\` removed (per-domain failure counts stay bounded by domain size)\n\n## Decisions locked (this session, vamseeachanta)\n\n| Decision | Choice |\n|---|---|\n| Domain owner | All domains owned by \\`vamseeachanta\\` (single owner; CODEOWNERS encodes this) |\n| Aggregator gate policy | **All domains must pass** for merge (no informational-only mode) |\n| Run cadence | **Touched-domains-only on PRs**; **full matrix nightly** (cron) |\n| Deprecation policy | No test deletion or blanket-skip without AI-attested 'source X is gone, no other consumers' commit per \\`feedback_attestation_enables_contradiction_detection\\` |\n\n## Sub-tasks\n\n- [ ] **A** — Domain inventory PR (\\`tests/DOMAINS.md\\` mapping every test directory → domain → dep set; informs all subsequent PRs). Read-only repo analysis. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **B** — \\`.claude/quality-gates.yaml\\` refactor: one gate per domain with its own command + deps. Aggregator gate depends on all. Drops repo-wide \\`maxfail\\`. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **C** — GH Actions matrix workflow: \\`.github/workflows/quality-gates-by-domain.yml\\` with \\`strategy.matrix.domain\\` for parallel runs + aggregator job. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **D** — Touched-domain detection: PR-time job that diffs changed paths against \\`tests/DOMAINS.md\\` and emits the matrix subset to run. Nightly cron uses full matrix. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **E** — Per-domain conftest extraction: move shared fixtures/skipifs from \\`tests/conftest.py\\` into each domain's local conftest. ~1 PR per domain (small each). \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **F** — CODEOWNERS file with \\`vamseeachanta\\` as default owner. \\`digitalmodel\\`.\n- [ ] **G** — Update workspace-hub agents.md + CLAUDE.md to encode the 'always test by domain during development' rule for AI agents. \\`workspace-hub\\`.\n\n## After A-G land, the deferred cluster work re-routes by domain\n\n| Cluster | Issue | New domain home |\n|---|---|---|\n| A — sys.modules pollution | [#2623](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623) | \\`contracts\\` domain |\n| D — infrastructure dep wall | [#2624](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624) | \\`infrastructure-core\\` domain — its own \\`--with redis --with psycopg2-binary --with motor\\` |\n| E — marine-engineering regressions | [#2625](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625) | \\`marine-engineering\\` domain — its own targeted suite |\n\nThese clusters become much cheaper to fix per-domain than under repo-wide CI.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Domain inventory exists and is reviewable\n- [ ] PR for trivial change (e.g. docs typo) only runs domains that own changed paths; runs in <2 min\n- [ ] PR that touches multiple domains runs all touched domains; aggregator gates correctly\n- [ ] Nightly full matrix runs all domains; failure surfaces per-domain in GH Actions UI\n- [ ] No test files deleted as part of this work; debt that surfaces is filed per-domain for owner triage\n- [ ] Existing admin-merge cycle ends — next post-A-G PR merges through the new gate without override\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2614](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2614), [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616) — investigations that surfaced the masking\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) — issue-state drift / quality-gates audit umbrella\n- [vamseeachanta/digitalmodel#543](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/543) — PR that introduced \\`maxfail=20\\`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2628","display_title":"epic(digitalmodel-ci): domain-divided CI architecture replacing maxfail-masking pattern"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2510","title":"feat(cad): build Python layout/CAD automation demo for chip/package geometries","issue_number":2510,"body":"## Context\nParent: #2507\nRoadmap: `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\n\nBuild a Python-first geometry/layout automation example that bridges CAD practices into semiconductor layout and package/interposer-style structures.\n\n## Proposed scope\n- Use KLayout scripting and/or GDSFactory to generate a parameterized layout artifact.\n- Export/import GDS/OASIS and at least one human-inspectable representation such as SVG/DXF where practical.\n- Extract metadata: layers, polygons, bounding boxes, ports/nets where available, geometry invariants.\n- Include tests around geometry invariants and repeatability.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Python module/script for parameterized layout generation.\n2. Sample output artifacts or reproducible generation command.\n3. Metadata extraction report.\n4. Tests and documentation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Demo can be run locally without proprietary tools.\n- Generated artifacts are deterministic enough for tests or snapshot checks.\n- Report explains relevance to chip design, packaging, and CAD automation roles.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2510","display_title":"feat(cad): build Python layout/CAD automation demo for chip/package geometries"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625","title":"investigate(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster E — marine-engineering domain regressions (~60 failures across catenary/wave/ocimf/RAO)","issue_number":2625,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). ~60 failures concentrated in \\`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/\\` and adjacent. Without per-test detail (the maxfail=200 probe used \\`--tb=line\\` only), root causes can't be characterized inline.\n\n## Affected (by file, partial — full list at \\`/tmp/qg-failures-clean.txt\\`)\n\n| Count | File |\n|---:|---|\n| 16 | \\`test_cathodic_protection_dnv.py\\` |\n| 11 | \\`test_mooring_catenary.py\\` |\n| 11 | \\`legacy/test_mooring_catenary.py\\` (mirror of above) |\n| 5 | \\`test_unified_rao_reader.py\\` |\n| 4 | \\`test_catenary_adapter.py\\` |\n| 4 | \\`legacy/test_component_database.py\\` |\n| 3 | \\`test_marine_eng_perf.py\\` |\n| 3 | \\`integration/test_hydro_rao_integration.py\\` |\n| 3 | \\`catenary/test_simplified.py\\` |\n| ~10 | scattered across catenary/wave/ocimf integration files |\n\n## Recommended next step\n\nRun a targeted \\`--tb=short\\` re-run on \\`tests/marine_ops/marine_engineering/\\` to characterize root causes. Likely sub-clusters:\n\n- Domain code regressions (real defects in catenary solver, RAO reader, etc.)\n- Missing data files (test fixtures gone)\n- Knock-on effects of test pollution (could be related to Cluster A → [#2623](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623))\n\nAfter per-cluster characterization, file individual sub-trackers and link back to this umbrella.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nThis issue is the umbrella; close it once sub-trackers are filed and assigned.\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2625","display_title":"investigate(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster E — marine-engineering domain regressions (~60 failures across catenary/wave/ocimf/RAO)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624","title":"policy(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster D — decide whether redis/psycopg2/motor belong in QG suite (51 errors)","issue_number":2624,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). 51 errors in \\`tests/infrastructure/core/\\` are environmental — pure dep-wall, not feature defects.\n\n## Affected\n\n- \\`tests/infrastructure/core/test_cache.py\\` (32 errors) — requires \\`redis\\` backend\n- \\`tests/infrastructure/core/test_database_manager.py\\` (19 errors) — logs \\`No module named 'psycopg2'\\` and references \\`motor\\` (async MongoDB)\n\nThese would also fail in the original CI run with the same env. Whether they were ever expected to run in the QG suite is a policy question.\n\n## Decision needed\n\nPick one:\n\n- **(a) Add deps to QG environment**: \\`--with redis --with psycopg2-binary --with motor\\` to the QG command. Keeps tests in the QG tier. Adds CI install time. Requires actual Redis/Postgres/Mongo containers in CI for tests to pass (not just import). Likely heaviest infrastructure lift.\n- **(b) Mark with import-probe skipif**: add \\`@pytest.mark.skipif(not _has_redis, reason=\\\"redis backend unavailable\\\")\\` per-class in those test files. Cheap fix; tests skip cleanly when the backend isn't available; tests still run on machines that have Redis/Postgres/Mongo.\n- **(c) Move to a separate \\\"integration\\\" tier outside QG**: e.g., a separate pytest mark or a dedicated tox env that QG doesn't invoke. Cleanest separation; requires a tier-routing decision.\n\nRecommendation: (b) for fastest unblock; (c) for long-term cleanliness.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] 51 errors in \\`tests/infrastructure/core/\\` either pass (with backends installed) or skip cleanly (no errors)\n- [ ] QG command produces 0 of these errors\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)\n- [#2129](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2129) — quality-gates audit umbrella","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2624","display_title":"policy(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster D — decide whether redis/psycopg2/motor belong in QG suite (51 errors)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623","title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster A — sys.modules pollution leaking MagicMock into tests/contracts/ (2 failures)","issue_number":2623,"body":"## Context\n\nSurfaced during [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616). Two tests in \\`tests/contracts/\\` fail in the full suite but pass in isolation — classic test-pollution Heisenbug, made deterministic by \\`-p no:randomly\\`.\n\n## Defect\n\n- \\`tests/contracts/test_assetutilities_common_contract.py::test_update_deep_dictionary_importable\\` → \\`AssertionError: <MagicMock> == 1\\`\n- \\`tests/contracts/test_assetutilities_data_contract.py::test_AttributeDict_importable\\` → \\`AssertionError: <MagicMock> == 'value'\\`\n\n## Root cause\n\nSome upstream test in the suite registers \\`assetutilities.common.update_deep\\` and \\`assetutilities.common.data\\` (or similar \\`assetutilities.*\\` modules) as \\`MagicMock()\\` in \\`sys.modules\\` without restoring them. With \\`-p no:randomly\\` the ordering is deterministic, so the pollution is reproducible. When the contract tests later import the real symbols they receive the mock.\n\n## Verification\n\n\\`pytest tests/contracts/\\` in isolation → **17/17 pass**. Sibling repo \\`../assetutilities/src/assetutilities/common/update_deep.py\\` carries both \\`update_deep_dictionary\\` and \\`AttributeDict\\` correctly.\n\n## Fix options\n\n- **(a) Hunt the polluter** — search candidates: tests that \\`import unittest.mock; sys.modules[...] = MagicMock()\\` or \\`@patch.dict('sys.modules', ...)\\` without proper teardown. Similar to the existing \\`tests/conftest.py:92\\` pattern for \\`digitalmodel.subsea.catenary_riser.legacy.catenary_riser_summary\\`. Then add proper teardown to that test.\n- **(b) Defensive autouse fixture** in \\`tests/contracts/conftest.py\\` that purges polluted entries before each test:\n  \\`\\`\\`python\n  for k in list(sys.modules):\n      if k.startswith('assetutilities') and isinstance(sys.modules[k], MagicMock):\n          del sys.modules[k]\n  \\`\\`\\`\n\nRecommend (a) for the right fix; (b) as a safety net if (a) requires more bisection time than is available.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Both contract tests pass in the full suite under \\`-p no:randomly\\`\n- [ ] If (a): polluter identified and fixed; \\`tests/contracts/\\` no longer needs special handling\n- [ ] If (b): conftest fixture documented with the polluter's path so it can be revisited\n\n## Related\n\n- [#2616](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2616)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2623","display_title":"fix(digitalmodel-tests): Cluster A — sys.modules pollution leaking MagicMock into tests/contracts/ (2 failures)"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112","title":"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking","issue_number":2112,"body":"## Summary\nBackfill the minimum SubseaIQ equipment-count fields required to unblock #2055 subsea cost benchmarking.\n\n## Context\nAssessment of #2055 found two active blockers:\n1. `status:needs-data`\n2. the required equipment-count fields are absent from every current GoM record in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n\nCurrent state from assessment:\n- required threshold: >=10 GoM fields with equipment counts\n- current state: 0/10 records have `num_trees`, `num_manifolds`, or `tieback_distance_km`\n\nWithout this backfill, the cost benchmarking implementation issue cannot proceed honestly.\n\n## Scope\n- Backfill or derive, for at least 10 GoM fields, the following fields in the SubseaIQ-derived dataset:\n  - `num_trees`\n  - `num_manifolds`\n  - `tieback_distance_km`\n- Document provenance / confidence for each backfilled field\n- Update the dataset and any ingestion/normalization helpers needed to preserve the fields\n- Add a minimal validation check proving the threshold is satisfied\n\n## Target data\n- `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n- any normalization or ingestion surfaces that write/read these fields\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- At least 10 GoM records contain non-null equipment counts and tieback distance\n- Provenance/confidence is documented\n- A validation command or test confirms the threshold is met\n- #2055 can remove `status:needs-data` after verification\n\n## Relationship\n- Direct prerequisite for #2055\n- Should be completed before any cost-per-tree / cost-per-manifold implementation work\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2112","display_title":"data(field-dev): backfill SubseaIQ equipment counts to unblock cost benchmarking"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2558","title":"data(field-dev): primary-source GoM equipment/cost source pack for #2112/#2055","issue_number":2558,"body":"## Summary\nCreate the next #2112 source-quality pass: a primary/public-source evidence pack for #2055's GoM field-development cost benchmarking gate.\n\nThis is a follow-up to the provisional artifact committed in `624c87f12`, which intentionally kept #2055 blocked because the candidate dataset failed source/semantic review.\n\n## Parent / related\n- Parent blocker: #2112\n- Downstream benchmark feature: #2055\n- Related field-development roadmap: #1842, #1861\n- Current provisional report: `docs/reports/field-development-unblock-2112.md`\n- Current provisional dataset: `data/field-development/gom-field-development-unblock-2112.json`\n\n## Problem\nThe provisional dataset is useful as a source-lead list but is not correlation-ready. The adversarial review found that:\n\n- `num_manifolds` was sometimes proxy-coded from drill centers/risers/field centers.\n- `tieback_distance_km` mixed incompatible definitions.\n- `cost_usd_bn` mixed public project costs with estimates/seed values.\n- Some facts came from snippets or weak secondary references.\n\n## Scope\nBuild a citable source pack and normalize definitions before any correlation/model implementation.\n\nRequired source-backed fields per accepted record:\n\n| Field | Required interpretation |\n|---|---|\n| `num_trees` | actual subsea/wet tree count, or clearly sourced well/tree count |\n| `num_manifolds` | actual subsea manifold/PLEM/PLET count only; no drill-center proxy |\n| `tieback_distance_km` | one explicitly named definition, preferably subsea field/well/cluster to host/receiving facility |\n| `cost_usd_bn` | project/phase cost with year/currency/scope; no generic cost-class seed |\n| `source_notes` | durable public URL(s), source type, quote/paraphrase, confidence |\n\n## Deliverables\n- A source evidence table/report under `docs/reports/` or `.planning/intel/`.\n- Updated dataset or companion JSON that distinguishes accepted records from rejected/needs-source records.\n- A validator that fails if accepted records lack source/definition evidence.\n- Clear recommendation on whether #2112 can close and whether #2055 can drop `status:needs-data`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- At least 10 GoM records pass the public-source/no-invented-values semantic gate, **or** the report explicitly says fewer than 10 pass and keeps #2055 blocked.\n- Every accepted numeric field has a durable public citation and consistent definition.\n- No snippet-only, proxy-coded, or seeded numeric values are treated as accepted facts.\n- #2055 remains `status:needs-data` unless the validator confirms the threshold.\n\n## Execution constraints\n- Plan first per repo policy; do not execute until approved.\n- Use TDD/validator-first for the accepted-record gate.\n- Prefer null + `needs_source` over estimates.\n- Do not implement cost correlations until the source pack passes.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2558","display_title":"data(field-dev): primary-source GoM equipment/cost source pack for #2112/#2055"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055","title":"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts","issue_number":2055,"body":"## Summary\n\nBuild cost-per-equipment-unit benchmark curves (cost/tree, cost/km-flowline, cost/manifold) by water-depth band, correlating SubseaIQ equipment counts against sanctioned project costs.\n\n## Problem\n\nThe scaffold (#1861) provides `SubseaProject` with equipment-count fields and `load_projects()`, but zero cost functions exist. No module currently correlates equipment counts with project costs.\n\n## Data Prerequisites (must be met before implementation)\n\n- [ ] Equipment counts (num_trees, num_manifolds, tieback_distance_km) backfilled for >= 10 GoM fields in `data/field-development/subseaiq-scan-latest.json`\n- [ ] Confirmed name-match mapping between SubseaIQ project names and CostDataPoint project names for overlapping records\n\n## v1 Scope (band-level aggregation)\n\nUse depth-band-level aggregation to compute cost-per-equipment ratios. This avoids per-project name-match fragility and works with sparse data.\n\n### New functions in `digitalmodel/src/digitalmodel/field_development/benchmarks.py`:\n- `cost_per_tree(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\n- `cost_per_km_flowline(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\n- `cost_per_manifold(projects) -> dict[str, float]` by depth band\n- `unit_cost_curves(projects) -> dict` combining all three metrics\n- `cost_benchmark_bands(projects) -> dict` with low/base/high by depth band and concept type\n\n### New helper in `worldenergydata/subseaiq/analytics/cost_correlation.py`:\n- `correlate_equipment_costs(subseaiq_records, cost_records)` — depth-band-level join\n- `aggregate_costs_by_depth_band(merged)` — band-level cost aggregation\n\n## Deferred to v2\n\n- Per-project cost-per-tree correlation (requires CostDataPoint schema extension with equipment fields)\n- Cross-validation of equipment-derived costs against individual sanctioned project costs\n- Full 71-record equipment-count backfill\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Equipment counts populated for >= 10 GoM fields\n- [ ] `cost_per_tree` returns values in $10M-$200M range per depth band\n- [ ] `cost_per_km_flowline` returns values in $5M-$50M range per depth band\n- [ ] `cost_per_manifold` returns physically reasonable values per depth band\n- [ ] `cost_benchmark_bands` returns nested dict with low/base/high structure\n- [ ] All 27 existing `test_benchmarks.py` tests pass unchanged\n- [ ] New cost-related tests added and passing\n- [ ] `benchmarks` exported from `digitalmodel.field_development.__init__`\n\n## Dependencies\n\n- #1861 (scaffold) — DONE\n- Equipment-count data backfill — REQUIRED before implementation\n\n## Labels\n\n`enhancement`, `priority:high`, `cat:engineering`, `dark-intelligence`, `agent:claude`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2055","display_title":"feat(field-dev): subsea cost benchmarking from SubseaIQ equipment counts"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/18","title":"WRK-023: Property GIS development timeline with future projection and Google Earth animation","issue_number":18,"body":"## WRK-023: Property GIS development timeline with future projection and Google Earth animation\n\n\n\n**Status:** Stage 17: Reclaim (n) | **Priority:** low | **Category:** engineering | **Subcategory:** pipeline\n\n**Repo:** ['assethold'] | **Complexity:** complex | **Machine:** dev-primary\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n### What\n\nFor a given property, build a GIS-based timeline showing how the surrounding area has developed over time and project how future development may evolve. Leverage historical satellite imagery (Google Earth historical imagery, Landsat, Sentinel), permit/zoning data, and development patterns to create:\n1. **Historical timeline**: visual progression of development around the property over years/decades\n2. **Future projection**: anticipated development based on zoning plans, permit filings, growth patterns\n3. **Animation**: Google Earth-style animated flythrough or timelapse showing development progression\n\n### Why\n\nUnderstanding how an area has developed — and where it's heading — is critical for property investment decisions. A visual GIS timeline makes development patterns immediately apparent and supports investment thesis with spatial evidence rather than just market comparables.\n\n\n\n---\n\n\n\n<details><summary>Implementation Summary</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Final Plan</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Acceptance Criteria</summary>\n\n- [ ] Historical satellite/aerial imagery retrieval for a property location across multiple time periods\n- [x] Development change detection: identify new construction, road expansion, land use changes\n- [x] Permit and zoning data integration where available (local government open data)\n- [x] Timeline visualization: side-by-side or slider comparison of historical imagery\n- [x] Future development projection based on:\n- [x] Google Earth animation / KML timelapse export\n- [ ] Blender 3D visualization option for development progression\n- [x] Report output with timeline narrative and key development milestones\n- [x] Document data sources, projection methodology, and confidence levels\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>TDD Results</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Evidence and Cost</summary>\n\nNot yet available\n\n</details>\n\n<details><summary>Stage Progress</summary>\n\n- [x] **1. Capture** — done\n- [x] **2. Resource Intelligence** — done\n- [x] **3. Triage** — done\n- [x] **4. Plan Draft** — done\n- [x] **5. User Review - Plan (Draft)** — done\n- [x] **6. Cross-Review** — done\n- [x] **7. User Review - Plan (Final)** — done\n- [x] **8. Claim / Activation** — done\n- [x] **9. Work-Queue Routing Skill** — done\n- [x] **10. Work Execution** — done\n- [x] **11. Artifact Generation** — done\n- [x] **12. TDD / Eval** — done\n- [x] **13. Verify Gate Evidence** — done\n- [x] **14. Future Work Synthesis** — done\n- [x] **15. Resource Intelligence Update** — done\n- [x] **16. User Review** — done\n- [ ] **17. Reclaim** — n\n- [ ] **18. Close** — pending\n- [ ] **19. Archive** — pending\n\n</details>","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/18","display_title":"WRK-023: Property GIS development timeline with future projection and Google Earth animation"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327","title":"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation","issue_number":2327,"body":"## Context\n\nCadQuery is a Python library for parametric 3D CAD on the OpenCASCADE B-rep kernel. STEP export is high-fidelity, so downstream meshers (gmsh, Salome) can control density without being trapped by authoring-time facets — a good shape for offshore component geometry that feeds AQWA.\n\nDiscovered via HN discussion 2026-04-17 (180 points, 47 comments): https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\n\n> **Revised 2026-04-17 per [adversarial review](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327#issuecomment-4269065194)**: time-boxed; scope narrowed to one family and one pipeline; go/no-go criteria quantified; LLM-codegen question dropped (covered by #2328); current-state baseline required in plan.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Time-box:** 4 hours of hands-on work. Stop and report at the cap regardless of completeness.\n\n**Target pipeline (committed):** STEP (CadQuery) → gmsh → AQWA `QPPL DIFF` panel diffraction. Chosen because panel diffraction has the tightest mesh-quality requirements (hardest pipeline test); digitalmodel already consumes AQWA downstream; OrcaFlex line-element path is a trivial follow-up if this works.\n\n**Target family (committed):** H-link mooring shackle, 4 variants (MBL 45 t, 65 t, 85 t, 125 t). Chosen because mooring hardware is a recurring workspace-hub domain (#2070 cost-tracking, mooring-failures knowledge seed) and 4 variants exercises the \"generate N from parameters\" claim without inflating scope.\n\n## Current State (document in the plan's Resource Intel section)\n\n- Current `digitalmodel/` geometry pipeline — tools, inputs, outputs\n- Where STEP files come from today (if at all)\n- Which solver consumers exist and their geometry-input formats\n\n## Go / No-Go Acceptance Criteria\n\nAll three must hold for **go**:\n\n1. **Time-to-first-STEP < 30 min** for a Python-comfortable engineer working from CadQuery docs alone (no pre-existing exemplars)\n2. **Regenerating all 4 H-link variants < 60 s** of compute total\n3. **STEP → gmsh → AQWA `QPPL DIFF`** runs with no fatal mesh errors on first attempt for at least **2 of 4** variants\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/h-link-family/` — 1 parametric script, 4 STEP outputs, 4 meshes, 1 successful AQWA run log\n- `digitalmodel/examples/cadquery-spike/README.md` — timings, mesh quality notes, explicit verdict against the 3 criteria above\n\n## Planning Workflow (per CLAUDE.md)\n\nPlan file: `docs/plans/2026-MM-DD-issue-2327-cadquery-digitalmodel-spike.md` using `docs/plans/_template-issue-plan.md`. Resource-intel section must include the current-state documentation above. No implementation until `status:plan-approved`.\n\n## References\n\n- Wiki entity: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\n- Source summary: `knowledge/wikis/engineering/wiki/sources/2026-04-17-hn-cadquery.md`\n- Marine cross-page: `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/entities/cadquery.md`\n- Sibling issues: #2328 (tool comparison — covers the LLM-codegen question), #2329 (methodology decision — blocked by this)\n- HN thread: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47772725\n- CadQuery: https://cadquery.github.io/","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2327","display_title":"digitalmodel: CadQuery spike for parametric offshore geometry generation"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2028","title":"feat: review gate strict mode + GitHub Actions CI enforcement workflow","issue_number":2028,"body":"## Enable review gate strict mode and add GitHub Actions CI enforcement\n\n### Current State\n- require-review-on-push.sh exists but REVIEW_GATE_STRICT defaults to 0 (advisory)\n- Pre-push hook calls review gate but only warns\n- No GitHub Actions workflow for PR-level enforcement\n- Compliance at 20% (improving from 4%)\n\n### What Needs to Happen\n\n**Phase 1: Enable Local Review Gate Strict Mode**\nAfter plan gate is working (#2025), escalate:\n```bash\n# In .git/hooks/enforcement-env:\nexport REVIEW_GATE_STRICT=1\n```\nThis will block pushes without cross-review evidence.\n\n**Phase 2: GitHub Actions CI Workflow**\nCreate `.github/workflows/enforcement-gate.yml`:\n```yaml\nname: Enforcement Gate\non: [pull_request]\njobs:\n  check-plan-approval:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Check Plan Approval\n        run: bash scripts/enforcement/require-plan-approval.sh --strict\n      - name: Check Review Evidence\n        run: bash scripts/enforcement/require-review-on-push.sh HEAD $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)\n  compliance-dashboard:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - name: Run Compliance Check\n        run: bash scripts/enforcement/compliance-dashboard.sh\n```\n\n**Phase 3: Progressive Rollout**\n| Week | What | Risk |\n|------|------|------|\n| 1 | Plan gate strict (local only) | Low -- blocks obvious violations |\n| 2 | Review gate strict (local only) | Medium -- may block legitimate work |\n| 3 | CI workflow (PR level) | Medium -- external visibility |\n| 4 | Full enforcement (all gates) | Low -- everything tested |\n\n### Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] REVIEW_GATE_STRICT=1 in enforcement-env\n- [ ] Push without review evidence is blocked\n- [ ] GitHub Actions workflow created and passing\n- [ ] PRs without plan approval are blocked by CI\n- [ ] Compliance dashboard runs as CI step\n- [ ] Bypass logging captures all override attempts\n\n### Related\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2025 -- Plan gate strict mode and hook wiring\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#1876 -- Enforcement infrastructure (parent)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2017 -- Agent bypass resistance (metrics)\n- vamseeachanta/workspace-hub#2012 -- Review backlog audit\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2028","display_title":"feat: review gate strict mode + GitHub Actions CI enforcement workflow"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2474","title":"feat(canonical-spec): add OrcaFlex native reverse-parser equivalence proof","issue_number":2474,"body":"## Summary\nAdd stronger OrcaFlex native YAML -> canonical semantic extraction/equivalence proof so OrcaFlex reaches parity with the OrcaWave roundtrip proof added in PR #528.\n\n## Why now\nPR #528 established OrcaWave reverse parser roundtrip coverage for L03 and deterministic forward OrcaFlex YAML assertions for jumpers/risers. The handoff identifies bidirectional OrcaFlex reverse parsing as the next major semantic-proof gap.\n\n## Scope\n- Inventory existing OrcaFlex native YAML readers/parsers, if any.\n- Define the minimum semantic extraction contract for generated native OrcaFlex YAML back to canonical intent.\n- Add tests for at least the currently covered PLET-to-PLEM, lazy-wave, and steep-wave fixtures if feasible.\n- Document intentionally ignored native defaults/formatting differences.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Licensed `.sim`/`.dat` binary parsing without OrcFxAPI.\n- Full solver execution proof; track separately under licensed-machine proof.\n\n## Related\n- Closed first wave: #2455, #2456, #2457\n- Existing real `.sim` fixture issue: #1652\n- Handoff: `docs/handoffs/2026-04-23-orcawave-orcaflex-semantic-proof-exit-handoff.md`\n- digitalmodel PR: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/528\n\n## Acceptance target\nA plan should define parser/extractor ownership, TDD tests, equivalence criteria, and explicit binary-file/licensed-machine boundaries.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2474","display_title":"feat(canonical-spec): add OrcaFlex native reverse-parser equivalence proof"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2516","title":"feat(digitalmodel): flexible pipe and dynamic riser cross-section mechanics follow-up","issue_number":2516,"body":"## Summary\nPlan a deeper flexible pipe and dynamic riser cross-section mechanics module after the basic cable/umbilical/pipeline schema exists.\n\n## Why\nFlexible pipes/risers have layer interactions, carcass/liner behavior, pressure armour, tensile armour, outer sheath, collapse, bend radius, torsion, fatigue, and annulus monitoring concerns. They should not be squeezed into the first generic cross-section schema without dedicated mechanics and validation.\n\n## Proposed scope\n- Research public standards/source map for API 17J/API 17B, vendor primers, and open literature on unbonded flexible pipe layers.\n- Define what can be represented as metadata vs what requires mechanics/calculation modules.\n- Identify OrcaFlex property export needs for flexible risers and dynamic jumpers.\n- Produce a bounded implementation plan with fixtures and tests.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- Clear boundary between first-pass cross-section schema and flexible-pipe mechanics.\n- Source-backed taxonomy of layers: carcass/liner, pressure sheath, pressure armour, tensile armour, outer sheath, insulation or anti-wear layers where applicable.\n- Follow-up implementation plan includes TDD fixtures and validation paths for bend radius, collapse/pressure metadata, and section-property export.\n- No implementation starts without plan review and user approval.\n\n## Related knowledge\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/subsea-cable-umbilical-cross-sections.md`\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/comparisons/offshore-wind-oil-gas-cross-section-assessment.md`\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2516","display_title":"feat(digitalmodel): flexible pipe and dynamic riser cross-section mechanics follow-up"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2513","title":"feat(knowledge): catalogue offshore wind and oil gas subsea cross-section sources","issue_number":2513,"body":"## Summary\nBuild a source-backed catalogue for offshore wind and oil and gas subsea cross-section families: inter-array/export cables, electro-hydraulic umbilicals, power/optical umbilicals, rigid pipelines/flowlines, and flexible pipe/riser follow-ups.\n\n## Why\nThe new marine-engineering wiki synthesis shows that cross-section data is scattered across offshore wind guides, vendor PDFs, umbilical training material, and pipeline/coating standards. We need a durable catalogue before implementing reusable digitalmodel geometry/section-property tools.\n\n## Seed knowledge already created\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/concepts/subsea-cable-umbilical-cross-sections.md`\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/sources/offshore-cable-umbilical-cross-section-recon-2026-04-26.md`\n- `knowledge/wikis/marine-engineering/wiki/comparisons/offshore-wind-oil-gas-cross-section-assessment.md`\n\n## Initial source anchors\n- Guide to a Floating Offshore Wind Farm: array/export cable core and outer pages\n- Prysmian 66 kV submarine cable systems PDF\n- SUT Subsea Umbilical Systems PDF\n- Prysmian Power / Optical Umbilicals PDF\n- DNV-ST-F101 submarine pipeline systems page\n- Vallourec line pipe coating solutions page\n- Octal concrete weight coating page\n\n## Proposed deliverables\n- YAML/Markdown catalogue of source exemplars with provenance, asset family, typical layers/components, and known numeric ranges.\n- Standards map for API 17E/17J/17B, DNV-ST-F101, DNV-RP-F106/F102, IEC/CIGRE submarine cable references, and offshore wind cable guidance.\n- Gap list separating open/public sources from paywalled/manual standards.\n- Update wiki pages with any higher-quality exemplar data found.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- At least 12 source/exemplar entries across offshore wind cables, oil and gas umbilicals, rigid pipelines, and flexible pipes/risers.\n- Each entry records URL/local path, source type, asset family, key layers/components, usable numeric fields, and licensing/access status.\n- Duplicate/overlapping source entries are deduplicated and linked to the wiki pages above.\n- No proprietary or paywalled standard text is copied into repo artifacts.\n\n## Planning note\nThis is an issue-intake artifact only. Per workspace policy, implementation requires a canonical plan, adversarial review, and explicit approval before code/data changes beyond planning.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2513","display_title":"feat(knowledge): catalogue offshore wind and oil gas subsea cross-section sources"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2507","title":"Feature: semiconductor chip-design CAD/FEM career lane","issue_number":2507,"body":"## Summary\nCreate a focused semiconductor / chip-design CAD + FEM career lane in workspace-hub, using ACE Engineer strengths in CAD automation, FEM, document intelligence, and job-market scanning.\n\n## Roadmap\n- `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\n\n## Why now\nLocal job-market scans already show semiconductor-adjacent roles in ASIC program management, advanced packaging, IC packaging simulation, and semiconductor mechanical engineering. This lane converts existing offshore/CAD/FEM strengths into a credible semiconductor portfolio.\n\n## Scope\n1. Build semiconductor CAD/FEM research and job taxonomy.\n2. Create an open-source EDA RTL-to-GDS demonstration using OpenLane/OpenROAD and open PDKs.\n3. Create a Python CAD/layout automation demonstration using KLayout and/or GDSFactory.\n4. Create a semiconductor packaging thermal/thermo-mechanical FEM benchmark.\n5. Build a job-application packet mapping artifacts to target roles.\n\n## Policy\nFollow repository gates: issue -> plan -> adversarial review -> user approval -> TDD implementation. This umbrella tracks the lane; child issues should carry implementation details.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2507","display_title":"Feature: semiconductor chip-design CAD/FEM career lane"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2509","title":"feat(eda): create reproducible OpenLane/OpenROAD RTL-to-GDS demo report","issue_number":2509,"body":"## Context\nParent: #2507\nRoadmap: `docs/roadmaps/chip-design-cad-fem-career-roadmap.md`\n\nCreate a small open-source EDA demonstration that proves RTL-to-GDS flow literacy and produces a hiring/client-facing report.\n\n## Proposed scope\n- Select a tiny reference RTL design: counter, FIFO, simple ALU, or equivalent.\n- Run or document a reproducible OpenLane/OpenROAD flow against an open PDK such as Sky130 or GF180MCU.\n- Capture and explain timing, area, power, DRC/LVS, DEF/GDS, and log artifacts.\n- Generate an HTML or markdown report that explains what the artifacts mean.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Container/environment instructions.\n2. Source design and flow config.\n3. Generated report artifact and artifact inventory.\n4. Tests/smoke checks where practical, especially around scripts and report generation.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- A fresh checkout can reproduce the demo or clearly replay committed sample artifacts.\n- The report maps EDA artifacts to semiconductor job keywords.\n- No proprietary PDK or licensed-tool dependency is required.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2509","display_title":"feat(eda): create reproducible OpenLane/OpenROAD RTL-to-GDS demo report"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1572","title":"Domain-specific capability roadmaps — OrcaWave/OrcaFlex, structural, hydrodynamics, pipeline","issue_number":1572,"body":"Child of #1567. Create 4 domain roadmap docs mapping: implemented modules → test status → open GH issues → gaps. (1) docs/roadmaps/orcawave-orcaflex-capability-roadmap.md, (2) structural-capability-roadmap.md, (3) hydrodynamics-capability-roadmap.md, (4) pipeline-capability-roadmap.md. Cross-ref with GH issues. Related: docs/plans/2026-04-01-orcawave-orcaflex-intensive-plan.md","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1572","display_title":"Domain-specific capability roadmaps — OrcaWave/OrcaFlex, structural, hydrodynamics, pipeline"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2473","title":"feat(canonical-spec): prove OrcaWave-to-OrcaFlex hydrodynamic handoff semantics","issue_number":2473,"body":"## Summary\nAdd an end-to-end semantic proof for OrcaWave hydrodynamic outputs flowing into OrcaFlex vessel/hydrodynamic native inputs without losing units, headings, frequencies, phase/sign, body axes, damping, or added-mass provenance.\n\n## Why now\nThe first wave proved selected canonical specs become semantically correct native YAML. The handoff gap is now the critical workflow proof: `OrcaWave output -> RAO/added-mass/damping data -> RAODatabase/bridge -> OrcaFlex vessel input`.\n\n## Scope\n- Use existing RAO extractor / database / `rao_data_to_diffraction_results()` / OrcaFlex export surfaces where possible.\n- Define fixture(s) and provenance fields to assert.\n- Include checks for frequency/heading preservation, rotational-unit convention, phase/sign convention, damping/added-mass matrix provenance, body-frame metadata, and source-file traceability.\n\n## Out of scope\n- New licensed solver execution unless separately approved under the licensed proof protocol.\n- Rebuilding the already closed #1592/#1605 pipeline unless resource intelligence shows a true gap.\n\n## Related\n- Closed first wave: #2455, #2456, #2457\n- Prior closed handoff work: #1592, #1597, #1605, #1765, #1766, #1768\n- Handoff: `docs/handoffs/2026-04-23-orcawave-orcaflex-semantic-proof-exit-handoff.md`\n- digitalmodel PR: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/528\n\n## Acceptance target\nA plan should identify exact existing code/test surfaces, avoid duplicating closed work, and specify deterministic TDD tests proving hydrodynamic semantic preservation.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2473","display_title":"feat(canonical-spec): prove OrcaWave-to-OrcaFlex hydrodynamic handoff semantics"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2472","title":"feat(canonical-spec): validate CALM/SPM buoy OrcaFlex semantic proof","issue_number":2472,"body":"## Summary\nAdd a canonical `spec.yml -> ProjectInputSpec -> ModularModelGenerator -> native OrcaFlex YAML` semantic proof for CALM/SPM buoy systems.\n\n## Why now\nPR #528 closed the first semantic-proof wave for PLET-to-PLEM jumpers, lazy/steep-wave risers, and the L03 OrcaWave ship benchmark. The exit handoff identified CALM/SPM buoy systems as the next high-value offshore structure family not yet proof-covered.\n\n## Scope\n- Identify or add a minimal CALM/SPM buoy canonical fixture.\n- Prove object identity and generated native OrcaFlex structure for buoy, mooring/anchor/fairlead intent, line types, environmental/load-case intent, and vessel/tanker interaction boundary where applicable.\n- Keep this deterministic and license-free unless a separate licensed-machine issue is approved.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Full licensed OrcaFlex execution/load-run proof.\n- AQWA vs OrcaFlex benchmark comparison from legacy #21 unless explicitly pulled into this issue by plan approval.\n\n## Related\n- Closed first wave: #2455, #2456, #2457\n- Existing related SPM benchmark issue: #21\n- Handoff: `docs/handoffs/2026-04-23-orcawave-orcaflex-semantic-proof-exit-handoff.md`\n- digitalmodel PR: https://github.com/vamseeachanta/digitalmodel/pull/528\n\n## Acceptance target\nA plan should define the fixture path, generator assertions, TDD commands, semantic-equivalence criteria, and follow-up licensed-proof boundary before implementation.\n","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2472","display_title":"feat(canonical-spec): validate CALM/SPM buoy OrcaFlex semantic proof"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2454","title":"feat(canonical-spec): validate flagship generic-track OrcaFlex mooring case via turret-moored FPSO semantic proof","issue_number":2454,"body":"## Summary\nValidate a flagship generic-track OrcaFlex mooring case — preferably turret-moored FPSO — for semantic equivalence from canonical `spec.yml` to generated native OrcaFlex inputs.\n\n## Why\nCurrent review shows the largest remaining OrcaFlex readiness gap is not generation capability but family-level proof for generic-track structures. A turret-moored FPSO is the highest-leverage case because it exercises vessel, mooring, environment, references, and generic pass-through behavior in one structure family.\n\n## Scope\n- Select a representative generic-track mooring model, with preference for `c03_turret_moored_fpso`\n- Establish canonical fixture path from `spec.yml` to generated native OrcaFlex YAML\n- Run semantic comparison using the OrcaFlex semantic taxonomy\n- Identify allowed vs blocking differences explicitly\n- Document claim boundary for this family\n\n## Deliverables\n- Fixture-backed validation for one flagship FPSO/mooring case\n- Semantic diff artifact mapped to `digitalmodel/docs/domains/orcaflex/SEMANTIC_DIFF_TAXONOMY.md`\n- Regression test or validation harness committed in digitalmodel\n- Short readiness note added to the roadmap/operator docs if the family is promoted\n\n## Success criteria\n- Generated native inputs preserve analysis-significant fields for the selected FPSO case\n- Remaining differences are classified and justified, not hand-waved\n- The family can be upgraded from partial to ready in the readiness matrix\n\n## Parent roadmap\n- Reopened roadmap anchor: #1572\n\n## Related active issues\n- Proof/hardening: #1652, #1788, #1586\n- Structure readiness source: `docs/roadmaps/orcawave-orcaflex-canonical-spec-contract-roadmap.md`","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/2454","display_title":"feat(canonical-spec): validate flagship generic-track OrcaFlex mooring case via turret-moored FPSO semantic proof"},{"url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1788","title":"OrcaFlex .sim snapshot testing: HTML report from minimal_test.sim fixture","issue_number":1788,"body":"## Context\n#1652 requested integration tests with real .sim fixtures and HTML snapshot testing. The minimal_test.sim fixture is now committed. The next step is building the snapshot test.\n\n## Deliverables\n1. Test that loads minimal_test.sim metadata (without OrcFxAPI — use the .dat text companion)\n2. Generate an HTML report from the model metadata\n3. Snapshot test: compare generated HTML against committed baseline\n4. Extend to mooring_with_raos.sim if feasible without OrcFxAPI\n\n## Fixtures available\n- digitalmodel/tests/fixtures/minimal_test.sim (318 KB)\n- digitalmodel/tests/fixtures/minimal_test.dat (140 KB, text-readable)\n- digitalmodel/tests/fixtures/solver/mooring_with_raos.sim (13.8 MB)\n\n## Related\n- #1652 (parent)","state":null,"user":null,"assignees":null,"labels":null,"milestone":null,"state_reason":null,"comments":null,"created_at":null,"updated_at":null,"closed_at":null,"display_url":"https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/issues/1788","display_title":"OrcaFlex .sim snapshot testing: HTML report from minimal_test.sim fixture"}]}},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
